      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>2 mcDOUGALL's paterit SELF-RAISING FLOUR d'AEGIUMN.</head>
        <p>2 mcDOUGALL's paterit SELF-RAISING FLOUR d'AEGIUMN.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>THIRD ROUND, Warrington Win at Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men added to their cup-tie laurels by winning the third round engagement at Huddersfield on Saturday. Beautifully bright summer-like weather pre- vailed, and the Fartown ground presented a fine spectacle when packed with 13,000 people. Warrington were well-supported, about 1,000 passengers making the journey to Huddersfield per L. &amp;N.-W. Railway. There was plenty of confidence in the wirepullers," though it it was recognised that Huddersfield would make a great effort, and this proved to be the case. The home team le&amp;at half-time, but were vanquished in the second half by a more powerful team. Teams and officials : Huddersfield, Bartholemew, back; Kitchen, Davies, Wagstafle and Wade, three-quarter backs; Hopkins and Muxworthy, half-backs; Ainley, Swinden, Cole, Hurst, Longthorne, and Wilson, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, back; Fish, Isherwood, Taylor ' and Brooks, three-quarter backs; Lees and Hockenhall, half-backs; Boardman, Heath, Shugars, Naylor, Thomas, and Belton, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. McCutcheon, Oldham. Warrington who lost th'e toss, had to face a blazing sun and a slight breeze in the opening half. The first ten minutes' play was marred by a succession of penal- ties against each side, these doubtless being the result of over-eageruess. The home pack, with the assistance of Kitchen, carried play into the Warrington quarters, where some loose rushing by both setspf forwards was seen. Here Kitchen got the ball and darted down the left touch line. He was tackled', but was allowed to get up and wriggle past Brooks. The ball then went to Dlaxworthy, who opened the scoring amid great cheer- ing. Wagstaffe failed at goal. This success afforded immense encouragement to the Huddersfield men, and some capital passing bouts were indulged in by thp halves and three-quarters, these divisions also obtaining valuable assistance from Bartholomew. Warring- ton were very hard pressed by the 'fierce rushes of the home forwards. Play settled on the line and another score looked probable when Brooks intercepted the pass sent for Kitchen and raced to half-way. Warrington had a short spell on the attack, Taylor nearly bringing the home defence down by a clever cross kick which beat the full back and was only missed by inches by Fish. The latter had a shot at goal but failed, and then the homesters raced away to the Warrington end. Tilley was captured, and penalised for some reason when he passed back, and Bartholomew placed a goal. Warrington could make little headway and devoted themselves to (Menai). Near the interval the referee again penalised Warrington and the home full-back obliged with a smart goal. Thus at the interval Huddersfield held an advantage on the score sheet of seven points to nothing. Warrington now had the sun and the breeze at their backs, and Huddersfield found the elements a decided handicap. Within a quarter of an hour the "Wire- pullers" drew level. Brooks had exchanged places with Hockeahall before the interval and resumed operations at the base of the scrummage. In the first minute he dashed clean away and presented Isherwood with the easiest of openings, but the centre dropped the ball. Warrington were now playing with great dash. In five minutes some clever passing was seen among the Warrington backs, and after Lees, Taylor, and Isherwood had handled the ball, Fish galloped over, also adding goal points. The next few minutes' play raged in the Huddersfield quarters, aud the culminating point was a cleverly dropped goal by Taylor, which equalised the scores, and gave the Warrington supporters the first comfortable sensation since the beginning of the game. It was all going Warrington way now, Brooks being generally the mainspring. He again got away at centre, jumped over a would-be tackler, and tossed the ball across to Hockenhall, who scored near the posts after a fine 40 yards sprint. Fish added the goal easily. Five points behind Huddersfield worked hard, and they were rewarded by seeing Bartholomew land a fine penalty goal from long range, the ball striking the upright and rebounding on the right aide. Heath next charged a kick by Davies, and following up made straight for the line, forcing his way over. Fish failed at goal. The home forwards were showing signs of fatigue, and Warrington should have added to themscore when Thomas dashed away and threw to Heath dear the line but the ball went in touch. Near the close, Bartho- lomew made a brilliant run from his own quarter to the Warrington line and passed to Wade. The latter was knocked into touch by Fish. Final: Warrington 3 goals, 3 tries, 15 points; Huddersfield 3 goals 1 try. 9 points.</p>
