      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EASTER MONDAY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>EASTER MONDAY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Barrow 2-5 19 Hunslet 2-3 13 Halifax -3 13 York O-0 0 Bradford -2 10 Batley -1 5 Huddersfield ......... 8-5 31 Swinton -0 2 • Wakefield ............ 1-3 11 Hull -2 8 WARRINGTON v. LEEDS. Warrington were in splendid form for their match with Leeds on Monday and easily captured a couple of points. There was a fine holiday crowd of about 6,000 when Leeds started and opened the attack. The first few minutes did not produce anything very special and it lay to Warrington to open the scoring,Taylor capping a brilliant passing movement. Fish failed at goal. The restart saw the Leeds forwards dangerous, Tilley clearing well. Stead broke away, but his pass to a colleague was forward, and a good opening was lost. Ike Taylor changed the venue by a smart run to mid- field. He kicked towards the Leeds line, and Jordan following up smartly, raced Young,and grounded at the corner. The referee negatived the point and awarded a free kick to Leeds for obstruction. Smart footwork by the Leeds forwards transferred play to the Warrington end, but Tilley cleared. Loose play in the Warrington •half resulted in Young getting possession near midfield and dropping a splendid goal. After this Warrington got to the other end, and Jordan breaking through ran over underneath the post. Fish landed a goal. A pass- ing movement by the Leeds forwards was intercepted by Jordan, who ran to Young and then passed to Lees, the latter player scoring near the post. Fish added the extra point. Thomas, of Leeds, securing on his own quarter line, ran strongly and passed to W. Llewellyn, who scored near the post. Ward converted. Half-time: Warrington 16 points, Leeds T points. The second half was for a large part unexciting. Near the close how- ever Warrington attacked strongly, and Jordan -.scored by a brilliant individual effort. When near his own quarter line he snapped the ball from in front of a Leeds forward and beating all opposition raced over under the posts, registering a try worthy of Fish at his best. Fish goaled. Isherwood and Hockenhall subse- quently got over, Fish again adding the extra points and time was called with the score :—Warrington, 31 points; Leeds, 7 points. RUNCORN v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. At Runcorn, before 2,000 people. Butterworth Opened the scoring for Runcorn, but Jolley failed with the kick. Walton left the ground, and Walker went half-back. At the interval the home side had added a try and goal to a goal and a try, both the product of Harris's efforts. Then Walker scored a try, and Jolley kicked a goal, and another try came ere the close. Result :—Runcorn, 2 goals, 4 tries, 16 points; Broughton Rangers, 1 goal, 1 try, 5 points. WIGAN v. OLDHAM. At Wigan before 14,000 spectators. The match began II exciting fashion, for Ferguson kicked a goal three minutes from the start. Then Wigan got a try, so that at half time they led by 3 points to 2. The game was most exciting, and had a rare finish. Leytham added to Wigan's lead by kicking a goal ; but then white dashed over for Oldham, and Ferguson landing a 'oeautiful goal won the match. Result :—Oldham 2 goals 1 try 7 points; Wigan, 1 goal, 1 try, 5 points.</p>
        <p>Sr. HELENS v. SALFORD. At St. Helens, before 3,000 spectators. St. Helens made a good start, as they at once made their way to the line, and, taking advantage of a mull by Brady, J. Creevey scored, Toole failing at goal. Lomas kicked a goal from a mark, and Hillen added another try, and C. Creevey kicked a couple of goals from marks. J. Creevey kicked a goal from a mark and Briers scored, .J. Creevey kicking a goal. Interval :—St. Helens, 17 points; Salford, 2 points. On resuming, Briers 6roke away from the pack, and made a fine run to the Salford line, where he passed to Pope, who was well tackled. Bate kicked across to Pope, who passed for Mavity to score under the posts, Toole kicking the goal. Salford pressed, without result. Briers ran in for another try. Toole kicking the goal. while Creevey added another try. Lomas scored for Salford. Result :—St. Helens, 20 points ; Salford, 5 points. - WIDNES V. HULL. -</p>
        <p>At Widnes. Wallace scored for the visitors, and Carvell succeeded with the kick at goal. The latter next landed a penalty goal. Then at the other end Hull were penalised, and Aspey kicked a goal. Aspey, too, got over near the posts, and Davies landed a goal. Half-time: Widnes, 7 points ; Hull, 7 points. Lalley scored a try, and Davies kicked two goals, which practi- cally settled the visitors, whose defence was thrice more beaten. Result :—Widnes, 23 points ; Hull, 7 points.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY'S MATCHES. Oldham ............... 1 0 2 Hull Leeds 3 21 Hunslet 5 3 19 Huddersfield ...... 14 14 Warrington Wakefield HUDDERSFIELD'S REVENGE. Warrington paid another visit to Huddersfield on Tuesday with several changes from the cup team, and were beaten by 14 points to 3 points. The fine weather continued, and there was a crowd of about 3,000. Teams Huddersfield—Baker, back ; Ainley. Wagstaffe, Bartholomew, and Davies, three-quarter backs. Hop- kins and Mnxworthy, half backs; Binns, Cole, Charles - worth, Swinden, Hirst, and Longthorne, forwards. Warrington—Tilley. bask; Dickinson, Isherwood,</p>
