      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE BAND FOR FRANCE.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE BAND FOR FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Great Reception Prepared.</p>
        <p>Warrington people, who look back with great pleasure to the recent visit to this town of the band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, will be interested in the approaching departure to Lille of the band which is now stationed at Blackdown, Aldershot. The invitation, says "Military Mail," can be traced to an occasion when the band was playing on the Lees at Folkestone, during the time the battalion was quartered at Shorncliffe. There had been a special excursion from the North of France to England, and a very large number of the visitors landing at ,Folkestone and stay- ing in the town listened with marked pleasure to the playing of the band. From that the acquaintance began, and when steps were taken by the Municipality of Lille to organise a concert in aid of a well-known French charity, the suggestion was made that it would be a great attraction to get the South Lancashire's band to fill a plsoe inttohe programme. The suggestion was received with enthusiasm, and through the proper channel, a most cordial invitation was sent to the com- manding officer. Of course Colonel F. A. Adam could not accept such an invitation, pleasant as it was, with- out the sanction of the War Office, and it was immedi- ately forwarded to London. In due time, to the delight of every officer and man in the battalion, formal per- mission to accept the invitation was granted. The arrangements for the visit are now complete. The band will travel so as to reach Lille, which is in the Department du Nord, early in the afternoon of the 13th inst. The grand concert which has been arranged, and of which the performance by the band tops the bill, will be held in the Hippodrome, the largest building in Lille, on the following day (Sunday), and will be honoured by the presence of all the chief dignitaries of the town. The return to England, it is expected, will he made on the Monday. It may be added that the French Government and the War Department have intimated their intention to give the band an official reception. A representative of the War Department will meet the Lancashire lads at Calais, and conduct them to Lille, where they will be received by the Minister of the Interior, the Poinister of Foreign Affairs, the Pre- fect of the Department du Nord, General Lebon (com- manding the Ist Army Corps of the French Army), and the Mayor and Corporation. The French officials are determined to rank * the visit a memorable one, in view of the fact that it is the first time a band of the British Infantry of the Line has been so invited. Mr. A. Harris, L.R.A.M., the bandmaster, will take a band of forty-five musicians with him, and a full reper- toire of music. The party will be accompanied by Colonel F. A. Adam and Captain and Adjutant B. R. aoren, who will be officially on duty during the period the band is in a foreign country. Renter's Correspondent telegraphs :—The permission of the British Government allowing the band of the 2nd Battalion South 'Lancashire Regiment to go to Lille has produced a very good effect among the com- mercial and industrial population of the North of France. It is regarded as marking a desire for a con- tinuation of the entente cordiale between two nations, and the strengthening of commercial ties between them. Lille is preparing a great welcome for the band. The military bands of the garrison, two troops of the 6th Clia.sseurs tc, Cbeval, and several brigades of mounted police will meet the band at Lille Station, and will escort it to the Town Hall, the Prefecture, and the military headquarters, where the Mayor, Prefect, and the general commanding the garrison respectively will await them. On Sunday evening a banquet will take place, at which the English officers and civil and military officials will be present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SPRING-CLEANING DANGERS.</head>
        <p>SPRING-CLEANING DANGERS.</p>
        <p>When a woman begins to clean down she appreciates to the full What a saving in suffering, time and money follows the custom of keeping a box of Zam-Buk handy.</p>
        <p>* * * Her hands and arms are almost sure to be sore with the rough work, with constant wetting and half-dryings and with the use of irritating polishes and powder?. Both for the sake of comfort and appearance she is grateful for the soothing and smoothing influence of Zam-Buk, and the well-known healing and antiseptic action of this household balm are rendered absolutely necessary by the extra risks of blood-poisoning and festering she runs.</p>
        <p>* * * * Think of the dark, dusty ledges and , corners, of the musty nooks and crannies to be scoured out. Those cold-cracked, work-worn hands will be disturbing millions of microbes and disease germs, and no house- wife can afford to neglect her skin. Correction and protection with Zam-Buk is necetsary as well as nice. * * * *</p>
        <p>To avoid the same dangers the slightest bruise or scratch must be promptly dressed with Zam-Buk. For more painful mishaps—the sprained ankle or wrist, the crushed finger or foot, the cut, bruise.burn or scald that may happen at any moment—Zam-Buk's soothing and healing aid more than repays its shelf-room.</p>
        <p>You wouldn't countenance the leaving of a cobweb I in a corner, or a grease spot in the carpet. Then don't neglect even tiny blotches and pimples in your skin ' They are the result of the body's Spring-cleaning, and show that the skin is failing to take its part in the work. Zam-Buk acts as a skin-food and tonic cleanser in such troubles, and ensures a soft, clear, and healthy, skin despite the season's indoor and outdoor trials. Keep a box handy!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHALEUR BAY'S "FIRE-SHIP."</head>
        <p>CHALEUR BAY'S "FIRE-SHIP."</p>
        <p>According to Professor Ganong, an American scientist, the traditional " fire-ship " of Chaleur Bay, New Brunswick, appearing usually be- fore a storm, has a basis of fact. It is a hemispherical light, with the flat side towards the water, glowing sometimes without much change of form, but at other times rising into slender, moving columns, in which an excited imagination might recognise the flaming rigging of a ship. The general explanation offered is that this object is a manifestation of St. Elmo's fire, an electtical phenomenon; but the reason for its appearing only on or near the Chaleur Bay is not known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WONDERFUL CATARACT.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL CATARACT.</p>
        <p>The Falls of Iguazu, near the meeting-place of Brazil, Argentina., and Paraguay, seldom I visited by foreigners, are among the most wonderful cataracts in the world. They are particularly interesting on account of their I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>HELIUM IN BERYL.</head>
        <p>HELIUM IN BERYL.</p>
        <p>The Hon. R. J. Strutt contributed an in- teresting note to Nature on the occur- rence of helium in the absence of radio- activity. First discovered in the sun, and only some twenty-five years later found to be a constituent of the earth, helium is now considered to be one of the products of the breaking-up of the radium atoms which manifests itself in radio-activity. Mr. Strutt has been engaged in studying the inactive gases contained' in minerals which are not themselves radio-active. Among those ex- amined was beryl from New Hampshire, and this was found to contain helium in much larger quantities than occurs in ordinary in- active minerals. Thus, 250 grams of beryl gave 4.2 cubic centimetres of helium. And Mr. Strutt seems to think there is here a change of the element beryllium into helium without the phenomena of radiation which usually accompany the formation of the latter gas. "It seems likely," he writes, "that we have here a case of raylews change." But if it is the breaking up of the radium atoms which is the cause of the various emanations which occur in the case of radio-active minerals, it is not easy, re- marks the Globe, to understand how the atoms of beryllium can break up without similar outward manifestations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HOW SUNSHINE IS MEASURED.</head>
        <p>HOW SUNSHINE IS MEASURED.</p>
        <p>A scientific record of sunshine must com- prise an estimate of (1) its duration, and (2) its intensity.' The first is usually determined by means of Jerdan'g photographic recorder. This, says the Reader, consists of a brass cylinder, adjusted at such an angle that one side is..illuminated &amp;Irina the forenoon. the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WARRINGTON SAILING CLUB RACE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SAILING CLUB RACE.</p>
        <p>Sad Accident.</p>
        <p>The annual race in connection with the Warrington Sailing Club took place on the river on Saturday, when , twelve boats competed. The course was from Widnes, ' round a buoy moored near Walton Arches, and back again to the west end of Thewlis's wall. A close; and exciting race was witnessed by a large number of people. Pride, captained by Mr. William Warburton, and receiving 4,1, minutes' start, was first home, followed by Star of Hope, (three minutes), captained by Mr. J. Hatton, Rosie Mary from scratch (captain Mr. J. Leigh), obtained third prize.• I As the boats were passing under Messrs. Crostield's new bridge Mr. William Unsworth, a blacksmith, who resided in Lovely-lane, was standing on the deck of The Gipsy, when the boom swung round and knocked him into the water. He was pulled out of the water by Isaac Sankey of Howley-lane, Superintendent Bell and P.C. Woodladd rendered first aid. Mr. Unsworth, ever, was injured internally, and he died at his home on Monday afternoon. The deceased was 54 years of age, and leaves a family. Mr. S. Brighouse held the inquest on Wednesday evening. James Danesen, 25. Cartwright-street, captain of the Gipsy said that during the race he was at the tiller, and deceased was on deck though he had been repeatedly warned to go below. The sail was being palled over from starboard when the main sheet broke and catching theeact hwiantger.dea deceased between the legs knocked him in</p>
        <p>I A verdict of Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>FATAL FALL.-Mr. Thomas Ridgway, deputy coroner conducted an inquest at the Hall Cafe Stockton Heath, on Tuesday, on the body of Hannah Fairhurst, aged 66 years, of 44, Warburton-street, Stockton Heath,who died from injuries received from falling downstairs on Sun- • day night. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>STEAMER BURNED AT SEA.</head>
        <p>STEAMER BURNED AT SEA.</p>
        <p>Men of the pteamer Riftswood, which was burnt at sea, arrived at Southampton on Tuesday in the Royal Mail steamer La Plata. The Riftswood was chartered by the Admiralty to carry coal, and whilstoff St. Lucien a lamp was accidentally upset in the engine room and a quantity of paraffin caught alight. The flames spread rapidly, and the crew, who were compelled to leave the vessel, were rescued by a passing sailing ship,and landed at St. Lucien.</p>
      </div>
