        <p>vinur twang _ on eacn sine or the cylinder, at points corresponding with each other, is a tiny aperture, through which the light enters, and falls on a drum of sensi- tised paper, on which it describes a path from sunrise to sunset. Any interruption in that path is, of course, indicated when the chart has been washed in fixinF solution, and the relative duration of sunshine and ploud is then exhibited. To ascertain the intensity of sunshine, the Stokes-Campbell recorder is the instrument most commonly used, the , principal points of which are a glass ball, a bowl, and an adj4stable stand. The glass ball acts as a lens, and is mounted so that it focusses the light and heat from the sun on to a strip of sensitised paper that is placed' on the inner side of the bowl, and as the sun passes from east to west over the ball, the ,pentrated rays char the paper from west to east. The paper may eitner ne cuvicten so as to read the time direct, or in degrees of arc, from which the time can be calculated.</p>
