      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PEAT AND COAL.</head>
        <p>PEAT AND COAL.</p>
        <p>Coal is fossil peat. The peat-bogs of to- day might become coal-beds in some future geological age. Professor Potonie of Berlin says there is strict parallelism between the different kinds of peat and of coal. When the remains of plants collect under terrestrial conditions, an ordinary peat-bog is formed. This corresponds with "bright" coal. If the remains collect under water, an organic slime is formed, and this corresponds with "dull." or cannel, coal. When terrestrial and aquatic conditions have alternated during the collec- tion of the remains, " strata-peat " results, and this corresponds with coal deposits cop- slating of alternate layers of bright and dull coal. The chemical and physical properties of the various kinds of peat and coal show a similar correspondence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>AMBIDEXTERITY.</head>
        <p>AMBIDEXTERITY.</p>
        <p>A standing puzzle is the almost universal tendency of men and women of all races to use the right hand in preference to the left. Examination of skeletons has shewn, by the differences of bone development, that this ten- dency is of very ancient origin. It is often ascribed to the fact that the left hemisphere of the brain—which controls the right side of the body—possesses,. in normal persons, a superior development. But those who think that the preference for the right hand is an acquired habit, although one of immensely long standing, suggest that perhaps the left cerebral hemisphere has become better de- veloped as the result of the overuse of the right limbs. At any rate, • a society has been founded in London for the cultivation of am- bidexterity, and it will be for the physiolo- gists of the future to determine whether edu- cation in the use of the left hand can affect the development of the right side of the brain. AXIAL ROTATION OF VENUS.</p>
        <p>For over two centuries it was generally accepted that, like the earth, the planet Venus rotated on its axis in a little less than twi,nty-four. llours. • but since Schiauarelii_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL LEADERSHIP.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL LEADERSHIP.</p>
        <p>Ehe following nominations have been officially received for.the chairmanship of the Congregational Union of England and Wales for 1908 : Rev. W. F. Adeney. M.A., D.D., Manchester. Rev. J. Morgan Gibbon, London. Rev. J. D. Jones. M.A., 8.D.. Bournemouth. Mr. John Massie, M.A., D.D., M.P., Oxford. Rev. R. Wardlaw Thompson, D.D., London. The last duy of receiving nominations was March 1, and the election takes place at the annual business meeting of the Union on May 6 next.</p>
        <p>THE POLICE COURT AND PRISON GATE MISSION.</p>
        <p>The missionaries of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission of the Diocesan Church of England Temperance Society have for several years past amidst their multi- farious duties visited first offenders, who had been discharged into their care by the magistrates. Liverpool and a few other places, the justices have asked the missionaries to report to them at stated intervals as to the conduct of the accused. This pro- cedure has been so successful that the Home Secretary has introduced a Bill into Parliament entitled "The Probation of Offenders (No. 2) "Bill, for making law what has hitherto been custom. Under the Probation of First Offenders' Act of 1887, no fewer than 50,289 persons were saved from prison, from January Ist 1888 to December 31st 1905, and of these only about ten per cent were Called upon to receive judgment, or known to have been subsequently convicted qf a fresh offence At every police and petty sessional court in the Liverpool, Bootle, Southport, Wigan, Warrington, St* I Helens, Widnes, etc. dirtricts,there is a larger scope for I the work of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission. ' During the month of March, the missionaries made 253 visits to the courts. and conversed with 1,225 persons 1 on th 3 premises, and subsequently visited no fewer than I 620 of these in their abodes. 57 women, girls, and boys were taken to homes, restored to friends, or provided with employment. Eighteen men were offered work at the Society's Firewood and Mat Factory, Bootle. The Blood Is Life, let it be Pure. 1 magistrates had the co-operation of the missionaries on • Whalf of 110 persons, and the court officials and police Blotches, pimples on the face and body,scrofula,sores itching and redness of the skin, blackhead and eczema, in 74 instances. 293 persons from the courts and prisons Indicate impure blood and unhealthy constitution. were interviewed by the missionaries, and in their If efforts at prevention and reclamation no fewer than 233 your blood is