        <p>In his monthly circular to the Northumberland miners Mr. Burt, M.P., referring to the Chinese labour in the Transvaal, says: "Everything shows that shile there is naturally and properly every desire to recognise the supreme importance of the gold mining industry to the transvaal, and there is every readiness to give not only fair play, but every assistance, to that industry,yet Chinese labour as a permanent institution is doomed.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MURDERER HANGED.</head>
        <p>MURDERER HANGED.</p>
        <p>Edwin Moore, an ex-soldier, was executed at Warwick on Tuesday morning for the murder of his mother at Leamington. Ellis, of Rochdale, was the hangman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BLOOD AND SKIN DISEASES.</head>
        <p>BLOOD AND SKIN DISEASES.</p>
        <p>i; • fresso;:mit. • 1, C 1-7 , 4t.</p>
        <p>IT ACTS</p>
        <p>SCARLET FEVER.</p>
        <p>had been prevalent during the whole year (the' only months free were June, October and November) in a sporadic form and occurred in nearly every part of the district, one or two cases being reported monthly. Fourteen cases were notified, six in Sankey, one in Winwick and seven in Rixton Registration Districts. The disease was of a very mild character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SPOTTED FEVER.</head>
        <p>SPOTTED FEVER.</p>
        <p>The previons week a case of spotted fever was reported to him from Padgate, the patient dying the same day. Two cases had occurred in Padgate the previous year, but he did not think it necessary that the disease should be made a notifiable one. SPITTLE BRIDGE IMPROVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. MA.THER mentioned the question of the improve- ment of Spittle Bridge. At the last meeting of the Padgate Parish Council a resolution was unanimously passed in favour of the larger scheme for re-building the bridge and straightening the road. In the absence of IMr. Houghton, who had given notice of motion, he moved that the County Council be recommended to pro- ceed with the larger scheme. For the smaller scheme —the re-building of the bridge and the improvement of the approaches-2200 would be required for the pur- chase of land and £1,200 for the carrying out of the work. The other scheme would involve an expenditure of £7OO or £BOO on land and £5,000 for the work. many case there would have to be a new bridge and either scheme would involve expenditure to that extent. As the cost of the improvement, except for the purchase of the land, would come out of the County Council,they favoured the larger scheme. He was not at all satisfied with regard to the price asked for the land and he did not think they should pay that price if they could possibly help it. The opening of the new road would improve the surrounding land and that ought to be taken into consideration before a price was agreed upon. • Mr. Newcombe had seen the solicitors to the owners and understood that it would be scarcely possible to have the price reduced. The County Council could go to arbitration although he thought it would be better if they could agree with the solicitors. The CHAIRMAN: I don't know what the County will think, but we did not get any encouragement from the county surveyor when he was on the spot. Mr. MATHER : There is no doubt that the people of the parish favour the larger scheme because the money comes out of the county. The CHAIRMAN: The larger scheme would give motorists a chance to scorch whereas if we keep them going round corners they cannot do so. The old road will be quite good enough for the usual slow traffic and will act as a brake on the rapid traffic. Mr. BACKHOUSE urged that the price asked for the land was monstrous.</p>
        <p>Mr. SUTTON pointed out that the Goverm ent was to introduce a bill on the question of land purchase for publics improvements. Mr. MATHER : But we don't know when the Bill will pass. Mr. SUTTON: I understand that it is regarded as one of the important measure before the Government. Mr. MATHER : There is one thing we know and that is that Spittle bridge may drop in any moment. Mr. Mather's motion was approved. THE DUST NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROOKS on behalf of the Rixton Parish Council raised the question of the dust nuisance and asked that thf. roads at Rixton should be more thoroughly cleaned or that some dust laying preparation should be used on the main roads.</p>
        <p>Mr. NIWCOMBE said that about three weeks ago he spent the whole morning at the County offices trying to get included in the estimates the cost of a preparation for laying dust. Mr.Schofleld, county surveyor,said that the different patents as compared with the expense, were failures and that at the first sign of frost horses could hardly stand on the treated portions of the road. They were, unfortunately, not able to clean macadam thoroughly as if they did so the road in summer would become raw. In Cheshire it had been found that patent dust layers ruined the roads, and at Knutsford 2600 had to be included in the estimates fur the re-making of a length of road which had been treated. The reason why the layers were failures was that they kept the dust in the road in the summer, and in the winter the dust came out and completely ruined the roads. Mr. BACKHOUSE : I think we are at the wrong end. It should be compulsory for motorists to lay their own dust.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>combining the long and the short skirt, which, by he way, hails from Paris, will be warmly welcomed. The actual skirt is out walking length and trimmed with strappings—or any other fotin of trimming beneath which fastenings can beattaohed. Then a shaped flounce is provided cut in such a manner as to form a short train, and in a few moments it can be secured to the skirt, beneath the strappings, and the walking costume is turned into a more dressy toilette. For travelling, or short visits, when we would have as little luggage with us as possible, these combined skirts will prove delight- ful acquisitions, and to the home dressmaker they should present no overwhelming difficulties. The Royal Academy.—Whatever views the community of women Grtists may take of the question, they have certainly a grievance against the Royal Academy which withholds from them the privilege of being elected Acadamecians. Until recently they were not allowed to compete on level terms with the men at the Royal Academy schools, but since they obtained that right in 1903, they have succeeded in carrying off several of the principal prizes' awarded to students. But although they have not the privileges of academicians and associates, women artists have been very successful in recent years in getting their pictures into Academy, and the forthcoming Exhibition will probably see them in greater numbers than ever. in at least one picture in this year's Academy, the Suffragists will find a place. This is Mr. Sigismund Goetze's allegorical subject picture the "Greatest of These." The central figure is a young mother with her baby, and around her are grouped various types of women,mcluding the one with the banner. The picture is of course intended to emphasise the glory of motherhood. The Care of Veils.—A veil should never be left tied round a hat, if it is it will quickly become limp and unmanageable. It should be shaken, wiped over with an old silk handkerchief, then taken at the two ends,and rolled round and round—width ways—To mend a veil the thread of the mesh should be unravelled, and used after the same manner as the pattern employed. MADGE.</p>
        <p>On Ghosts.—The ghost that was recently seen in one 1 of the,b‘nsiest quarters in the heart of London, bad ! Ka"Y lost itself. It must belong to Hampton Court Conferring greater distinction than titles. It is uamZ▪ et or some ancient castle, where a ghost is looked n",, n— --artY always the ghost of a woman, and, downtrodden as she baa been in the past woman cannot complain that ' she has been unfairly treated by lordly man in the Matter of ghosts. She has almost a monopoly among them, and this, it has been said, accounts for the per- l'ersity that exists in the,minds of ghosts. They appear I t° People who do not want to see them, but very rarely indeed,to those who would welcome their coming. Thus we find Wordsworth's Margaret lamenting : 'rig falsely said That there was ever intercourse Betwixt the living and the dead • For surely then I should have sight Of him I wait for day and night • With love and longings infinite. Society Women and Rural Pursuits.—lreland supplies Pantities of the violets that are sold in the streets of ,Jmendon, and the great provincial towns, when April is aere—with its girlish tears and laughter, as the poet has it--and not a few come from the violet farm that is successfully run by Lady Aileen Wyndham-Quin in Co. Limerick. Another enterprising lady agriculturist is Ellen, Countess of Desert, who has recently started a tobacco farm in Co. Kilkenny. It seems singular that alle of the first attempts to grow tobacco in the " dis- tressful country" should be made by a woman, but many Irish ladies have turned their attention to gardening 241.1t4 agriculture, including the Hon. Frances Wolseley, he only daughter of Lord Wolseley, who is interested IP the school for women agriculturists at Glynde in Zussex. No less practical is Lady Algernon Gordon- 13enaor, who is establishing a fruit-bottling business at rAughton, Oxfordshire. Like her sister Lady Warwick, Algernoon has done much to promote rural Pursuits, and village industries, and believes that her etk_aterprise will do much to encourage fruit growing in mat district. il/44Pril Weddings. —Between Easter and the month of favYo' when marriages are considered unlucky, is a 18 rte time for Society weddings, but this year there Enatc„,ttlp more breathing time between them, owing to rush-Th' being exceptionally early. Still although the numb—tY be a little less, there is no falling off in the that " fashionable weddings, and if is suspected ma not a few Society people remain abroad until PersYni,,artb7 on that account. Unless one has a near is -;-"ali Interest in the bride or the bridegroom, there bee;;_aarneness about these functions, which is apt to al„„,_ monotonous, and the question of wedding gifts uecomes rather a serious one when a dozen or more sra °ne's acquaintances decide to appear at the altar. „Ile of the April weddings, however, will be of more !Pan usual interest to Scottish sociely, that of the Hon. Aber uudley G. second son of the Earl and Countess at Gordon.deen and Miss Cecile Drummond. The wedding htth is &amp;tea for the 25th May, may be said to consti- L'm an alliatice between the two historic banking nOuses of Cautts and Drummond. Miss Drummond's father is a partner in the banking house of that name, Nohilst Lady Aberdeen is a daughter of Mr. Dudley toutts, the first Lord Tweedmouth. Parasols.—lt would seem a little early to consider, question of sunshades, yet these dainty accessories are already given a prominent place in the shops. Flowers lead the way in the decorative design portrayed upon silk or chiffon, these having taken the place of the bird ill(!aigns which were so much the vogue last summer. „Irds'heads of all descriptions are largely employed for ;ne decoration of handles, but these are fixed to what • 118ElallA regarded as the ferrule end of the stick, the rhamsoi being carried as it were, upside down, when not Use. The other end of the stick is finished off with ea rain Mount, but many of the more serviceable tm;,•.,°,af-cas are provided with a leather loop of the aarag crop, which can be slipped over the arm, and j laffords a convenient way of carrying them. letWa, Simple Shirt.—The elaborate shirt is happily I The Rev. Thomas Law,Secretary of the National Council h evidence this Eastertide, the plain varieties has just sent a letter to the 900 Free Church Councils avi„'a Of g come into vogue again. There was always a want! in England and Wales suggesting that on Sunday, April Placriaement, and moreover, something very out of 14th special sermons should be preached having refer- °'about the elaborate shirt, and the simple, ence to the position on the Congo. Many of the d adorned solely with broad tucks; is a Councils have already passed strong resolutions cos- eopgarment, *pp ued relief. So far, the quite plain shirts that have demnatory of the existing conditions of the native areearad, have been built of woollen fabrics, but they population, and at the annual Council at Leeds the nuudavv being prepared in cotton, linen, lawn, and assembled delegates by a standing vote declared " that .ey,. 'la, for summer wear. The cut, needless to say, is the exploitation of that unhappy country has revived c4'thing and the best course the amateur dressmaker the A frican slave trade under worse forms." All Free ahi follow is to buy a perfectly-fitting tailor-made Church ministers are urged to join in this simultaneous /4 'ls and then carefully unpick as much as is necessary I effort to bring the terrible state of affairs on the Congo I,oae side, to take the correct pattern. before British Nolconformists. -tuf's-rassore Silk.—ln the scheme of woman's attire CONVENTION FOR WALES. th.,4°re silk nromises to play a large part throughout !ammer. This delightful fabric is made in several , The Rev. Thomas Law met the leading representatives It eights," the heaviest being employed for motor coats, of the whole of Wales at Shrewsbury to make arrange- .l earl be had in every colour, and every