      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>SAPIENT LEGISLATORS.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons, which derides the proceedings of the suffragettes as ridiculous, has just displayed its own wisdom by sitting twenty- six hours fifty minutes passing an Army (Annual) Bill through Committee. And Members seem quite childishly happy about it. It isn't quite the record, but rt is near it, only three previous sittings surpassing it in length. Oh sapient legis- lators! Oh illustrious governors !—Onlooker. A POPULAR HOSTESS.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt, says Hearth and Home, that Mrs. Herbert Gladstone is one of our most suc- cessful modern hostesses, for as well as being en- dowed with good 'corks Mrs. Gladstone possesses a great charm of voice and manner. Her tastes are artistic—as is evidenced by her pretty clothes, and she has the courage of originality, too, and dresses to suit herself, and not "because it is the fashion." In this respect she is like the Duchess of Rutland and her daughters, Lady Beauchamp, Lady Plymouth, and Lady Tennant, all of whom are to be numbered among Mrs. Gladstone's friends. BETWEEN-SEASON COAT.</p>
        <p>\ Little short-waisted coats possess a smartness all their own; but it must be remembered that it needs a well-set-up figure to carry them off successfully, and they must be accompanied by a well-eut, well-hanging skirt. Our model might I be carried out in almost any woollen fabric. The coat is arranged with a tabbed edge, the two</p>
        <p>front ones tieing oonsiderably long'er- than the others. Some pale cloth, toning with the colour</p>
        <p>chosen for the costume, 'is used to face the collar and cuffs, and these are slightly braided with dark silk cord. The skirt is pleated at each seam, and the front panel shews a small inset at either side, buttoned to correspond with the coat. The hat is of coloured straw, and has a turn-down brim and a small crown encircled with minute roses. A curved buckle lies across the fpont, and a atioured wing is placed on each sick of the hat. ROMANTIC STORY Or A LADY DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>The story of a young German lady's sucoessful struggle with circumstances has been brought to light with the departure for the Chins mission field from Edinburgh of Dr. Pauline Maier. Friulein Maier was the daughter \of a villa.ge builder. of Mockmuche, in Wfirtemberg, where she attended the communal sohool. Family affable six' a strong desire to see the world and pursue her studies further induced the Priiulein to take the bold step of coming alone to this country. In England 'the secured a position as mother's-help; but soon she met with a lady in Edinburgh who gained her admission to the Edinburgh Medical Missionary Society, the society undertaking the cost of her medical edu- cation, while the lady mentioned paid all per- sonal expenses. Studying with great assiduity, she took her degree at Edinburgh University. Physically well equipped for the arduous mis- sionary work of the Cowgate, Fraulein Maier performed a great deal of useful service in that quarter in accordance with the rule among students of the Medical Society. Latterly, through her connection with the Baptists of the city, Miss Maier MI seen by the Baptist Mis- sionary Society to be of the mettle necessary for missionary work abroad, and her friends in Edinburgh have every confidence that, as the first woman medical missionary to the Baptist Missionary Society, she will do ample justice to her reputation. ALPACA SPRING GOWN.</p>
        <p>The spring-like cootume which is before our notice has a somewhat elaborate Lir, but as a matter of fact it is a model that is well within the poweas of the home-worker. The gown is in a fine make of alpaca, a most serviceable mate- rial even in the lightest and most delicate shades. The Pinafore bodice part is of coarse gutipure, the yoke and tmeble- puffed sleeves</p>
        <p>being of the alpaca. The skirt is arranged with inverted pleats down the back and front, and the full lower part its decorated with broad tucks and bands of the guipure. The tippet stole, which, by the way, bide fair to be high in favour this season, is of taffetas matching the gown in colour. It is bordered with a slight molls of the same coloured chiffon, and is caught in at the waist with rosettes and tassels of silk cord.</p>
