      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>How TO PLAT THE PLICNO.</head>
        <p>How TO PLAT THE PLICNO.</p>
        <p>Never leave a piece until you have learnt it; six months later it will be much more difficult to you, says Mr. Mark Hambourg in the April World and His Wife. The maturing of a pteoe in performance—that is, a growing into •it until until you have made it, as it were, a part of yourself, and ,have grasped its meaning to your fullest ability—may require two or three years. By way of variety, it is a good thing, once in a while, to take up a piece that is too difficult, and to work at it; you are sure to learn some- thing in the process. It is an unwise thing to practise after the ear is tired. More 'time is gained in the end by taking up something else, or resting. When the ear is wearied, mistakes will creep in, and much of the good already ac- complished is lost.</p>
        <p>• TO-DAY'S RECIPIL POTAGE PRINTANIER.—Take half a pint of frefib green peas, and break up into small pieces a lettuce, two small or one large onion (or, if they can be had, a few spring onions), and a little parsley, place in a saucepan with a small pieoe of batter, and simmer till tender, using about one pint of water. Beat up the yolks of two eggs, and mix smoothly with about a third of the liquor. Take three pints of stook, and bring it to the boil with the remainder of the liquor, season well with salt and pepper, and just before serving add the eggs. Dried green peas may no usea betore tne tresn ones can oe procured, but must be soaked for at least tmolve bouts befpre using, and will take rather Inger to boil,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SPY'S CONFESSION.</head>
        <p>A SPY'S CONFESSION.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN K. LEYS,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Maiden's Victory," "An Emanci- pated Woman," "In the Grey of the Dawning," "The Old House by the Sea," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The principal incidents of the following story are strictly true—so far, at least, as we may trust the confession of a Russian spy, who went under the name of Nicholas Zarouboff.</p>
        <p>"What nonsense you talk, Count!" cried the lady, turning away her eyes, and ostentatiously looking at nothing. "Nonsense!" echoed Count 3/larioff, bending closer to his enslaver. "Do you call a devotion like mine nothing? Ah, if you only knew! If I only dared to tell you all!" The beautiful Baroness Rodenski gave the young man a look that made the blood course through his veins like wildfire, and the next instant dropped her eyes, as if ashamed of her indiscretion, or afraid to give expression to her feelings.</p>
        <p>"Why tire you-so hard to move? Why will ye 4: not believe me ? " he murmured.</p>
        <p>"Bemuse you talk so much nonsense that I never can tell whether you are in jest or earnest," returned the lady, with some spirit. "After making a fool of me about that Secret Treaty—" "After making a fool of you!" "Yes; trying to persuade me that the story was true, that you held the Government of the Czar in the hollow of your hand, as you expressed it--" "Hush! Hush!" He glanced behind him timidly, as though he feared that a police spy might be lurking behind one of the palms under which they sat. The sound of the sad, complaining violins cams more dreamily to their oars, with the frou-frou of rustling silk, and a subdued murmur of happy laughter and careless talk. "Well, after trying to make me believe all that absurdity, and almost getting me to swallow it, how can you suppose that I could take anything you say seriously ? " "But I tell you—l assure you on my honour— that what I confided to you was strictly true!" The lady gave a sigh, and then she smiled. "Surely you think I am very gullible!" she said. "Look here, Anna--" .</p>
        <p>"And who gave you the right to call me Anna, I should like to know ? " interrupted the lady. But he went on, disregarding her. "If I put the papers into your hands, will you believe me ?</p>
        <p>She had hard work to keep the flash of antic', pated triumph from appearing in her eyes, but elle looked at her adorer demurely enough as she answered: "Of course, it is all very absurd; but I suppose— Well, yes. Of course, if I were quite convinced that you had only spoken fife truth about that affair, if I were sure that you trust me, I would be much more ready to trust in you. That is only natural. But what is the use of saying any more about it ? " "When can I see you alone, Anna ? " asked the Count, ignoring her last words. "Oh, I never go out the day after a great ball like this. lam much too tired. And if you like, I will keep to-morrow afternoon for you. But I shan't be disappointed if you don't turn up. know what you young men are, with your foolish speeches. And now, you really must take me back. My husband will wonder what has become of me." "I have won! I have won!" cried the Countess Rodenski an hour later in her boudoir; and gather- ing the train of her ball-dress over her arm, she twirled round in the excess of her joy, waving her right arm triumphantly over her head. Her husband, a dapper little man of mean presence, stood leaning against the closed door twisting the ends of his moustache, and admir- ing her. "What have you won ? " he asked, speaking with a slight lisp. "The Governorship of the Sara Province. Listen! That young puppy is going to show me a copy of a certain treaty, to prove to me that he has been telling me the ttuth—that he is devoted to me,. and so on. Now, he will bring the papers here, but I do not mean that he shall take them away again —not until I have done with them."</p>
        <p>"And what will you do with them ? " "I shall put them in my muff, and go to the Minister, and say: Won't you let me have this province for my husband? He is such a nice man, and so steady, and so devoted to the Emperor.' And at the same time I will let him understand that I know all about the Secret Treaty with Austria, and have the papers to prove it. Apd then he will make you Governor. They daren't refuse, for they can't tell what use I might make of the documents---"</p>
        <p>"I say, Anna, you had better leave the papers at home, I think, when you call on M. de Trichot." "Well, perhaps you are right. But I will make him understand very clearly that I have them. Now, go away, like a good boy-". "But Anna, this is a very dangerous game to play!" "On the contrary, it is a particularly safe one, my dear Feodore. Make yourself. their master, and you may ask what you like. Besides, you know that we have very little money loft, and it is either some grand coup like this, or ruin. But my plan can't fail. If it should, I have another string to my bow. But I tell you, my bullet can't miss the mark. I salute your Excellency, Governor of Kara!"</p>
        <p>She made her husband a sweeping curtsey, and he west downstairs, still twisting (he ends of his moustache as he went.</p>
        <p>On the following afternoon the gay and handsome Count Marioff, of the Foreign Office, found the Baroness Bodenski alone in her drawing-room. She took (Are not to be the one to revert to the subject of the Secret Treaty. In fact, when the Count pulled a little packet of papers out of his pocket she affected for a moment to have forgotten the conversation of the night before. "I am afraid I am committing a grave indiscre- tion in shewing you theee documents," said the Count, smiling. "But I know that lam safe with you." The Baroness was pricked to the heart. She was obliged to bend over the papers the young man had plaoed in her hands to hide the blush that rose to her cheek.</p>
        <p>At that moment a heavy step, and a voice that tried to sound very loud and masterful, were heard outside, and the Baron walked abruptly into the room. He started and frowned slightly at seeing the Count, then bowed to him with ceremonious politeness. Meanwhile, to the Count's intense relief, the papers had vanished. In vain the Comp, lingered in the drawing-room. The muter of the souse shewed no sign of moving, and it was impossible to get a word apart with the Baroness. It was to the Count an insupportable thought that he must take his departure and leave.; these documents, involving the honour of his Imperial master, in the hands of an outsider, a stranger to the Department. But there was no help for it. He felt that he would rather trust the lady than the lady's husband; and he knew that if he were to ask her for the papers, the Baron would want to know what they were. He was forced to go away without them; but he felt relieved when the charming Baroness whispered to him as 14 gave him her hand, the word, "To-morrow 1"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HOLIDAY COUGHS AND COLDS</head>
        <p>HOLIDAY COUGHS AND COLDS</p>
        <p>Don't Forget that Handy Box of Peps.</p>
        <p>Nothing spoils the pleasure of holiday-making so completely as a nasty persistent throat and chest cough or a heavy cold on the chest or in the head. But a heady box of that unique discovery, Paps, is a sure and pleasant cure for the most obstinate cough or the worst cold.</p>
        <p>Easter is the most welcome of all the holidays,coming at the end of a long and dreary winter's toil ; but it is also the most risky of all the holidays from a health staadpoint, for the fickle weather of early Spring induces the constant fear of catching cold or some chest trouble.</p>
        <p>Keep Peps always handy; take them at frequent intervals during the day, and you will be a stranger to that old throat and chest trouble, which often makes its appearance at this season. As Pepg contain no narcotics and dangerous drugs, they are equally safe and certain for the deep chest coughs which worry the aged, as for the exhausting and distressing cough which troubles the children. Peps suit all ages, and, in an altogether unique manner, bring the pure healing breath of the rich pine-forests to every home. Of all chemists at ls. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box. Every box bears the registered name—Peps—on the lid. Beware of worthless substitutes. See the registered name Peps on every box; without this, none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
