        <p>If you are ill you can make no mistake by first</p>
        <p>doctoring your kidneys. The mild and extraordinary effect of Dr. Kilmer's Swamp-Root, the great kidney remedy, is soon realised. It is world-famous for its wonderful cures of the most distressing cases, and is</p>
        <p>sold on its merits by all chemists and stores in is. 1.41., 2s. 3d. and 4s. 6d. size bottles. You ruay have Vit,B azps: a sample bottle free, also a • qion pamplet telling you how to find Home of iwarap-Root. out if you have kidney or bladder</p>
        <p>trouble. Mention this paper when writing and address DR. KILMER&amp; CO., Dept., 329, TEMPLE CHLMBERS LONDON, E.C. Don't make any mistake, but remember the Jaime, Swamp-Root, Dr. Kilmer's Swamp-Root, and the address.</p>
        <p>o-tnors mon cm nett any toe zutroness was closeted with the Czar's Foreign Secretary. She stayed with him for half-an-hour, and then drove straight home. Her husba.nd as waiting for her in her boudoir.</p>
        <p>No sooner was the door closed behind the Baroness than she threw herself on a couch in a paroxysm of rage. She clenched her little white fists till they looked as though they had been carved out of a block of marble.</p>
        <p>"That M. de Trichot is a fool, a beast, an insensate ass!" she burst nut. "He would not understand me—though I am sure that he knew what I meant perfectly well— and kept on asking what special services you had rendered to entitle you to expect such rapid advancement. At last I told him plainly that you knew too much to be allowed to remain in obscurity. He asked what I meant, and I was obliged to hint that I had certain documents—"</p>
        <p>"You did what? You mad woman! We are lost'.</p>
        <p>"Not at all. M. de Trichot might have had the wit to know that he was making a blunder that would cost his master dear, but apparently he remained unenlightened----" "He unenlightened! You do not know him ! " "Then he refused my offer with his eyes open. I shall have the less hesitancy in selling the secret to the other side. I told you that I had a second string to my bow." "What do you mean ? " asked her h usband, turning very pale.</p>
        <p>"I niean- that we must go to London at ones, and ssf, what. filo, F.ncrligh will Dive us for the CODI 31 the Secret Treaty which that foolish boy gave me last night." "Anna, you must be crazy! I could never do iuch a thing!" ••incteen I Then 1. must go alone. But I did not expect that you would leave me all the work to do." "Anna, you will drive me mad!" cried the Count, striding up and down the little room clasp- ing his head with his hands. "You understand that if we do this we can never return to Russia "Of course not. And we must leave at once. We are not safe for an hour. I will shut myself up in my room and pretend to be ill when Marioff calls this afternoon. But we must leave St. Petersburg to-night. To delay even a day would be madness."</p>
        <p>That afternoon Count Marioff resolved that if he did not get back the copies of the Secret Treaty and the accompanying correspondence that very day he would go to the Foreign Minister and tell him all. He carried out his resolve, for his eyes were open at last. But his confession came too late. When the police called at the residence of Baron Rodenski, the Baron and his wife were safe across the frontier.</p>
        <p>A week later the Baroness Rodenski and her husband were seated at dinner in their private room in White's Hotel in Earl-street, Piccadilly. They were in good spirits, for the Baron had succeeded that very day in seeing the Permanent Under Secretary of the British Foreign OfEce. The Under Secretary had been evidently impressed with the importance of the secret which the Baron had *kr sale, and had hinted that if he was satisLed that the papers were genuine, a high price would be paid for them.</p>
        <p>The Ba;on had ordered a second bottle of cham- ).agne, and the waiter on his way to fetch it passed 'zhe manager, who was listening attentively to a tall, well-dressed man, who looked very much like a superintendent of police off duty. Four men in greatcoats stood in the vestibule._</p>
        <p>- "My name is Ilowitson, and I come from Scot- land Yard," said the stranger, speaking with a slightly foreign accent. "I hold a warrant for the arrest of two swindlers, a woman and a man. They have passed lately under the name of the Baron and Baroness Rodonski. Ah, I see you know some- thing of them. lam in luck to-night. Are they in the house at present ? " "Yes. They are at dinner. matter?"</p>
        <p>What is the</p>
        <p>"Only that I hold an extradition warrant for their arrest. The man is no more a baron than I am. He is an expert swindler, of the name of Bulenberg, and the woman is his accomplice. Just at present they are wanted for a jewel robbery committed some two months ago at Vienna. I will go up and arrest them at once." "But, look here," said the manager, laying a detaining hand on the arm of the detective, "what about nry bill?" "What have I to do with that? But, no," he added, after a moment's thought. "You shall not be the loser. Have the bill made out, and I will settle it when I have had a look at their luggage." This was all the manager wanted. His benevolent neutrality was now secured. The detective beckoned to his satellites, and together they proceeded upstairs, and entered the room in which the Baron and his wife were dining. They started up at the entrance of the five men, and the Baron demanded what they wanted. Without awswering a word, the four ruffians seized both him and his wife, and slipped handcuffs upon their wrists.</p>
        <p>The Baron was stupefied, overwhelmed by a sense of his helplessness. But the Baroness had more courage. She screamed loudly, till one of her captors snatched a napkin from the table, and barbarously forced it into her mouth. The pretended detective was in fact an agent of the Russian Government—the spy upon whose confession this story is founded. His next move was to search the luggage of the victims; and there, to his great joy, he found the papers which had been taken from the Russian Foreign Office. But he was not satisfied with them. His orders were to bring the papers in any case, and the fugitives if possible. And, being an unscrupulous scoundrel, he ruthlessly carried out the plot which he had designed. Two of his assistants strapped up the boxes of the Baron and his lady. Cabs were sent for, and the boxes were carried down and placed upon them. Then came the passage downstairs to the street. This was the only point at which there was real danger to the conspirators. But unfortunately for the captives, they knew very little English, and could scarcely make themselves understood when they passed on their way out of the hotel. The Baroness loudly demanded to see the manager; but he refused to see her, and sent her word by a waiter that she could tell what she had to say to the magistrate next day. His bill had been paid, and that was all he cared about. The Baron was forced into one cab and the Baroness into another. IShe_never saw her husband again.</p>
        <p>The spy entered the cab into which the Baroness had been thrust, and believing that she was more likely to give trouble than her husband, he got in beside her, while two of the hired bullies stepped into each vehicle. Tiacorder to drive to Scotland</p>
        <p>xere‘Wae rnuaircanea out, ana tne prisoners were carried away. In order to secure himself against appeals to passers-by, the spy, while in the cab, seized the Baroness by the nose, and squeezed it till the unfortunate woman was forced to open her mouth. He then thrust into her mouth an instrument like a hollow walnut, with holes in it to allow of the victim breathing—a gag, in fact, said to be used commonly in Russian prisons. Arrived at Scotland Yard, the two cabs were stopped at the entrance, while the sham detective got out and disappeared under an archway. In a few seconds he re-appeared, and as if he had received fresh orders, told the cabmen to drive to a certain house in a quiet street off Chancery-lane. This house was specially fitted for the purpose the Russian had in view, for it had been used in old days as a "sponging house," in which debtors were confined before being taken to prison, and was, in fact, a kind of lock-up. ii was in the possession of a private inquiry agency, who were privy to this cruel and disgraceful plot. They not only gave the abductor the use of their house, but supplied him with the four rascals who helped him to carry it out.</p>
        <p>By the time the cabs and their burdens arrived at Chancery-lane, it was a quarter-past seven. The Baron was taken into one room, and his wife was placed in another. The men who had taken the Baron upstairs left him, of course locking the door behind them; but it was not long before the door was opened again, and the spy came into the room. He rapidly transferred the key to the inner side of the door, locked it, and then approached the prisoner, who was lying on the bed. The sight of this man aroused all the manhood in the Baron's puny figure. "Who are you, who :dare to treat me in this way?" he demanded, speaking, of course, in Russian.</p>
        <p>For answer, the spy pulled out a paper, which he held so that his captive could see it, for the handcuffs had not been removed: It was a letter of instructions authorising the beirer to summon Rodenski to Russia, in order that he might give evidence at the approaching examination of Count Marioff.</p>
        <p>"But that is no excuse for your treating me in this brutal fashion!" exclaimed the prisoner. "And you forget that we are not in Russia."</p>
        <p>- "Thal is exactly what I remembered, Baron," said the spy, calmly. "Had we been in Russia, you would have been forced to obey the summons—here you would have laughed at it. So I was compelled to adopt these rough measures, which, I assure you, none regret more than I do. All we desire is that you should accompany us to St. Petersburg, and give what information you can about the doings of the Count Marioff. Surely you can have no object in shielding him ?" The Baron remained with his eyes cast down, deep in thought. He had no love for the dupe, and was perfectly willing to shield himself at the Count's expense; but 11%; was he to do this with- out incriminating his wife? " "Will you let me see the Baroness?" he asked.</p>
        <p>The spy- shook his head. "That cannot be allowed for the present," he said, adding immediately. "If you bath consent to come back to Russia with us, I might be able to permit an interview."</p>
        <p>- "I will not gl back to Russia!" cried the Baron, suddenly. "How can I tell what fate would await me there? How can you expect that I should pu faith in the promises of vermin like you ? " The spy was totally unmoved by this insult. "I could not help myself," he said, in a depre- cating tone. "There was no other way of securing an interview with you, and pressing on you the views of the Czar's Government. I repeat that there is no idea of accusing you of anything beyond imprudence in not immediately informing the Police of all you knew and suspected. And that Indiscretion you can now atone for by coming back with me voluntarily, and answering the questions that may be put to you." "Will you let me see the Baroness? " was the only answer. Again Petronieff shook his head.</p>
        <p>"I must see her! I shall go mad if Ido not see her! How can I tell what is happening to her at this moment? It is needless cruelty to separate us. I will promise anything if you will only take me to her, and allow us to remain together!" And the unhappy man stretched out his manacled hand/ to his gaoler, and burst into a flood of tears. But the spy was as impervious to an appeal of thie kind as he had been to insult. His answer was to gc to a cupboard, from which he brought a tumbler: some water, and a decanter containing brandy. "If you will give me your word of honour that you will attempt no violence, or make any effort to escape in the meantime," he said, "I will take off these handcuffs, which must be very much in your way, and offer you a glass of brandy and water. lam sure you must require it." The wretched man hesitated. There was silence, dead silence in the house. Not a sound fell upon the ear but the distant roll of a cab, heard indistinctly through the double window. "Long before your screams could attract attention, there would be a gag in your mouth," said the spy, :orreetly interpreting the prisoner's glance, "and you see that the casement is barred with iron. Come, you had better make the best of it, and allow me to offer you a glass of this excellent brandy." "Is it drugged ? " "Drugged? Of course not! See, I will take a little myself." The Baron yielded. His fetters were removed, and he eagerly swallowed a stiff glass of brandy and water. He was then persuaded to have a second, and after a short, time (spent partly in threats, partly in tearful entreaties to be allowed to see his wife), he consented to take a little more. This finished him. In a half-dazed condition he was taken down- stairs, again put into a cab, and driven to a rail- way station in time to catch the Continental mail train. He was then transported to Russia, being kept well plied with liquor the whole way. The Czar's agent had provided himself with a forged certificate of lunacy—that is part of his confession —in case of his victim making an appeal to the police; but the Baron was so bemused with brandy, and so thoroughly cowed by the treatment he had received, that he never made the appeal that might have saved him.</p>
        <p>The Baron was kept in prison for a year, and released at the end of that time, a broken and ruined man. He was sent to a small town in the interior of the country, and compelled to drag out his existence there, under the supervision of the police. As for the Baroness, her fate was still worse. She was kept gagged and handcuffed for two hours by her inhuman captors. At the end of that time she fainted. While in this condition brandy was poured down her throat by means of a funnel, and she sank into a stupefied sleep, which lasted till the following morning. Preparations had been made in advance for the transportation of this unhappy victim of the Czar. A coup, had been engaged in the train, and a private cabin on board the steamer. It was given out that the lady was mentally afflicted, and was being taken home under the care of her own servants. In a drugged and helpless state she was carried off to Harwich, and thence to Ostend. In due time Russia was reached. She was ;hrown into prison, and there—murdered. That is in say, she was never seen again, and about a year ifter her abduction it was given out that she was lead. Whether her death was caused by mental igony, or by the more merciful methods of poison n- strangulation matters little. Actual violence :ould scarcely increase the victim's sufferings, or uld to the indignation which every Englishman nust feel at the crime, or series of crimes, by which he was deprived of life. (Ti's END.</p>
        <p>Li foi 1111 -z.-. • vir -14-14 .14 in I 4:111.t an underst</p>
