      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXANLINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXANLINER.</p>
        <p>APRIL 6th. ISO 7</p>
        <p>National expectation is now THE COMING naturally enough, beginning BUDGET, to centre itself on Mr. ASQUITH. The Budget is almost due. Thanks chiefly to the millionaires, the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER will hi.ve surplus. What will he do with it ? The amount, it must first of all be understood, is not so large that it will work wonders. The realised surplus for the financial year just coming to an end is a little under five millions and a half. This, however, must not be looked upon as the actual sum of which Mr. ASQUITH has to dispose. Released surpluses are devoted to the paying off of the National Debt. They do not go back into the pocket of the taxpayer. Reduc- tions of taxation, when there are any, are made on the basis of calculations as to next year's national income and expenliture. The CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER does not merely attribute, as many people suppose, what has accumulated from the year before. There are good reasons for believing that the surplus estimated for in the coming Budget will fall rather considerably below five millions and a half. It will probably be three millions, and that is the amount of which Mr. ASQUITH will have to dispose through reductions or remissions of taxation.</p>
        <p>The CHANCELOR of the EXCHEQUER must be feeling something like the greedy countryman who said that the goose was an awkward bird because it contained too much for one, and not enough for two ; for this would be precisely the case with a surplus of three millions. Mr. As QUITH is expected on one hand to reduce the taxation on the sugar, and on the other to lower the Income-Tax. It is impossible to do both these things by any simple operation of finance. He can do one or the other. He can take a penny off the Income-Tx, for example, and this will leave him with £400,000 to dispose of, a penny now produc- ing the large sum of £'2,600,000. Nothing more strongly illustrates the rapid growth of the nation's wealth than the continuous increase in the pro- I ductiveness of the Income-Tax. When that impost was instituted a penny produced a million pounds. Since then its productiveness has gone on growing until now it produces more than two million and a half pounds. This extraordinary fecuudity of the tax on incomes has its drawbacks to a Chancellor of the Exchequer. The more a penny in the pound produces, the more difficult it</p>
        <p>becomes to remit the penny. Such a remission I swallows up a respectable surplus at once. Yet there are those who are expecting a reduction of twopence, or even threepence, in the pound. Doubtless the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER has drawers, or waste-paper baskets, full of letters telling him how it might be done. The Budget, we may be certain, will be far from being so simple an affair as the reduction of the sugar duty or the lightening of the Income-Tax would make it. The great Budgets have not been those which were built up on big surpluses. Mr. GoscHEN had one considerable surplus after another without seeming to do anything for the tax-payer. OR the other hand, Mr. GLADSTONE could work miracles with a small surplus or with none at all. It is fair to remember, however, that in the days of Mr. GLADSTONE'S great Budgets there was a much more complex system of taxation to deal with. Every - thing was tared, from salt to saltpetre, from cradles to coffins, a happy state of things which the Tariff Reformers seem eager to restore. If Mr. ASQUITH is to distinguish himself it will not be by just producing a surplus and giving it away. We are expecting from him a Budget which will be memorable for much more than that, and, in all probability, for its attempt to grapple with the whole Income-tax problem, with the view of retaining its productiveness whilst lightening its incidence on small incomes. It would be unjust to the CHANCELLOR Of the EXCHEQUER to say that he has a great opportunity. He has, however, an opportunity which may be converted into a gieat one if he has a real genius for finance.</p>
        <p>We are glad to see that the THE BOROUGH Finance ' Committee has AUDIT. recommended the appoint-</p>
        <p>scent of a professional auditor to audit the accounts of the Corporation. This is one of the things that the Non-Traders have always strongly adocated, hut the credit for the proposal having now taken practical shape belongs in a great measure to Councillor Ball who trongly advised a professional audit in the Qurse of a letter specially written to the Committee prior to the last Council meeting. The matter has since been considered and a return obtained as to the appointment of such auditors elsewhere. We have little doubt that the Council will on Tuesday confirm the resolution,though there may be some cavil at the appointment of another salaried official. It is probable that in addition to the professional auditor, the elective auditors would continue to be appointed, but their duties would become practically nominal.</p>
        <p>Among the names of Eng- THE HOWARD lishmen who have devoted MEMORIAL. their life work to the cause of suffering humanity, that of John Howard, the prison reformer, will ever hold a foremost place. He it was who brought to light the revolting evils of the old prison system and was the pioneer of more humane methods of dealing with wrong doors. We in Warrington are proud to remember that this truly great man was closely associated with the old town, for he often stayed here and his famous work on "The State of Prisons " which opened the eyes of England to the enormity of the prison horrors was issued from Eyres' press. It is fitting therefore that Warrington should have a Howard memorial and the tablet which was unveiled yesterday by Major. Griffiths will be a perpetual reminder to the community of the debt which England owes to men like John Howard who did not hesitate to sacrifice life itself to uplift the fallen and down-trodden and to arouse the public conscience to a higher conception of its responsi- bilities. That such an ardent prison reformer and faithful servant of his country as Major Griffiths should unveil the tablet was thoroughly appropriate. His speech at the luncheon was an eloquent tribute to John Howard's undying work, but his most interesting remarks were those in which he welcomed the change which is happily taking place in our ideas of dealing with crime. "Prevention is better than cure " is an old motto and a true one, and the Major rightly advocates the reformatory principle of weaning boys from disastrous courses before the evil becomes chronic.</p>
        <p>Easter was unusually enjoy- THE DUST able because of the glorious NUISANCE. weather which prevailed, but on tho high road enjoy- ment was tempered with dust. Cyclists and pedestrians got an inkling of what they may expect during the coming summer, and the prospect is anything but &amp;luring. There were motor cars in 'myriads everywhere and react-dust hung in the air to a depth of 20 feet almost continuously. We notice that the nuisance was brought thus early to the notice of the Warrington Rural Council on Wednesday and very seriously dis- cussed. Mr. Newcombe, the surveyor, gave it as his opinion and the opinion of other Surveyors that chemical treatment of the road was of little use from their point of view, for the simple reason that while it laid the dust it ruined the road. At Knutsford, be said, an extra cost of £6OO had been incurred on the portion of road which was chemically treated last summer. If this is the result of dust-laying by liquid, then some other method will have to be devised to remedy the evil. Apparently, the only way out of the difficulty is either to pave the roads with setts or to make them of a more holding material. This would so add to the cost that the State will have to come to the aid of the County and Rural Councils before they will undertake the work. We see no reason why the Government should not immediately concern itself more with " the King's highway." There are State roads in France and in other countries, and it will be much to the dis- credit of England if she shows herself unable te cope with the difficulties brought about by the increasing motor traffic for, though serious, they are not insnperable.</p>
        <p>Not since the famous A NOTABLE Maybrick case has so much REPRIEVE. sympathy been shown for a prisoner condemned to die as has been manifested fcr Horace George Rayner during the last few days. The tragic conditions of the young man's upbringing, and the pathos attaching to his claims, have united in creating more compassion for the murderer than for his victim ; and immediately Rayner was lodged in Pentonville, steps were taken to procure a rnonstre petition for the mitigation ot his sentence. In addition, the merits of the sentence were canvassed in the Press, all classes of people of eminence in various departments of public life being prevailed upon to give their opinions and it was with a feeling of general relief that on Saturday evening last it was made known that Mr. Gladstone felt warranted in advising his Majesty to respite the capital sentence with a view to its commutation to penal servitude for life. It would have been a sad commentary on twentieth ' century civilization if, after we had dragged Rayner from the very jaws of death by careful nursing, we had terminated his life a few months later by the hangman's rope, and we are glad that this will not be his fate. But such considerations are, after all, sentimental, and not in accord with justice. Rayner's was a clear case of nvirder, and as " Truth" has remarked, it is an injustice to other murderers that the last of their number ; should thus escape the extreme penalty of the law. The question which is brought home to us ! is the advisability of capital punishment being discontinued in England. Objections to it get stronger every year, and the feeling of the public seems to be gradually tending towards its aboli- tion. Now that. Rayner has been spared, the case of the abolitionists will be immeasurably strengthened, and we wish them well in their crusade against this relic of barbarism which finds , a place in English law.</p>
        <p>We are essentially a prac- LEEDS—AND AFTER. tical people, and _after we _</p>
        <p>have -had time to look back upon the great series ot meetings comprising the National Free Church Council at Leeds it is only natural that we should ask what these things stand for, what are the precise results of the meetings? In the first place we must not under- rate the enthusiasm engendered by these meet- ings. Tnere is a disposition amongst cold-blooded critics to discount enthusiasm, and set for nought the thrill that moves a great gathering. But should not the fervour which is created stand for some- thing very definite and produce practical results ? There could not have been a man or woman present at the Leeds meeting who was not moved and thrilled at some time. No one can work without inspiration. Any one who works at all knows what it is to firm the fire burning low and in need of rekindling. If leaders from all parts of the coiantry have been reinspired, should not each in his district pass on the sacred flame and let it result in warmth, light, and energy ! So let us not forget the fervid meetings and the vows solemnly taken, but harness the power to the wheels, and set the machinery going. There were many things we undertook to do at Leeds— to bring deliverance to the) Congo natives to assist in the national campaign on the Lord's Day, to help to terminate British connection with the opium traffic, to interpose with God and man on behalf ot peace between the nations. These things must not be forgotten. Then there are the great social questions at our hand. It is easy to " consider " them, to debate thew and pass resolu- tions on them, but we are hoping (says the " Free Church Choncicle ") that when the Council meets at Southport nest year it will be possible to record a real, advance on definite lines of social work. There is much to be done in the establishment of social institutes, Scc., and if the Leeds meetings produce work of this description they will not have been held in vain.</p>
        <p>Principal Fairbairn, in a SCHOLARSHIP AND fragment of theological CONGREGATIONALISM. autobiography in this</p>
        <p>month's- Contemporary," has a remarkable review of Germail philosophical thought and Biblical criticism. It focusses the period covered by the Victorian era. Recent controversies have been directing attention to the early centuries historically, to palreography, and to the work of the modern Germans. One, at any rate, of the nominees for the Presidency of the Congregational Union has done some- thing to bring the ordinary reader into two of these provinces, while the new President of the National Council of the Free Churches is an expert in the third. Principal Adeney is the editor of the Century Bible, an admirable series of anno- tated volumes, one devoted to each book in the canon, and all embodying the results of modern • constructive criticism, on the lines of the older Cambridge Bible. Like the Cambridge Bible too, they are well bound, and issued at a popular rate. Besides supplying two of these volatiles, Dr. Adeney, who is also Lecturer on the History of Doctrine at the University of Manchester, has written two serviceable volumes on the Church history of the first eight centuries —a field in which Dean Milman's work has long been famous. Dr. Massie, another of the five nominees for the Presidency of the Congregational Union, i a contributor to the Century Bible .and to Dr. Hastings' Dictionary of the Bible. It is worth observing that Dr. Massie, who was for seven years Yates Professor of New Testament Exegesis at Mansfield College, Oxford, where Dr. Fairbairn is Princip,d, has just been made President of that ancient and decidedly liberal body known as the Protestant Dissenting Deputies. Dr. R-ndel ifirris, who will speak at the Free Cnurch Council's Welsh Convention, has done notable work among the ancient documents. He was, of course, one of the editors of the Syrian Gospels discovered in palimpsest upon Mount</p>
        <p>L )rd Kitchener's statement INDIAN MILITARY t) the Council of the POLICY. Governor-General at Cal- cutta puts some aspects of the work he was sent to India to accomplish before the public mind. Indian military policy, as he pointed out, is purely defensive. Tne • British and the Native Army in India are not maintained for the purpose of being available for attack on any one. If war were to break out between this country and Russia their duty would be to keep the Indian Empire intact--their business does not include any attack other than that • necessitated by defence. But Lord Kitchener is determined, that for the pur- poses of defence his Army shall be as well placed as possible. Until he was sent out by Mr. Brodrick and Lord Roberts to put the Army in a proper condition there had been little change since the Mutiny. No oonsistent attempt had been made to distribute the forces at the dis- posal of the Indian Government in the manner most advantageous for the purpose for which they are intended. Conditions have changed since 1857. The strategic significance of the North- West Frontier has widened. Russia has come up to the borders of Afghanistan ; her scouts scan the passes that lead to the rich land beyond. Against these new conditions new measures are required, and it is to the task of providing them that Lord Kitchener has devoted hitatell. He is forming the Indian Army into divisions 60 established en 6chelon, one behind the other, along the rail- ways that they may be rapidly concentratea in time of work. The work is not easy of accomp- lishment, and Lord Kitchener has devoted con- siderable time to it. it is Co be hoped that his labours will be recognised when at last he has evolved a system out of the rather haphazard distribution of the troops upon which ludia has ' relied before.</p>
        <p>There is much virtue in THE " TWOPENNY twopence, which in Ireland, DAM." is pronounced toopence, • and in this country tuppence. Martin Tupper did not approve of the Irish way. This amount is convenieLt if a man desires to swear. Everyone knows that the Duke of Wellington, whose hingudge was always circum- spect, when he had a fit of ire in Spain said he did not care "a twopenny dam." It is acknowledged that, at heart, the British working-man is a good fellow, but a general regret is expressed that, in choice of words in conversation, his language is not of paler hue. If he would substitute for some of his scarlet geraniums of speech the " twopenny dam 'he might be admitted into the drawing- room, where masculine expressions other- wise are welcomed. The ladies being more modest than men .revel in the 'farthing. Twopence, to them, implies extravagance. If an article is marked one-eleveL-three they are satisfied although they know that they will uut receive a quarter-of-a-penny change. The Spanish dam was , equivalent to our mopence, and twopence is regarded by a cabman as equivalent to a pint of beer. If ladies would only recognise the virtue there is in twopence they would not dread the caustic tongue of the cabby, as they frequently do. Men seldom have trouble with cabmen, owing to the secret they possess. If a cabby is given sixpence over his legal fare he regards the donor as a tool, and, with an injured air, demands a shilling. If be gets his exact fare his feelings are hurt, and he considers the person who has hired ;iim stingy. The point is to pay him his just due and twopence -extra. He theil looks pleased. Sometimes he touches his hat. In any case, he regards the giver of the twopence as either a gentleman or a Duchess, as the case may be. Shop Fire,</p>
        <p>Considerable damage was caused by a fire at the shop of Mr. Arthur Booth, Buttermarket-street, on Saturday. The outbreak was observed shortly after one o'clock and Superintendent Turner and a number of firemen at once turned out with the steam fire engine "Captain." On their arrival they found the beams running alongside the chimney well alight, the flames haring been caused by a defect in the brick- work. To avoid damaging the goods a number of hand chemical extinguishers were used, and the fire was soon extinguished.</p>
