        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>He : " It's absolutely unless to urgue further. Ton ought to know that. My will is of iron." She: "Yes, I know —pig iron." *</p>
        <p>* * , Notwithstanding an increase of 2d. in the pound in the call upon the rates for Educational purposes, it is expected that Alderman Bennett will be able to announce on Tuesday a considerable reduction in thegeneral rates of the Borough.</p>
        <p>* * , Peace and harmony have reigned supreme at the local vestry meetings—a fact which has been parti- cularly welcome to the long-suffering scribe who has had only too/much reason to remember the" scenes" of byegone gears. * * 4</p>
        <p>Death has unfortunately been busy in our midst this Easter and five well-known townsmen in the Persons of ex-Councillor T. Wright, Mr. William Porshaw, Mr. T. G. :Grounds, Mr. William Cummins, and Mr. Josiah Whittle have all passed away.</p>
        <p>Socialism for the Socialists! A younger son was wont to air opinions of a very Socialistic character in the house of his father,a millionaire. To this particular son, on account of his very advanced views, the ionaire has left (says the " World ") nothing "eYcald what would come to him by settlement.</p>
        <p>* The waggon of Socialism needs to be hitched to ut star of religious faith."—Rev. R. J. Campbell]. Ihe ancient faiths linked star to star, and baund Them into constellations circling round The spacious dome of home. The new would hitch Them to a mill to grind the rich. London Opinion."</p>
        <p>0 ....The title of Britain's wealthiest woman is held by P-Iss Emily Charlotte Talbot, an unmarried lady, who ls but little known outside her native Wales, where 8110 owns two magnificent estates. Miss Talbot inherited from her father all his real estate in Wales, Which is said to be worth over a million and a half. She lives very quietly on her Welsh property (says the " Tatler "), rarely. visiting her London house, and dispenses charity m a splendid manner. There is a friendly rivalry between Warrington's 'two leading Male Voice Choirs and on Monday they found themselves in competition at the musical 'festivals at Chester and Rhyl. On each occasion the Apollo Choir had the good fortune to be victorious a feat upon which they are deserving of the warmest congratulations. Nor had the Warrington Male Voice Chou. reason to be disheartened, for they carried off t,he second award at Chester, and are no doubt look- ing forward to beating their Warrington friends at 84P_Ine future Eisteddfod. On present form, however, "le Apollo Choir will not be easily accounted for. • *</p>
        <p>". It is a very charming tsibute which the National nion of teachers have paid to Dr. Macnamara on his Ittirement from their ranks. They have presented genial lii4ibman's wife with the lease of the clotor's house at Hernehill. Very properly it is a i.028e with an Irish name, Clontarf, and it is conven- ,,„_":Qd3r near some golf links. Dr. Macnamara is still rni.A.to have done so much. He began his life ti;4ll, y " in arms," for he was born in Canada where. tather was serving as a non-commissioned officer. 448 been in arms ever since; but nobody bears _4 Malice on that account, for he fights in such a breezy way that he never leaves a wound uenind.</p>
        <p>,Ma i°,l' Arthur Griffiths told an amusing story in '''SP°ll_,Ciing to a vote of thanks at the Old Academy ; its„eteraaY. When he was Deputy-Governor of one of prisons, a prisoner was brought before him, escorted by two warders, for misconduct. The Major ,asked him what he had to say, and he retorted "I have got this to say" and stooping down he took off his boot and hurled it at the head of the astonished Deputy-Governor. Whereupon, continued Major Griffiths, one warder drew his staff and knocked the other warder down. Amid laughter the Major pointed the moral that he was quite knocked down" on receiving the invitation to perform that ceremony, as he saw no reason why he should be asked. * * A pleasing incident at the final meeting of the old zloard of Guardians on Wednesday was the tribute ?aid by several members to the services of Mrs. Clay. has to be recalled that Mrs. Clay was the first lady eardian in Warrington, and that she entered the 450ard Room twelve years ago when all the other tembers were men. At , that time the presence of ulies on public bodies was not so common as now and Zs. Clay was a pioneer on behalf of her sex. She N. 417 soon proved not only her personal usefulness and of ?tie, but the suitability of ladies for various branches °or Law work. As Mr. Forshaw so well said 1.,,8Qe had a keen and correct judgment and by her gnation, which they all deplored, the Board lost of its best members.</p>
        <p>~The Rev. J. S. Hughes concluded fourteen yearsl Istry at Hill Cliffe Baptist Church on Sunday last, t gell a large congregation assembled in the evening hear his farewell sermon. The respect in which aughes is held was evidenced at the close of the rliee, when he and Mrs. Hughes were made the toe°iPients of handsome presents. When he first came the district Hill Cliffe Church was in very low ;Later, but by dint of untiring effort Mr. Hughes 'anged all this, and a few years ago it was one of the ;71.105t flourishing places of worship near Warrington. &amp;ftterly and Unfortunately differences of opinion arose Vuat resulted in a split in the congregation. Mr, h.ghes fought well under these adverse conditions for Church, but lately his health has given way under strain, and he has been forced to seek rest and retirement from active work. We join with the c°llgregation in wishing him well in the future.</p>
        <p>* * * The management of the Savoy Theatre, who had arranged to revive "The Mikado," have.in consequence tl representations made to them, decided not to place The Japanese opera on the stage at the present moment ere is no doubt, says the London correspondent of s Liverpool "Daily Post and Mercury" that the Lspresentations referred to have been made at the tance of the Japanese Government. The Mikado ',ade have been somewhat of a caricature of Japanese as, but its amusing extravagance deprived it of the ..11aracter of offensive or ill-natured satire. The npanese Government have in this matter shown lieroselves a little too sensitive, and they would have '°/le wiser if they had regarded "The Mikado SS :erely a Western ,burlesque of Japanese habits and otions. The Mikado. however, is regarded in Japan as almost a sacred person. To do anything that woald 12tay him ridiculous or comic would be highly offen- 81ve to the Japanese, and it was, I believe, on this f;:lsvand that the representations were made to the Savoy ;;Istinagement. In view of the existing relations between irls country and Japan, it was not possible to resist " appeal, but the public will regret the indefinite tdatPonement of one of the most amusing of the bert-Sullivan operas.</p>
