      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON;</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON;</p>
        <p>SHOP ASSISTANTS AND MR. SEDDON, M.P.</p>
        <p>At the annual conference of the National Union of Shop Assistants on Monday,the delegates on the motion of Cardiff, appointed Mr. J. A. Seddon, ALP., as their Parliamentary representative, with a salary of £lOO a year. VIOLENT EARLESTOWN PRISONER.</p>
        <p>William Lee, who has a record of thirty-six previous convictions and a well-known Earlestovvn character, was sent to gaol at the Warrington County Sessions on Wednesday for one month for a violent assault on Police-constable Dawson on Saturday night. The officer stated that Lee was creating a disturbance, and when taken into custody he became very rough, butting him in the face and kickin shim several times. FUNERAL OF THE LATE MR. LI.MOND.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. Walter Limond who died on Tuesday last week took place on Saturday it Newton Cemetery, and was attended by a large number of friends from Preston and Rarlestown. The deceased had been employed in the estate office of the London and North-Western Railway at Preston for over 20 years, but always returnad home at the end of the week The Baptist denomination in Barlestown has sustained a severe less, as deceased was the secretary of their Sun- day school, and principal bass singer in their choir and will be sadly missed. There were several beautiful wreaths from the members of the family, Sunday school choir, congregation, friends and fellow workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LOCAL PRESENTATIONS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PRESENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>A presentation has been made to Mr. J. W. Thorp, of the Wellington Inn, of a smoker's cabinet, from mem- bers of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers, in recognition of his services as treasurer for 39 years A beautifully framed certificate was given to Bro. Davies, for his services to the society. Bro. Fred Stott, of Warrington, was the chairman. Mr. Edwin Worsley, of Barlestown, has been the recipient of %. very handsome travelling bag, from his fellow agents 3f the Blackburn Philanthropic Burial Society, on his relinquishing his connection with the society, to take up similar work in Toronto. Mr. Holt, president of the society, in making the presentation, expressed his regret at parting with such a valuable colleague, and wished him every success in Canada.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SCANDALOUS STATE OF BURTONWOOD CEMETERY.</head>
        <p>SCANDALOUS STATE OF BURTONWOOD CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of his sermon on Sunday eyening the Vicar of Burtonwood, Newton-le-Willows; drew attention to the pressing need of cemetery drainage. Re-opened graves, he asserted, were often found to be little better than puddles, and he had been forced, much against his will, to bury the dead in mud-and-water graves. The state of things would go from bad to worse and claimed immediate attention. The work should be done quickly and thoroughly ; nothing should be put before it. Some person or persons had ignorantly declared the garish Council to be free from all obliga- tion as to this important !matter. Responsibility for seemly and reverent burial of the dead must certainly rest upon the new Council. He hoped Easter would sea the matter taken up earnestly by all denomina- tions.</p>
        <p>FATAL SEQUEL TO AN ACCIDENT AT THE</p>
        <p>VIADUCT WORKS.</p>
        <p>Last Thursday morning Mr. Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted an inquest at the Town Hall on the body of William Shaw, of 24, Brunswick-road, aged 39, who died after an illness of several months, and the question was whether an accident which happened last May was a cause of death.—Mr. Sumner, factory inspector, was present.—Mr. C. W. Eiarnett attended on behalf of the London and North-Western Co., and in answer to the coroner, said the Company did not dispute the facts, or that the accident was the cause of death. The Company had in fact paid compensation.—lVlrs. Shaw, the widow. said her husband was a furnaceman at ithe Viaduct works, When he returned home from work on May 11, last year, he complained of a " tup "having fallen on him and hurt him. Dr. Gange attended him ; he I was operated on in Manchester Inflrmary,and afterwards went to a convalescent home, but never recovered and never worked again. The Company had paid him 325. per week during his illness.—Fellow-workmen of the deceased, named Henshaw and Cartwright, explained that the " tup " was a piece of iron weighing about 2icwt., slung by a chain, and used for ramming in a key. Helping to lift the tup off the hook, the deceased fell down with it, and said "I'm done." He tried to go on with his work, hut being in great pain be went and reported the accident to Mr. Clare, the foreman, He never worked after. It was not clear whether the hurt was caused by the tup falling on him or by strain- ing himself in the lifting.—Dr. Gray (successor to Dr. Gange) who had made a post-mortem. examination, said death was caused by exhaustion; the primary cause being injury to the right hip-bone.—Verdict, "Death from accident."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>AMPLE PROPORTION</head>
        <p>THE AMPLE PROPORTION</p>
        <p>Mark Twain tells the following story :—I visited Quite frequently one msy cut right through the the Governor of Buffalo. There appeared to be about middle of a loaf of currant bread and discover only two sixteen doors to his spacious room. I seated myself or three solitary currants. In puddings e of ddingilland in ceurjneanetbroreuad there is famous ymvous on the corner of the table, and immediately from rule— each .of the sixteen doors a young man emerged, and clever housewife, anxionsgto fosteerepirni her sprinkling."family the thefi, ysixteen lined up. The Crevdt•nor said to them : I love for this remarkably nutritious and appetising fruit, our services are not required. Mr. Clemens is ', learns to govern her hand so as never to fall short of sitting on the bells." There was a cluster of sixteen the ample proportion. , bell buttons on the corner of the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>"NEUTRAL IN ALL MATTERS POLITICAL AND RELIGIOUS."</head>
        <p>"NEUTRAL IN ALL MATTERS POLITICAL AND RELIGIOUS."</p>
        <p>THE " GUARDIAN'S " CONVERSION.</p>
        <p>The phrase at the head of this column is a familiar one to those local newspaper readers who have patronised the "Guardian." For many years it has appeared weekly and mid-weekly beneath the title of the paper. People have doubted the neutrality of the "Guardian" at times of political stress and excite- ment, only to be brought up suddenly by its oft- repeated public profession, of faith: "Neutral in all matters political and religious." We have heard that the constant repetition of a story or a saying is suffi- cient to ensure that it will ultimately be believed. The (.` Guardian" apparently works to this plan. By telling the public week in and week out that it really is neutral, people have alinost got to put faith in the statement. How much longer the phrase in question will continue to appear on the " Guardian " title-page is a problem, for the " Guardian " has changed hands, and the "hands " are not by any means those of men usually considered neutral in all matters political and religious. Since the beginning of the year] there have been rumours in interested circles that the " Guardian " had been bought by the Tories and the Brewers. People were horrified at the prospect of such a sad fate. The "Guardian" has been the fountain which for so long has poured forth streams of colourless opinion. It has been a sort of newspaper grandmother which never hurt anyone's feelings in matters political and religious. All its sins were sins of omission and not of commiasion. It , never said you must vote for "so and so "in preference to his opponents. Instead it gave" so and. so " grandmotherly•pats on the back and forgot all about the other fellow, which answered the purpose just as well! Alas and alackl Rumour seems but too true. We learn that Mackie and Co., Limited was registered as a new company on the 18th February last with a nominal capital of £25,000, and that the seven gentlemen forming the company according to statutory requirements are : Sir Gilbert Greenall Mr. Wm. Peter ,Rylands Mr. Roger C. Parr Mr. S. Fulton Smith Mr. E. J. Hall Mr. J. Charlton Parr Mr. W. H. Bleckly.</p>
        <p>Of these gentlemen he first five have each a seat on the Board of Directors,of which Sir Gilbert Greenall is chairman. A sixth director is Mr. William Richard- son, the only survivor of the old Board. While the naper may remain As neutral as ever, by no stretch of imagination can it be conceived that there is any political or religious neutrality about the personnel of the new Board of Directors. They are all well-known Tories, and two of them are directly associated with the two principal breweries in the borough. If the " Guardian" succeeds in maintaining its neutrality under such strongly partizan control it will have accomplished the most remarkable feat of its career. We shall be interested in seeing how it comports itself when such matters as the new Licensing Bill, Tariff Reform, and a local Parliamentary election are on the tapis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES-.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES-.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNCIL AND BAD RAILWAY SERVICE. At the monthly meeting of Runcorn Urban District Council on Wednesday, Mr. Marshall, the secretary,said now was the time for the Council to take action to get an improved railway service to Chester during the summer months.—Mr. unison said a representation</p>
        <p>ought to be made to the railway • company as to the accommodation prpvided by thel.lo train to Liverpool. It was disgraceful and scandalous the way the company treated the public who had the misfortune to travel by that train. People were herded like cattle.—A com- mittee was appointed to make representations to the railway committee. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 6, 1907, OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Services for Sunday:</head>
        <p>Services for Sunday:</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church: Preacher, 10.30 and 6:30, Rev. John Yonge. Bold-street Wesleyan Church: Preachers, 10.30 Rev. S. Pitt; 6.30, Rev. J. Manning. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church: Preachers, 10.30 Rev. J. Manning ; 6.30, Rev. S. Pitt. Golborne-street Baptist Church: Preacher, 10.30 and 6.30, Mr. John Harrison, Liverpool. St Paul's Church. "We are pleased "says St. Paul's Parish Magazine, "that we have been fortunate enough to receive from the Liverpool Diocesan Benefices Augmentation Fund another promise of £lOO towards our Endowment Fand. The condition attached to this promise is that we as a congregation mast meet it by another £lOO. Then in November next we offer £2OO to the Ecclesiastical Commissioners. We are sanguine enough to anticipate that they will meet it by another £2OO. This then will increase the living of St. Paul's another £l2 a year for all time."</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. BRACECAMP presided over the annual vestry meeting which was held last evening. The accounts showed that the income during the year from the offertories had been £509 14s. &amp;L and that the total income of the church had been £987 is. 7d. The year commenced with a debit balance of Viand closed witha deficit of £4 17s.—The vicar nominated Mr. J. H. Smethurst as his warden whilst Mr. Rigby was re-elected sepia's warden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Rev. J. S. Hughes Closes His Ministry.</head>
        <p>Rev. J. S. Hughes Closes His Ministry.</p>
        <p>After having for fourteen years occupied the position I of pastor of Hill Olin Baptist Church. the Rev. J. S. Hughes on Sunday closed his ministry in connection with that place of worship. In the evening he delivered his farewell sermon to a crowded congregation. For his text he took a portion of St. Paul's valedic- tory address to the Romans: "For lam not ashamed of the Gospel of Christ, for it is the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth to the Jew first, and also to the Greek." Romans i. 16. In the course of a very earnest sermon, Mr. Hughes pointed ont that that was the text from which he preached on the first Sunday of his pastorate nearly 14 years ago. He began by preaching the Gospel of Christ, and he would finish by doing so. There were many things in cennection with religion, and with religious life of which they might well be ashamed. There were superstitions and heathen practices of the dark ages the rags of which he was ashamed to say,some would still cause to clothe the comely form of religion at the present day and there were many things in the Churches which made them bow their heads in humilia- tion and shame. Narrowness, bigotry, strife, spleen, ambition, pharasaical bitterness were the things which made the Churches stink in the nostrils of the people outside and which made them wish that their Lord would come again with a whip and cleanse His temple. Of those things he was ashamed but he saw no reason why he should be ashamed of the Gospel. He had preached no fewer than 2,000 sermons from that pulpit, and he could sincerely and earnestly say that his ambition and endeavour had ever been to proclaim the gospel the great Apostle Paul was so ready to preach in ancient Rome. and he (Mr. Hughes) enunciated those principles of which the apostle saw no need to be ashamed amid the splendours and culture of that proud city. Christ and His Cross had been his all. sufficient theme. He believed in social reform, in a, higher standard of education for all people, in improv- ing the stamina of the race, and in philosophy, but he did not believe that the sin inherent in man was to be cleansed and eradicted by looking at picture galleries, by Hiring in flower gardens, or by mastering problems in Euclid. These things might brighten the intellect and sharpen the mind. but they wen only the shell of the soul. They could never go so deep into the human nature as to save the soul. With these declarations he brought to a solemn and peaceful close his ministry at that church. They had added to the church since the autumn of 1893 enough members to have made the membership fully three times as large, if there had been no deaths and removals. Very few churches could say that, let alone a church situated as theirs had been. They had been engaged in all kinds of Christian 'work, and they had co-operated in the work of the town. His aim had been to make the church, although it was a small one, truly democratic, In the accomplishment of that they had had to face enormous difficulties, and all he would say about those difficulties was that they ought never in the sight of God to have existed, and whilst be had spent the happiest years of his life at Hill Cliffe, he had also experienced his most trying moments there. At the conclusion of the service a public meeticg was held, Mr. E. Englandipresiding. • The Chairman said Mr. Hughes had been a very faith- fnl pastor of the flock, and he INA preached and ex- pounded the Word of God to the best of his ability. without censidering the fear and favour of man, and during his pastorate the cornmnnion had been thrown open to otherchurches who believed in the Lord Jesns Christ. The pew system had also been abolished. Mr. Hughes was not leaving the neighbourhood at present, but he would be missed. He would give his services to the cause of God, although he would not be identified with any particular church. Still, they felt the parting very strongly. (Hear, hear.) Mr. William Arnold, Mr. Shnnton (formerly superintendent of the Hill Cliffe School), and Mr. Reeves having spoken, Mr. Joseph Moss, jun. (treasurer of the church), in resentful, Itr. Hughes on behalf of the congregation with a handsome drawingroom timepiece and orna- ments to match together with a case of cutlery said that when Mr. Hughes came to the church it was in a low state, but there was soon a wonderful revival, and he believed that if he had had fair play the church would have been in a different state to what it was at the present moment. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. .1. S. Hughes, in reply, said it was no easy matter to relinguish his hold of the church, but owing to the precarious state of his health, as well as family arrangements, it had been a necessity for him to have a real change for a while, and to have freedom from Ihe care of a church. It was not an easy matter for him or for his wife to break the connecting link with that church, with which he had been personally in touch for about 30 years. A great uncle of his from the same village from which he (Mr. Hughes) came used to minister at that church about 70 years ago, and in that way and through his wife's family the connection with the church went back 100 years. He hoped in some measure to be able to keep in touch with the church. The Rev. J. F. Matthews said he was glad to be able to join in that double function—to congratulate Mr. Arnold on accepting the pastorate at Golborne, and to ta3 e part in the farewell to Mr. Hughes. He would tell the congregation frankly that he was glad Mr. Hughes was going, for his health's sake. The strain upon him and been great, but it had now been removed. At the same time let him say that he was heartily sorry Mr. Hughes was going. for in losing his friend he would lose the official headrof Hill Mille Baptist Church. He joined with them in express- ing to Mr. Hughes, on behalf of his own church and himself, their most cordial goodwill in his retirement. He hoped be would be spared for many years. If he should ever take another pastorate, the restored health land added experience be gained at Hill Cliffe would stand him in good stead. (Hear. bear.) Mr. John King, a member of the congreeation, on behalf of the congregation,presented Mrs. Hughes with silver rose bowl, and Mr. William Arnold, who has been connectd with the church for a long time as a Sundaar school teacher, &amp;c., and has accepted a minis- terial appointment in Golborne, was presented with a gold watch (anonymously subscribed) 'in recognition of his services to the church. Suitable replies were made in each case, and the proceedings were brought to a close with a united Communion service, in which the members of the Providence and the Wesleyan Churches !took part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Association Football.</head>
        <p>Association Football.</p>
        <p>There waq great excitement in Warrington school circles on Saturday when teams representing the War- rington and District Elementary Schools Association Football League. and Liverpool's similar nrzalizatio.p, met on Messrs. Rylands' ground. The local eleven had been selected only after several trial matches, and as they undoubtedly were the best team which could have been , placed on the field, a win was ,confidently expected. This, however, was not to be, and the home- sters had to bow before the greater weight and skill of the Liverpudlians, whose bulky representatives' liter- ally brushed Wile all opposition, and registered five goals to nil. There was a crowd of over 4.000 present, end generally the greatest satisfaction was felt that though beaten, Warrington gave a most plucky display.</p>
        <p>Crosfield:s Fire Brigade.</p>
        <p>The currant number of" Fire and -*ater " contains an illustrated article descriptive of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons' fire station and brigade.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD's</p>
        <p>"ROYAL GINGER"</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>THE NEW TEA TABLE DELICACY.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS GIRL'S BONFIRE.</p>
        <p>Annie Bridge, aged seventeen, of Union-street. was admitted to the Providence Hospital, S. Helens, suffer- ing from extensive burns. She was playing in the streets with some other girls, when they found a match, and proceeded to make a bonfire of paper, itc. During the process the girl's clothing became ignited, and she ran home screaming. ST. HELENS GLASSWORKS ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Charles Coleman, aged thirty-four, Canal-street, was early on Tuesday, injured at the Ravenhead Glassworks St. Helens. Coleman was employed in the casting hall, and by some means or other fell under the wheels by which the " pot " which contains the molten glass is carried. He was run over and sustained a compound fracture of the left leg, as well as some barns. Cole- man was conveyed to the St. Helens Hospital.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND THE FEEDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Education Committee have decided, in connection with the new Act, that an investigation be made of cases of under-feeding and igaproper feeding of children in all the elementary scho6ls of the town, and for that purpose the 'secretary for elementary education be authorised to engage temporary assistance and, further, that it be left to the discretion of the medical officer and the secretary, after consideration of the result of such investigation, to decide as to which cases are to be examined and reported on. The secre- tary' is also instructed to make inquiries as to the voluntary help, personal and financial, that can be secured to carryout the provisions of the Act. DEATH OF MR. GEORGE BARTON.</p>
        <p>News of the dbath of Mr. George Barton was received with very great regret in St. Helena on Monday. Mr. Barton was the oldest son of the late Mr. Geore Barton, the famous Lancashire tenor. He was forty-six years of age, and was very well known as a tenor vocalist, who had sung the leading part in %lite a number of operas in connection with the old St. Helens Operatic and other societies. For many years he sang in Lowe House Catholic Choir, and in his early days was connected with the Parish Church Choir, when his father and other well-known singers were members. For twenty-five years Air. Barton was in the office at Sutton Glass works, but for eleven years he has been proprietor of the Lingholme Hotel. For two years past he had been in failing health, and a severe attack of pleurisy a year ago left him in a serious state. Mr. Barton was a member of the Prescot Lodge of Free- masons. He leaves a widow and four children. ST. HELENS CIVILIAN RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The new range of the St. Helens Civilian Rifle Club at Denton's Green was opened on Saturday afternoon by Mr. C. H. Jolliffe, chairman of the club, in the presence of a large gathefing of members. The club has been in process of formation for several months plst, and starts out with a range equipped in the most up.-to-date manner in Littler's Field. There is one firing line with three sets of twelve targets, at 25, 50, and 100 yards from the firing point.—Mr. J. S. M'Lean, the secretary, made a statement to the effect that there were already 300 members, although a shot had not been fired. Miniature rifles of the latest pattern, approved by the War Office, had been purchased. They intended shortly to construct moving targets, and also have a pavilion for indoor practice. Mr. Jolliffe said he was glad to welcome so inanY main hers to the range. They would agree that it was beauti- fully fitted up, and that it promised to be very bene- ficial to the men of the town. The young men could come there and learn to shoot, and it would be interest- ing practice for those who had had some experience The club was not intended in any way to clash with the local Volunteer corps; in fact, he thought the Volun- teer battalions would be likely to benefit, for if a man after practising there found that he could shoot well, he would be likely to join the Volunteers so that he might have a chance- of competing in the great competitions at Risley. The men of the district had come forward in the most encouraging manner, and thel club started on its career with the very best prospects. He felt that if the members kept up their interest the club was sure to do well.—Captain F. J. West, of the Volunteers, who is one of the most successful Lanca- shire riflemen, gave a brief address on the value of such a ranee for teaching men to shoot and to know how to handle a rifle properly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>EASTER EISTEBDFODAU. – Warrington Successes.</head>
        <p>EASTER EISTEBDFODAU. - Warrington Successes.</p>
        <p>Warrington choirs on Monday figured with success at the annual Easter Crown Eisteddfod at Chester. The, principal item on the programme was a choral competi- tion for choirs of male voices, not under 40 in number, the test piece being Maldwyn Price's "Crossing the plain." The first prize was £l5 and a silver crown (presented by Mrs. Mond) for the conductor. The sEcond prize was Z. Out of seven entries five choirs competed—the Crewe Apollo Choir (conductor, Mr. J. Stubbs), Warrington Male Voice Choir (Mr. W.Nesbitt), Hoylake and West Kirby (Mr. Charles Hughes), Broughton and- District (3r.Evan Evans), and Warring- ton Apollo (Hr. Harry Berrey). The adjudi- cator, Mr. E. Maengwyn Davies, • R.A.M., fixed the maximum number of marks at 100. The Warrington Male Voice Choir gained 85 marks distributed thus :—Quality and blend of voices, 22; time and accuracy of reading, 9 : articulation, emphasis, and ,attack, 13; intonation, ; expression and phrasing, 13; pitch, four • conception of piece and general effect, 17. Hoylake and West Kirby gained 61, Crewe 69, and Broughton and district 79 marks respec- tively. In regard to the Warrington Apollo choir, the adjudicator said the prayer of the piece was truly ren- dered by this choir. It was given with true emotion ; not in machine-like fashion. It was a most excellent rendering showing good emphasis, articulation, and expression. The marks gained were :—Quality and blend of voices 23; time and accuracy of reading nine; articulation, emphasis and attack 14; intonation nine; expression and phrasing 14 ; pitch four; conception of piece and general effect 19 ; total 92. (Applause). The Warrington Apollo received the first prize and the crown, and the second prize went to the Warrington Male Voice Choir. There were four entries for the juvenile choir com- petition, but only two appeared on the platform, viz: Talke Juvenile Choir (conductor Mr. Fred J. Bossons), and the Warrington Harmonic Juvenile Choir (Mr. Thomas Brough). The first prize was £6 and a silver- mounted baton for the conductor' and the second prize £2. In awarding the first prize toTalke the adjudicator said the conductors were men of fairly good brains, or else they could not have infused such an idea of expres- sion into the children. The altos in both choirs were a ltttle bit weak in comparison with the sopranos. There was an attempt to give a variety of reading on the part of the second choir whmch,it it had been carried out, would have placed the choir higher in the competition. . • _</p>
        <p>_ . Following their success at Chester, the Warrington Apollo Choir proceeded to Rhyl Eisteddfod, where they were successful in carrying off the flrst'prize of £2O. The test piece was" Spartan heroes" (Dan Prothero), and the adjudicator Mr. Peter Edwards. The other competing choirs were the Warrington Male Choir' Old Colwyn Male Voice Choir, and Crewe Apollo Male Voice Choir.</p>
        <p>At the Rhyl Eisteddfod, Miss Lily Fairhurst,Warring- ton was the winner of the contralto solo competition. At the Liverpool musical festival for children. Violet Brown and Edward Greaves, of Warrington, won the grst prize in the duet competition. The test piece yvas "Oh ! come again sweet month of May."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>BISHOP ON THE SUNDAY NEWSPAPER.</head>
        <p>A BISHOP ON THE SUNDAY NEWSPAPER.</p>
        <p>Severe Criticism.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Salford (Dr. Casartelli), preaching in St. John's Cathedral, Salford, on Sunday, said he did not wish to condemn Sunday newspapers simply because they were Sunday newspapers, but on account of the articles and news that were published in such papers. It was a shocking thing to see the columns .of these journals filled with garbage and the particulars of every kind of trial from the various courts. The) particulars of divorce courts, which respectable papers would not publish were published in all their fulness and foul details in these so-called Sunday papers,. as well as articles of a most irreligious and anti-Christian char- acter, and which, read by young people, especially those not well educated, were calculated to be of grievous harm. These papers, he understbod, were read very largely on account of the sporting news they con- tained, and if the demand for sporting news was such that the public could not wait until Monday for it, he asked—Could not newspaper proprietors of theimore respectable kind produce a respectable Sunday paper or papers? It should be possible, with the resources of a rich country like this, with a flourishing journalism, to supply young people with harmless and healthy Sunday literature. Any journalistic agency which had the courage or patriotism to take up a work like this would merit very well of the country and prove of great service to the rising generation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>THE LATE COUNCILLOR BROUGH. —A MEMORIAM TABLET. —We understand that the members of the Wesleyan Bible Class attached to the Padete Wesleyan Church are having a marble Tablet placed in the chapel to the memory of the late Mr. Brough who was the leader of the class. The Tablet bears a suitable inscrip- tion. It is intended to have a formal unveiling of the Tablet during the course of the present month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman. Reader, of this column-are invited to assist in</p>
        <p>making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to, make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded- in confidence to the Editor of the column M.S.S. mast be addressed to John Work man " Examiner " 05ce, Warrington and . should be • sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>saop !</p>
        <p>Congratulations to the Shop Assistants on -the success of their last conference, and the promising signs of virility in their recent prolonged agitation against "radius agreements," "living in,' and other evils which have formed part of the shop assistant's lot so long. There appear to be healthy signs that Shop assistants and other semi-professional workers are rapidly, realising their kinship with the manual worker; and the narrow respectability, snobbery, and aloofness which have stood between them and the light are now disappearing, giving place to a saner estimate of modern industrial conditions. When all is said and done, the clerk, warehouseman, manager, buyer and shop assistant are found to be in the same relation to industry as the manual worker. They all have their labour, their experience, their knowledge, to give in return for a livelihood, in the same manner as the artisan. Their weapons of combat against aggregations of capital (and con sequent dividend hunting) are the same as used by manual workers viz., combination, organisation, agitation and collective bargaining. The 380 Branches and the 20,000 members of the Union have done great things, but what a myriad of assistants still remain outside its beneficent control! Come boys -and girls—look your industrial position in the face, remember your power and influence in combined effort—realise the moderate nature of even your most pressing demands—and then march forward boldly assured of victory. An extension of the Truck Acts to kill "living in/'—a sixty hours week to ensure you reasonable leisure, —abolition of radius agreements to return your lost liberty —thsse reforms you need now. Public sympathy and opinion are tending your way, but the public helps those who help themselves—therefore join the Union and give yourselves a chance</p>
        <p>LAZARUS.</p>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS INITIALS-THE " 1.L.P."</p>
        <p>It will have been seen from previous articles that the Warrington Trades Council and Labour Repre- sentation Committee are really federations, eonsisting of branches of trade unions. The Independent Labour Party known as the I.L.P. is a branch of the National Party which has similar branches in hundreds of towns and villages in Great Britian. The I.L.P. was founded in Bradford in 1893, by a number of trade unionists and other reformers who had become dissatisfied with the Liberal and Conservative parties. The.Warrington branch was formed some two years later, its first meeting being held in a room near the " Observer " Office. Councillor Stott and Mr. J. Pod- more, jun., are two of the oldest members, both being present at the first meeting. The I.L.P. is open for membership to both male and, female who agree with its objects and are not 'connected with either Liberal or Conservative parties. Although no question is ' asked as to whether a candidate for membership is a member of a trade union, the bulk of the male mem- bers are trade unionists. The I.L.P. is a Socialist organisation, and by far the largest in Great Britian. The branches carry on a vigorous propoganda both summer and winter by holding indoor and open air Raeetings, and by the sale and distribution of Socialist literature. The The Labour Leader "is the official organ of the party. At the present time its member- ship is increasing at a remarkable rate, two of its latest recruits being the Hon. Charles Lister, and the Rev. R. J. Campbell. At least twenty of its mem- bers are members of the House of Commons, and its representatives on local governing bodies can be counted by the hundred. The Warrington branch has been responsible for practically the whole of the work done in the borough on behalf of independent labour representation, Councillors Stott and Ball, the late Mr. Thomas Wright who repre- sented Bevvsey Ward for tw.) years, and Mrs. Wilkinson, a member of the new Board of Guardians, all being members of the I.L P. The I.L P. has met with stern opprisition from the members of the two great political parties, but has ben able to withstanl</p>
        <p>it, and has now come to be recognised as a per- manent factor in British p Aides.</p>
        <p>THE SCOUT:</p>
        <p>TaE EVERLASTIN;3- RIILWAYS.!</p>
        <p>In former issues I have had something to say of the effect of Railway, Rates upon agriculture, and of the importance of agriculture to the country. Possibly some of the patient souls who are good' enough to read my remarks will wonder at my relegation. of Industry (capital I please), to a secondary position. I plead guilty, but if I err at all I err in good company, for the Railway Companies themselves were sl‘ow to. recognise the value of the carriage of industrial imple- ments and products. It is recorded that when'one of our Railway Companies was first asked to. carry con! one of the directors exclaimed in indignation " What Carry coal! Why they'll ask us to carry dung next.' And when the London and North-Western Raikriiy, as it is now styled, first carried this useful commodity, it was usual to cover the coal trucks so that their con- tents might not be suspected. Nowadays the attitude of the companies has changed; and they look upon coal and many other dirty but useful articles as legitimate sources of revenue; and that they charge for that and other goods at a much higher rate than do the foreign railways for similar service is proved by the subjoined' facts. and' figures. Finished iron and steel is sent from the works of Couillet in Belgium to the port of Antwerp —a dis- tance of 70 miles —for 38. per ton,whilst iron and steel send from Staffordshire to Gloucester, 56 miles, is charged by English, railways Gs. Si. per ton. Nails; which are made in Belgium can be sent to Wolver- hampton via Harwich at a total transit charge of 16s. 4d. per ton whilst English nails made in Wolverhampton cost £1 Is. 4d. per ton to send to Harwich!' Troa wire from Antwerp to Birmingham costs 168. 81 per ton and the same article is charged at. the rate of 1i Bs. 4d. per ton from Birmingham to London! Iron girders from Antwer!Ato Binning- ham via Grimsby are 16s. Bd. per on whilst from Birmingham to Grimsby the charge is £l. Flax is sent from Hull to Belfast at 18s. 4d. per ton, this of curse being foreign flax. English flax from Selby; 30 miles nearer to Belfast, is charged Os. Bd. per ton! Many more instances of a similar character can be given to prove the case against English railways. S-) far I have endeavoured to prove.thlt the railway rates af our English Companies •</p>
        <p>ARE KILI,I.NG AGRICULTURE ARE CRIPPLYNG INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>Next week I hope to make some comparisons in passenger fares and to conclude the series of articles by indicating the remedies.</p>
        <p>STANLEY LORD.</p>
        <p>THE TARIFF QUESTION AND SOCIAL EVILS. Dear John,—l am writing these few lines to direct your attention to some interesting, mints that have recently puzzled me above a little. You will remem- ber, John, that the General Election of 1906 was mainly fought on the issue Free Trade versus Tariff Reform —the first-named policy being, as a rule, defended by the Liberal nominees, whilst the latter principle was supported (more or less) by many Con- servative candidates.</p>
        <p>The extensive development of our foreign trade throughout the course of the last half-century and the enormous increase of our national wealth during that period were repeatedly dlaimed by Free Trade advo- cates in the electioneering campaign as a full iustifi- cation for a continuation of our present free import system. On the other hand, the Tariff Reformers declared that on account of the Protective tax placed on goods imported into altnqst every civilized nation our manufacturers were being gradually driven out of many of their best markets abroad, whilst the foreigners had also become serious competitors in our home markets as our free import system enabled them to "dump" their products into the United Kingdom on more advantageous terms than could be obtained by our manufacturers elsewhere. Let us therefore, sug- gested these Tariff Ref lrmers, impose a small protec- tive tax on foreign goods bronht into this country, granting special preferential terms to imported Colonial products. So shell we partially shield our national industries against unfair foreign competition in the English markets and consequently provide more work for our own people. We shall also bring the Mother Country into "closer touch" with the scattered Colonies and Dependencies and thus utilise the common interests (as trading communities) as a means of bindinethem together into one huge, consolidated, Imperial federation. _</p>
        <p>A.ll this fine talk, John, naturally bewilders an</p>
        <p>! tit:jet:mad working man and causes me to "think much." For instanee, despite the enormous increase of our national wealth, of which our Free Trade friends so proudly boast, I notice that a large propor- tion of the workers, (who are, after all, the wealth producers)are comparatively poor whilst many of them i are actually below-the poverty line.Then,in my humble ; opinion,. John, it is fallacious to congratulate ourselves ;on the development of our foreign trade, whilst, at the sametime,in. the. United Kingdom there is a large , army of: unemployed men and women, and many , thousands of our loyal, industrious fellow-citizens are herded together. in jerry-built hovels amidst over- crowded and insanitary surroundings. In addition to Ithese deplorable social evils a large percentage of the children attending our public elementary schools are both ill-clothed and under-fed and our industrial veterans after years of badly paid toil are virtually thrown on the scrap-heap and stigmatised as paupers should they accept, .as parish relief, a miserable weekly pittance of five or six shillings. The alternative claims, of the tiriff-Rehrmers also appear to me a little misleading: It has been estimated that the Colonies annually purchase. 1'30,000,000 of foreign manufactures. With the adoption of a Colonial preference, M. Chamberlain believed that most of this trade would be transferred to us. Since this plan was first put forward in 1903' our total export trade has.increased far more than £30,000,000, but it has not appreciably minimised our social evils for wages are still low, and povertyand destitution )et rampant in the land, in addition to the unsolved unemployed problem. Then there is the questiou of Foreign Coinpetition about which T confess to be somewhat perplexed for I discover on enquiry that £2,000,000,000 of English capital is invested in foreign countries. When the capitalists have more money than they can spend, they invest their surplus in those particular enterprises (either home - or foreign) from which they can obtain the most profit, and so we find that whilst ouramemployed are starving and thousands of our people lack decent clothing. and live in badly furnished houses our rich men are actually investing their capital abroad to find work for the foreigners The home workers, by consenting (as•they do at present) to toil hard for ridiculously low wages, allow the capitalists to make huge profits which are partially-invested' in foreign industries, the products of whichcompete with oar own manufac- tured goods in the world's markets. It appears to me, John, that neither Free Trade nor Tariff Reform will really solve any of our burning social problems and I suggest that the dhstruction of the poor is their poverty, and that the want of a larger purchasing power on the part of the great mass of the people is the fundamental'cause of most of the deplorable social evils from which,society is suffering at present. In concluaion, Alm, let us, as practical social reformers, give a loyal, hearty and active response to the stirring • appeal of Edward Carpenter who in " England's Ideal " writes :</p>
        <p>"-Re firm, helpful towards each other, forgetful of differences, scrupulously honest in yourselves and charitable even to your enemies, but determined that nothing shall move you from the purpose you have set before you—the righteous distribution in Society of the fruits of your own and others men's labour, the return to Honesty as the sole possible basis of national tile and national safety, and the redemption of atgrand from the curse which rests upon her."—Yours etc., DE NIOS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>HISPITAL SUNDAY.</head>
