      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSMEN f COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMEN f COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley Congratulated on his Return</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington.Union Assessment Com- mittee was held at the Workhouse yesterday under the presidency of Mr. A. J. Jolley.. There were also present: —Messrs. G. Mather,A. Broolts,W. Hickman, J. Billinge, J. C. Sutton (clerk); R. R. Meacock (valuer), W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warrington), .1. Smith (assistant overseer for Great Sankey). and S. Walker (assistant overseer for Hoiighton).—The Valuer reported that lists of properties requiring attention had been handed to him by the assistant ovir.seers and were being dealt with.—Mr. J. Atherton appealed against a shociting rights assessment in respect of Cuerdley Marsh, which was £25 gross and £25 ratable. The matter I was referred to the valuer.</p>
        <p>This being the last meeting of the •cornmittee as at present constituted, Mr. Billinge moved a vote of thanks to Mr., Jolley for his services in the chair. He heartily congratulated him upon his return as a Guardian and hoped he would long be spared to attend to the duties. Mr. MA.THER seconded and said he was glad Mr. Jolley had been returned, not on persenal grounds alone, but from the Union standpoint. It would have been a distinct loss to the Union and especially to the Corn.. mittee if Mr. Jolley had been defeated. Mr. BROOKS supported and agreed that if Mr. Jolley bad not been returned it would have been a distinct loss to the Board—in fact the Board would not have been the old Board without Mr. Jolley. Mr. HICKMAN said he very sincerely supported the vote. He knew Mr. Jolley had attended very regularly to his duties and hid rendered very faithful servics. They might not always agree, but although they might differ, in policy, he had never had any personal feeling against Mr. Jolley, whatever Mr. Jolley might feel towards him. At elec- tion times a good deal was said that perhaps ought not to be said, and which was not altogether correct, and wrong constructions were placed upon statements. Re had, however, always hail a high appreciation of Mr. Jolley as a man, but he hud differed with him many times with regard to his policy. The electors had sent him back, and he would no doubt dos good work in the future as he hod done in the past. He (Mr. Hickman) was pleased to see him in the chair, and hoped he would long be spared to devote his valuable services to the good of the ratepayers. The vote was unanimously passed. Mr. JOLLEY said he ras extremely obliged for that renewed expression Of confidence and good will and he could only say his efforts to discharge the duties of the committee had always recceived very valuable and cordial support from his colleagues. The questions with regard to which considerable conflict of opinion had arisen in connection with the work of the Board of Guardians had not affected their work on the Assess- ment Committ3e to any appreciable extent. Their work was largely of a judicial and often of a confidential character and it had been a pleasure to feel that any efforts he hAd put forth to secure equitable treatment of all appellants and equitable assessments, not merely as between Individual ratepayers but as between parish and parish, had received the most loyal support from the committee. He concluded by moving a vote of thanks to Mr. , Billinge for his servines in the vice-chair. I Mr. BROOKS seconded, Mr, HlcicAT AN supported az4 the motion was carried,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>(ColVright.) MAUD VALPY'S FATE.</p>
        <p>nvose or nts- ramny witn Ins society. But, in secret, Maud swore to her lover that she was his and his alone; that, though she might never become his wife, she would still be his faithful fiancée; that nothing should ever come between them. And so, for the time, they parted. Three months later Ilario Francis, who had been in Paris for some time, returned to London, and on the first night he arrived sat down and wrote the following note to Maud Valpy—a note that, as was afterwards noted and commented on at his trial, had no form of endearment or even of ordinary intimate address whatever. It ran: "I have returned to London from Paris, at which city I learnt some strange news, not only from an English friend but also from an English newspaper. I heard and read l that you are to be married—you who swore that if yoq could not be my wife you would never be the wife of any other man, and that, short of myself, Death would be the only mate you would ever know. If this is true I call upon you now to keep at least one other solemn vow you made me when we parted. I call upon you to grant me the one promised and final interview at any time and place which I might suggest. The time and place that I should there- fore suggest, if all could be as I desire, would be my own house; but. I will not subject you to any gossip which such a visit might give rise to. There- fore I ask you to meet me, openly, publicly—since I will do nothing in secret—at the upper end of Sloane-street to-morrow, or the next day, or the next day after that, at any hour you choose to select. I will be there alone, in my phaeton, and we will drive for an hour or so into the suburbs. It will be our last farewell. After that I shall trouble you no more. I call upon you to redeem this vow at least. I expect you." •••</p>
        <p>BY JOHN BLOUNDELLE-BURTON Author of "Servants of Sin," "The Scourge of God," "In the Day of Adversity," "The Clash of Arms," "Across the Salt Seas," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"This," said the Lord Chief Baron in his summing up to the jury, "is what you alone have to consider, since the motive for such a crime has been suggested by the counsel for the Crown, in the fact that the deceased lady had undoubtedly refused to fulfil her engagement with the prisoner, or, in common parlance, had jilted him. You have to consider how, if the poison were administered to that unhappy woman by the prisoner at the bar, it could have been so administered ; whether the opportunity arose in the circumstances stated for him to administer it; and, if so, how the deceased could haare suffered death at the prisoner's hands by partaking of a dish of fruit which he shared with her, and from which it is undoubtedly evident that •he himself experienced no ill-effects whatever. I confess," his lordship continued, while all in the dimly- lighted court hung eagerly upon his words, "I confess that, to my mind, nothing has been presented to you which leans heavily, or as positive proof, against the prisoner: while I feel myself right3ously called upon to state that, if anything, the evidence on the part of the Crown has told more in his favour than against him."</p>
        <p>After which remarks and a considerable number of other observations, his lordiThip dismissed the jury to their duty of deciding whether the Count Ilario Francia had administered poison to Maud Valpy or not. And perhaps it does not need to be said that, after the above strong expressions on the judge's part as to what his opinions were, the jury almost immediately returned a verdict of "Not guilty," and the Count Ilario Fra,ncia became once more a free man.</p>
        <p>This case had been one which, for more than three months, had caused not only the whole of London, but also the whole of England, as well as the inhabitants of many foreign cities abroad, much excitement; and, indeed, it was the most notorious cause celebre of the year in which it occurred—which year need not here be specified. Briefly, the following is the story: The Count Francia was a young Italian noble- man of high position jind illustrious family connec- tions in Venice, who, for some time, had bee. domiciled in London owing to the fact that he was well known to be a member of the "Young Italy" party, which was a modern version of the old Carbonari secret societies, having for its head the renowned Mazzini. Unlike, however, so many of his countrymen, who, from the early part of the present century until the period when Italy became united, had made England, and especially London, their home, this young man appeared to be extremely well to do, and, instead of sheltering in a garret or a poor lodging-house, or a cheap boarding-house in one of the commoner suburbs of London, had been able to live well and handsomely. He rented a small but expensive house in the neighbourhood of Sloane-street; he kept a small staff of servants, and he was the possessor of some good horses as well as one or two well-appointed carriages, on, or in, which he was daily to be observed in the most fashionable resorts. He had also come to London provided with introductions to a number of well-known families of good position,. so that, ere long, he was not only made welcomPat their houses but was also a member of least two good clubs, one being the Palladium, which was political, and the other "Hooper's," at which gambling went on to a very considerable extent.</p>
        <p>In the fashionable circles to which he was almost at once admitted Ilario Francia met, among other beautiful girls, Maud Valpy, the young lady for whose death by poison he stooe later that trial from which he emerged a free and acquitted man. He met her and, as has ever been the custom with most Italians when thrown into the society of any girl who is particularly dis- tinguished by beauty from her compeers, he fell in love with her, and not only fell in love with her, but instantly proceeded to make hot love to her vith the intention of winning her affections. • He succeeded in doing so as, froin the first, it was an absolute ,certainty he would succeed. With his soft dark eyes, his soft voice, his passionate expressions, so different from the cold and dignified tones of admiration—of suggested admiration— which her English admirers were in the habit of expressing stiffly .to Maud Valpy in a half-hearted, hesitating manner; with all the glamour about him of•a handsome young Italian bred from his boy- hood in all the arts of perfervid love-making, it was not possible that he should fail. Maud Valpy was soon madly in love with the Count Ilario Francis and, honest English girl as she was, did not hesitate to acknowledge her love. She vowed in answer to his latest pleading—the last request that the accepted lover has to make ! that she would never love any other man but him, and that she would never be any other man's wife than his.</p>
        <p>From that moment, however, difficulties began. Maud Valpy's father was a wealthy man, an extremely wealthy man, and she was his only child and the heiress to a large fortune, and it did not fall in at all with .Mr. Valpy's views• for the future disposal of his wealth that it "should ever find its way into the hands of an Italian, however good the position of that Italian might be. For this he was not to be blamed when one remembers that, at the period of this narrative if at no other, the Italian race did not stand favourably in the eyes of the English, and especially in the eyes of the upper and wealthy classes in England. Italy itself was torn. by;political dissensions as well as divided into a dozen petty states, which might some day become blended into a united country or might sink into an even lower. slough of despond than they were at present in; while the habits and customs (as well as the morals) of the Italians were regarded as something that, in our land at least, did not qualify them for admission into the domestic circle.</p>
        <p>The result of all this was that Mr. Valpy flatly refused to hear of his daughter becoming the wife of the Count Frannie, and suggested that, for the future. thill eentleman should not 11^nrn0 his</p>
        <p>The main facts and evidence of that trial before the Lord Chief Baron, of which the result has already been given, shall now themselves be briefly offered to the reader. _</p>
        <p>The first witness called was the bereaved Mr. Valpy, who stated in broken accents all. that has been told about his refusal to allow his daughter to marry the prisoner now standing in the dock accused of having poisoned that daughter. But, to this evidence, he added the fact that, on August Ist, his daughter. who was soon to be married to an English gentleman of high position, had been absent from luncheon and had returned home too late for dinner'; that she had gone to her room after stating that she felt extremely unwell but refusing absolutely to say where she had spent the day: and Mat early me next morning he had been arousea bficries and calls in the house; by his daughter's maid shrieking that her. mistress was dying and had been poisoned, while also stating in a loud yoke that the "Italian" had poisoned her.</p>
        <p>The second witness Was the maid herself. She stated that her mistress, who was always very con- fidential with her, had told her that she had been forced to grant an interview to the Count Francis, whom she had once loved dearly, but whom she had ceased to love on learning that he was not only a gambler, who lost heavily almost every night at his club and had been forced to leave England for a time in consequence of his inability to pay his debts at cards, but was, also, intimate with ceveral women in London whose reputation was rtnything but good. Miss Valpy had likewise told her, the woman added, that she would never have been unfaithful to the Count except for these reasons, and also that she had come to feel perfectly sure that it was her money and not herself which he desired. As regards the night of Miss Valpy's death, the maid-servant continued, her mistress had said, in between her terrible spasms of agony, that she believed she had been poisoned; but, on being questioned, had also stated that it was impossible the Count could have so poisoned her. They had partaken, in an inn-garden at Kingston, of three peaches which the Count had bought in the Market-place—he being struck by their extreme fineness and beauty—but of nothing else; while the Count himself had eaten as much as she °Peach peach, he having divided the fruit equally between them. Miss Valpy also added that she had drunk nothing whatever, though the Count had partaken of a pint of claret. She (the maid) had cried out that the Italian had poisoned Miss Valpy because she was excited.</p>
        <p>The third witness was Sir Ezra Brown, M.D., a noted analyst, as well as an authority on toxicology. His evidence as to what Miss Valpy had died of was clear and distinct, yet it proved nothing, or very little, against the prisoner. The pulp of the peaches found in her body, Sir Ezra said, was undoubtedly impregnated with a subtle poison which had caused her death. Asked as to what that poison was, he said that he was scarcely prepared to say, beyond the fact that it was doubtless a vegetable poison of extreme power. "Would it be," counsel for the Crown asked, "a tasteless or a colourless poison, do you think, of, say, the nature of Aqua Tqfana ? " "I cannot say," Sir Ezra replied. "Aqua Tojana is an Italian poison of which I have no knowledge." "The court will understand the suggestion of the learned counsel," exclaimed the barrister who was defending Francis, "but it will also remember that if the poison was in the peaches from eating which Miss Valpy died, it is strange the prisoner did not die too." - _ _</p>
        <p>But now, while many in that court thought that it was almost time the Lord Chief Baron should `stop the case on the ground that nothing whatever had been proved which was sufficient ,to condemn Francia, his counsel commenced to call witnesses on his behalf. These witnesses, he said, addressing the judge, were perhaps almost unnecessary, seeing that the prosecution had entirely broken down; but, so that his much-injured and abused client should be able to leave the court without a stain, or the faintest breath of suspicion upon the honourable and illustrious name which he bore, they should, nevertheless, be called. Whereupon, for his first witness, he put into the box the woman from whom the Count had purchased the peaches. Those peaches, she said, were rathored that morning from her son's orchard at Molesey. She had a hundred for sale on her stall, all of which she had sold before night, yet she had not heard of anyone being taken ill from eating them except Miss Valpy. Yes! She distinctly remembered the prisoner buying them. He descended from his gig—as she called it—selected three of the finest in the basket, gave her eighteenpence for them, and then got into the "gig' again, and drove into the inn-yard. That was all she knew, except that she saw the prisoner and lady dove out again an hour or so afterwards. The lady looked the same as she had done before, but she thought the foreign gentleman looked very pale. The next and last witness was the waiter who had served the Count with a half-bottle of St. Zulien in the garden of the inn. The gentleman put op his phaeton, this witness said, and then walked with the lady to the garden overlooking the river and sat down in an arbour. He, the waiter,' followed them, to take any orders they might give. The prisoner asked the lady if she would ctrink anything, to which she replied: "No, she was not thirsty.' Then the prisoner told him to bring the St. Julien and also,, a large tumbler. "You English," he said with' a smile to the witness, "do not know how to drink claret or any light wines. 'Your silly little green and red glasses are no use whatever to a thirsty man." Continuing,, the witness said, in answer to the counsel's' questions, that, on his return with the big glass and the half-bottle of wine, the Count bade him 'pour the whole of the latter into the former, and then, taking the tumbler, emptied it at one draught. He was astonished, as he had never seen claret drunk like that before. _</p>
        <p>_ Still continuing, the waiter said that, on his return with the wine and the glass, the prisoner had got three fine peaches before him on the paper bag in which he brought them, and he asked the Count if he should get him a fruit-knife to out or peel them. "No," was the reply, "we Italians generally carry our own fruit-knives; our own particular fruit-knives, as we eat so much fruit." Then he put his hand in his waistcoat pocket and drew • out a beautiful little gold fruit-knife, having one fairly long gold blade with which he divided each of the peaches equally, giving the lady three halves and keeping three for himself. Later, as the lady and gentleman were going away and he was summoned to be paid for the wine, he noticed that the lady had eaten only, two of her halves, and that the prisoner threw the remaining one into the river, saying he could eat no more himself. '"Did you overhear any of their conversation ? " asked the counsel for the Crown, in his cross- examination. - -</p>
        <p>Yes, the waiter said, he had done so, owing to the next arbour being one in which he kept glasses and other things, and sometimes washed them there.</p>
        <p>"Tell the court something of what you heard." "I heard the lady say that the gentleman had deceived her, and that he cared more for other women than he had ever done for her. I heard her say, too, that she could never marry a gambler." "What did the prisoner reply?" "He saidsits..w.ap rich enough to...zombie as much</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Ssturday, April 6, 1907.</p>
        <p>1 • w • . • . • , • I • • • , /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>WARRPIGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the annual vestry meeting in cnnnectinn with the Warringtnn Parish rlhnrch which was held in the Nationsl Schools nn Tnnsdav evening. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presided.</p>
        <p>The acconnts were n-Agented by Mr. S. Taylor. varien, who said there was rin adverse balance Of r:,5,1 18s. fit Ttmicht he concluded that the wardens had not been as anonomieal as they might hove been, but he would point out that £56 had .bepi anon* on a new boiler and heating armarß". This W 55 /1 ‘,4bIRbIA asset, and they would yet-mire nothing' of the kind for the next 25 years. The offertory in itself v•es not sur9cient to meet, all the requirements of the church, and the wardens had never received one penny from any sale of work or bazaar since the last large bazaar which took nlaCe in the Parr Hall. It was quite time they saw that the wardens received a portion of the sums raised at sales of work and bazaars to meet the. eTneuees. The pane&amp; offertories amonnted to £285 13s. 114.: th, Day Schools offertory totalled £lO 7.1.114. thr. Choir 'Fund offertory amounted to £22 2q. 5d.: the e10,,,v Ptind offertory came to £43 25.; while the Rector's Easter offertory realized £47. The accounts were pissed on the motion of Mr. R. Isherwood. seconded by Mr. P. J. Whittle. The Rector re-apnointed Mr, .1. G. Craik as his 'Donnle'a werden. and Mr. S. Taylor drag re-elected Peonle'g warden, on the proposition of Mr. Whittle. seconded by Mr. E. Isherwood. The services of both n'ficers were mirmly enlogised, and the Rector said he was gratified that the meeting had decided that Mr. Taylor should hold office for another term. Mr. Craik and Mr. Taylor re-anoninted as their sides- men Mr. E. Atkin and Mr. James Wood respectively. The other retiring eieht sidesmen—Messrs. R.. W. Antwia. R. W. Collin. R. Davies, F. Harding. E. Isher- wood, T. Locker. W. Rogers and P. G. Tarlorl—were again proposed. alone with the following: Messrs. W. Deintith..T. E. Coates, G. Brown and S. Tilling. Mr. Lunt snegegted that sidemen who had served •continuously for three years should retire to give an opnort unity to others to share the honours. The suggestion, however, did not meet With support. The eight ele:tted were: Messrs. W. Rogers, R. W. Antwig, T. Torlrer, S. Davies, E. Isherwood, F. G. Tailor. R. W. Collin, and G. Brown. Mr. T. Everall was elected rpnregentative eidesman for St. (lenrea'f:. end Mr. C. Gandy for St. Clement's. The ley renregentativea (Messrs. .1. D. Buckton and Jameg Wood). auditors (Meagre. P. C. Price and J. T. Whittle\ and vestry clerk (Mr. . A. White) were re-elected.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brown moved a vote of thanks to the choir and the nr.,flist ( \fr. Darby) for their admirable seryiras and mild e anenial trihote to their renderine of "The Orneifixion "on no,l Friday and of the festal aervice.. invindine "The Halleluiah tThorns " on Easter Day. Mr. TT. Gandy seconded and said the choir were second to none.</p>
        <p>The vote MIR heartily accorded. Mr. R. A. White was thanked for his gratuitous 'services as vestry clerk. THE WORK OF THE YEAR. The Rector remarked, in closine the meeting, that the receints from all sources during the year amounted to £651 lie. 6d.. as against £640 14a. 10.1. in 1905. That was very satisfactory. The Mission Churches were going on most satiscsetorily. and the cnrrent expendi- ture was about £lOO a TART. With reeard to the .extension of St. George's. the cost was £1.160 of which £960 had been reised I.ayiny amnia time in which to raise the remaining £2OO. St. Clement's had a balance in bend of £65 towardg extension when that was deemed necessary. He (the Rector) knew no parish chnrch which had a superior choir, or even one equal to their own. They also owed \fr. Darby a debt of gratitude for voluntarily Undertaking to conduct the Men's Bible Class during the year. (Annlame.) There ware R 52 •communicants at the Perish Church end the Missions .on Raster Dv. He (the Rector) thanked them very much for their Raster offering. It was a real pain to him to receive it. He'had done more than he ought to have done in the pest : therefore. he very much valued their kindness, although it was distressing to him ever to receive anything. ST. ANN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annnal vestry meeting WAS held on Tnesdav even ine. the Vicar, the Rev. .Tames Davies, presiding. Mr. Winter and Mr. Corbett were re-appointcd wardens, and the followine were elected sidemen for the ensning year Messrs. G. Armstrone, H. H. Tireth.rton, :E. Rnnch. :I. E. Goodwin. .T. E. Harrison, T. Hnnebton. 4. Owen, vred Taylor and Hallett. Messrs. Ti. 14. Bretherton and G. Armstrong were anrminted the lay representatives. 1114 The acconnts of the wardens showed that there was a balance in hand of several pounds Fifthr payment of all expenses.</p>
        <p>The officers of the Church were heartily thanked for their servicea duiing the year. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. C. E. Carleton, vicar, presided over the -annustl meeting, in connection with the above church which wee held on Tuesday evening in the vestry. • Mr. Gill, in snbmitting the accounts, said the total receipts from all sources, including organ renovation, -asnounted to 2408 Is. IM.' and after setting aside a sum for the repair fund, which it was desired to augment, in view of undertaking at an early date the repair of the decayed i outside stonework, end making contributions to the en*ary and Poor Panda. there was a balance in hand of £1 7s. M. as against a debit balance of £8 the previous year,</p>
        <p>The accounts were anproved. Tha Vicar nominated 'Mr. William Gill as his warden, and Mr. Welshy was re-anpointed people's warden. Mr. Gill selected Mr. H. Fergnson as his siclesman and Mfr. 11)7e1shv chose Mr. James Hardinq. The other sidesmen anpointed were Messrs. ;fames Harding, James Bowcock, E. Dyke, William Gandv, W.</p>
      </div>
