      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>FORMER</head>
        <p>FORMER</p>
        <p>J. Forshaw, J. Gerrard, G. Gill, J. Hesketh, Joseph Flesketh, Hignett, T. Hunt, El. Milling, J. Pickles, E. Rose. W. Shirlaw, Abe Walsh, Emmott, L. R. Rowson and W. W. Leigh. • Messrs. John Hesketh and E. Rose were appointed the lay representatives. Votes of thanks to the Church officers and choir for past services concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>—•--- - The Rev. H. R. Wilde presided over the annual vestry meeting on Thursday evening. The balance sheet showed that the total income was £66 19s. 11d., and that after meeting all expenses there was a balance in hand of 22. • Messrs. W. Jackson and A. Oldfleld were elected wardens, the sidesmen appointed being Messrs. Corley, H. Dendy, J. Penne, T. Ellison, J. Warburton, C. Dendy, R. Armstrong, H. Taylor, A. Hewitt, C. H. Massey, J. Toft, and T. Taker. Messrs. Ashcroft and Horobin were re-elected auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS':CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS':CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The vestry meeting was held on Thursday evening under the presidency of the Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth).—The statemenk of accounts was pre- sented by Mr. W. A. Proud and showed an income of £l2l 16s. 24. and eKpenditure totalling £ll2 2s. 10d.' leaving a balance of £9 13s. 41.—The Vicar nominated Mr. Evan Evans as his warden and Mr. %V. A. Proud was re-elected people's warden.—The Vicar nominated as his sidesmen : Messrs. A. J. Allen' T. A. Jones, and E. Liddell ; and Mr. Proud selected Messrs. G. Rolfe, Tomkins and J. F. Morgan.—The sidesmen elected by the meeting were Messrs. C. Skinner, E. Iddon. W. P. James and S. Boyle.—Messrs. E. Evans and W. A. Proud were elected lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ST. JAMES.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES.</p>
        <p>- The vi car, the Rev. H. B. Firth, presided over St. James vestry meeting which was held in the schools on Thursday evening. At the outset the vicar thanked the church officers for their services during the year. The balance sheet showed that the income of the Church had been. general collections £lO3 65., special collection £4B 13.. 2d. The year commenced with a deficit of £4 I:3s. 4d. and closed with a debit balance of £2 10s. Bd. It being a rule at St. James Church that the office of warden should circulate the vicar nominated Mr. Foster as his warden in place of Mr. Fargher. Mr. Eckersley was re-elected people's warden. Mr. Foster nominated Mr. Fargher as his sidesman, Mr. Eckersley appointing Mr. Astley. Mr. Rathbone and Mr. E. Leigh were re-elected wardens of St. Hilda's mission. The lay delegates appointed were :—Messrs. C. S. Burgess and H. N. Houghton. Messrs. Brayshaw, Blythe, Acton, A. Grounds, J. Grounds. and H. Warburton. (St. .Tames) Mr. Furmstone (St. Hilda's) were appointed sidesmen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ST. PETERS CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PETERS CHURCH.</p>
        <p>- - In the absence of the Vicar through illness. the Rev. H. Russell presided over the vestry meeting at the above Church on Wednesday. The accounts, which were approved, showed that the year opened with a balance in hand of £5 9s. 7d. and closed with a balance in hand of £2 Os. 11d. The total receipts during the year were £lB2 12s. Gd. The Vicar nominated Mr. Bamforth as his warden in succession to the late Alderman John Roberts, whilst Councillor Steel was elected people's warden in lieu of Mr. Bamforth. Mr. Edgar was nominated as people's warden, but declined the office in favour of Mr. Steel. The sidesmen appointed were : —Mr. Stanley and Mr. J. W. Kay, wardens, sidesmen, Messrs. Jeseph Taylor, J. Cowden, R. Knight, J. J. Hands, James Taylor and E. Clayton, Vicar's sidemen, and Messrs. R. Lashford. John Taylor, T. Reddish, P. Edgar, J. reaves, R. Brownbill, and R, Higham, appointed by the vestry. Messrs. J. W. Kay and P. Engar were elected by repre- sentatives. A vote of condolence was passed with the Vicar in his illness. The services of the late Alderman Roberts, 'for 25 years Vicar's warden, and of Mr. Bamforth for 11 years people's warden, were placed on record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH..</head>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH..</p>
        <p>- - The vestry meeting %am held at noon on Tuesday, the Vicar (Rev. James Collins) presiding. The minutes of the last meeting contained a resolu- tion of protest against the Government Education Bill. The Vicar ,sliid they were glad pp see that the Bill had not pass4lllkito law and he hAped the new measure which had just been brought forward would not become an Act. In the absence of Mr. William Richardson (people's warden), Mr. F. Pearson presented the statement of accounts. Last year there was a halanc4 against the church wardens of £7 3. 4d. and this year it was £1 Gs. 4d. The collections amounted to 1'7128 17s. 6d. as against £244 7s. The accounts were passed. The Vicar nominated Mr. Henry Greenall aq his warden. Mr. William Richardson wrote intimating his decision I to relinquish the office of people's warden. He expressed the pleasure it had afforded him during his term of office to work for the church and he wonld always be Willing to assist his successor. The Vicar expressed his regret at the retirement of Mr. Richardson who has acted as warden for eleven years. and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded. to him for his services. Mr. T. J. Lee was elected neople's warden, testimony being borne to his' work for the Church extending over many years.</p>
        <p>The -folio-wing sidesmen were elected :—Messrs. A. DnWne, J. H. Clarke, .1. .Teffes. T. R. Westbrook, J. A. NfcGregor,W. Mentyre,T. Sharrocks, W. Richardson,J. Melhonrne.L. G. Davies, J. Parkes. and A. J. Willett. Messrs. J. A. McGregor and T. Sharrocks were re-appointed lay representatives. A vote of thanks was accorded to the church officers, choir, organist and choirmaster, and assistant organist for their work during the past year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>VcrALTON SUPERIOR (ST. JOHN'S).</head>
        <p>VcrALTON SUPERIOR (ST. JOHN'S).</p>
        <p>The Easter Vestry meeting was held on Monday. Mr. William Weir presided, in the absence of the Vicar. Sir Gilbert Greensll, Bart., was re-elected Vicar's warden, and he appointed Messrs. Turner and Yarwood as his sidesrnen. Mr. William Weir was re-elected people's warden, and be appointed Messrs. Cooper, Kemp and Basnett as his sidesmen.</p>
        <p>ST. MARY'S CHURCH, SANKEY. The annual vestry meeting was held in the Sunday School, Liverpool-rod, on Tuesday evening, under the presidency of the Vicar (the Rev. J. R. Jones). The accounts showed that there was a balance in hand of £l7 16s. 7d. The accounts were passed. The Chairman nominated Mr., Harry Fairclough as his ward3n. Mr. William Woods was unanimously re- elected people's warden. The following gentlemen were elected sidesmen : Messrs. Clarke, Hodetinson, Aitken, Taylor, John Ray, Binghan, Pownall, Often, James Ray, James Millington, D. Howard, T. Owen, J. Ray, jun., Hill and Kilshaw. 'Messrs. Aitken and Dixon were elected lay repre- sentatives and Mr. George Clarke was unanimously re- elected auditor.</p>
        <p>The Chairman proposed a vote of thanks to the wardens, sidesmen, auditor,. choir, Sunday School teachers, anist, Sr.c.. and this was heartily accorded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>APPETITE ALL GONE.</head>
        <p>APPETITE ALL GONE.</p>
        <p>PAINS IN STOMACH AND BACK, WIND,</p>
        <p>HEADACHE AND CONSTIPATION.</p>
        <p>A NORWICH WOMAN'S SUFFERING</p>
        <p>CURED ONLY WHEN SHE GOT MOTHER SEIGEL'S SYRUP.</p>
        <p>Again and again it has been shown that Mother Seigel's Syrup will cure indigestion and biliousness when every other known means has failed. Grateful people in all parts of the world are constantly testify- ing to its wonderful powers, and assuring us that they have been cured by it after wasting time and money on all sorts of useless preparations. Here for instance is a statement from Mrs. Elizabeth Farrow, of 3. Living- stone Place, Ber Street, Norwich, who describes how Mother Seigel's Syrup cured her, when even the doctor had failed. Writing on February 25th, 1907, she says: "I had lost all appetite and suffered very much with pains in my stomach, and wind that formed after every- thing I ate. It was little .1 did eat, for I was soon satisfied ; yet after a time I used to feta as if I had had nothing at all. I bad frequent headaches, and suffered also from constipation. Then I began to have pains in the back, and these were worse even than the stomach pains.</p>
        <p>"There was a doctor attending me, of course, but he did me no real good, and I continued to suffer for months. But at last I was advised to try Mother Seigel's Syrup, and that did what the (lector's medicine had failed to do—completely cured me. I took only half the contents of a 2s. Gd. bottle, yet that proved , sufficient to restore me to perfect health."</p>
        <p>Here is clear proof that the sooner you take Mother Seigel's Syrup, the sooner you will be cured. Get some to-day if you are ill and take it according to directions; you will not be disappointed.</p>
        <p>THE 2/6 BOTTLE GONTAINS THREE TIMES AS MUCH AS THE i/i SIZE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>as he liked, and that every gentleman in nis country gambled. He said the Government even encouraged it, and also said something about lotteries that I couldn't understand." "And about 'the other women? What did be say about them ? " _</p>
        <p>_ "He said her jealousy was absurd. He said no Italian was thought any the worse of for being an admirer of the ladies when he was single, but that they made the best husbands in the world." "Anything else? Any threats or reproaches with regard to her having thrown him over ? " asked the counsel for the prosecution. "I did not hear any. ' The prisoner seemed more sorrowful than angry. He said he hoped she wouldn't regret her change of mind, though he dared to say he could bear it. But he did say that in his country women generally found that it was a bad thing for them to deceive their lovers. Then he laughed and said that they were ' not in Italy, however, but in England; and the only revenge a man could take in England on a woman who had thrown him over, was to forget her as soon as possible and find another who would be more true to him."</p>
        <p>This trial would have been ,the final incident connected with the death by poison of Maud Valpy, and it is possible that, with the acquittal of the Count Ilario Francia, it would have passed into the limbo of forgetfulness, if, some three' or four years later, another incident had not occurred which revived it again in the minds of all men—an incident, indeed, which not only brought to light the manner in which Maud Valpy had been' sent to her doom, but also by whose hand she had been sent. - The Count had long ago left London, since, in spite of his acquittal, he had found that society seemed no longer as anxious to make him welcome in their midst as it had once been, but, instead, regarded him with anything but cordial glances. He was, therefore, at the time now arrived at, domiciled in Paris. But here, it would seem, members of the "Young Italy party were not treated with as much respect, nor as much con- sideration for their political opinions, as they were in the habit of receiving in London; and one morning the name of this individual was once more brought prominently before the eyes, and to the recollection of, the public, by what they read in their particular newspapers. It was announced in those papers that various members of the above party had been arrested for etnnsniracv ; that domiciliary visits had been paid, and their property sealed up by order of the French Government, and that those who were not under arrest were only in that position because they had managed to escape in time. Amongst these latter was the Count Ilario Francis, who. it was thought, had now departed for some South American State.</p>
        <p>It is true that even this revival in people's minds of a name which had for a time been so prominently notorious caused at first but a faint stir of interest, and it was highly probable that, even with this fresh mention of the man, he would soon have sunk again into forgetfulness, had not the Paris correspondents of the papers also given a description of some of his belongings which he had left behind in his flight. " Amongst those belongings," wrote these corres- pondents, each in their own words, "were many strange things papers containing schemes for overthrowing various Governments in Italy, for recruiting men and for, in some cases, assassinating rulers. But that which, to all English people, will prove of more interest than aught else is the following. In an English safe which this man left behind him (and which was blown open by order of the Government) was discovered a small gold fruit-knife that was, beyond all doubt, the knife of which so much was heard in the trial of Francia for the murder of Miss Valpy. One side of the blade was, when discovered, perfectly bright and clean, the other was black and corroded ,as though •with some acid. And, not far from where it was found in the safe, was a packet of letters, a small bottle, or phial, and close by that phial—indeed, it had undoubtedly been once wrapped round it —a paper on which was written in Italian the following: "Let the side of the knife with which you sevet the fruit he lightly smeared over-night with some of the contents of the phial by the aid of a camel's hair brush, while the most extreme care must be team that not one drop, nor the hundredth part of a drop, touches, or runs over, on to the other side. Thereby, the fruit, when divided, will contain in its one-half all that is necessary to produce certain death within twenty-four hours, whilk the other half will be perfectly free from anything that is harmful; and, of two persons partaking of that fruit, the one is doomed, the other escapes with impunity. Send me the money promised; lam almost starving. If I were not, I would sell no man this secret.—Thy friend, "Tin Dome."</p>
        <p>LIE END. i</p>
        <p>FOOTBAIIL PICKPOCKETS. Pickpockets who attbnded the Football Cup semi final at Birmingham were before the stipendiary at Birmingham on Tuesday. A gentleman lost a fifty- pound diamond scarf pin, and another a gold watch. A man identified by his finger-prints as a well-known London criminal was sentenced to 12 months imprison- ment. GOOD TEMPLAR MAGISTRATE.</p>
        <p>Lord Loreburn has odded the names of 20 gentlemen to the Commission a the Peace for Worcestershire. These include that of Mr. J. Matins, who is almost uni- versally known as the Chief of the Good Tempters organization.</p>
        <p>One of the Greatest Scandals of our Time. THE above is the flattering description applied by men of almost every party to the Dublin or ' Castle ' Government in Ireland. and a perusal of official figures or returns connected with that curious institution certainly goes far to justify the accusation. In the first place, it costs nearly 7?,, millions a year— an absolutely ridiculous burden on so poor a country as Ireland. Scotland. for instance, though richer and more populous, pays only si millions. The extravagance and non-success of the Irish Government is undoubtedly due to its being a completely bureau- cratic organization which spends Irish money as it likes. Accord- ing to the estimates of toos, no less than half of this 7i millions is officially included under salaries, pay, pensions, gratuities or allowances to Government emfiloyees, who now number about 80,000 in a population of 4 3-8 millions. Ireland, though less populous than Scotland. pays £60,000 a year more for her local government and Lao,ooo a year more for her judicial system. Although for many years she has produced fewer criminals than England. and only three-fifths as nrany as Scotland ta fact which may be verified by reference to official returns or to The Statesman's Year Ecok,') yet she pays a million more for her Constabulary than Scotland.thereby swallow- ing up many thousands of pounds that are urgently required for education and other essentials of that poverty-stricken people. The true cause of this maladministration, which, on its smaller scale almost rivals our late war scandals, is that the whole system of' Castle' government has now continued for 507 years without any serious measure of reform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MY DEAR NIECES IND NEPHEWS,-</head>
        <p>MY DEAR NIECES IND NEPHEWS,-</p>
        <p>cIP I expect you would be a. bit surprised if 1 I told you that I commenced this letter last I winter, and did not finish until the spring, and without leaving off. Well, according to our great scientists, this happens to be exactly the case. ..You see, it's like this. I started writing this afternoon about 6.15 until about 7 o'clock, and, according to these learned pro- fessors, I find spring oommenced at 6.33. I didn't hear it come, you know; but, according to the beautiful weather' I'll take it for granted that it is here. Hail 1 beautiful Spring. This week I am publishing a few puzzles for you to worry your brains about, and I am giving two prizes for the first two correct solutions sent in. I am printing the second part of that very pretty Japanese fairy tale, "Urashima." I hope you did not miss the opening part Last week. Did any of you recommend the "Christian Circle" Stories to your friends? if so, I shall be pleased to hear how they enjoyed reading them. I am glad to say that our roll of members is on the rise again—three Nephews this week. Just fancy, I believe, after all, that my Nieces are using their gentle persuasions to some good advantage. The prize this week goes to Hilda Mounoe, for a very original bit of poetry, entitled, "Poor Mr. Brain." Well, bye-bye, now, and don't be afraid to send me in some solutions for the puzzles. With the best of good wishes from</p>
        <p>lINCL3 NZD.</p>
        <p>WELCOME TO Reggie Lockwood. Bobby Smith. Augustus Straker.</p>
        <p>.---4--, REPLIES TO CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>Fred. B.—No Freddy, the Milky Way was not made by the "Cow that jumped over the Moon." Many thanks for kind letter. Atha K.—l think you are wrong in what you mention; in fact, I don't believe there is any cUocolate to equal Fry's. Their "Five Boys" Brand is truly delicious. Try it, and then write me again. PUZZLES. A WORD SQUARE. 1. To become weak. 2. Part of a railway. 3. A girl's name 4. Apparatus. • ~,HIDDEN FOOD NAMES. Paste will spoil if kept too long. Universal money would be a great boon to</p>
        <p>travellers. Hava you ever seen a bee fly? Eric eats all day' long. You might tell James I want him! Geoff is here with his tackle.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a, piece 6 poetry, entitled:</p>
        <p>POOR MR. BRAIN.</p>
        <p>Poor Mister Brain fell ill one day, I don't know</p>
        <p>what he'd got, But he seemed the same all over, and felt</p>
        <p>neither cold nor hot. At last his case became so bad, and he had go</p>
        <p>so thin, That he thought that he was going to die, so he</p>
        <p>called the doctor in. The doctor came and bade him tell what 'twas</p>
        <p>that ail'd him so, And Mister Brain explained to him his bitter tale of woe. "Dear sir, your case is very bad," at Last the doctor said; "It's a dreadful case of laziness, and I wonder you're not dead; However, we must not despair, we must take a cheerful view Of this distressing malady, and see what we can do. 0 We must poultice you with Industry—it cures so many ills— And we must put you on a course of Energetic pills; Your food we must attend to, you must take a daily course Of History and Geography, with Arithmetic for sauce; And you must take some Interest in everything you do, Or you won't digest your food at all, and 'twill</p>
        <p>do no good to you. You had better take, when you wake up, a good strong dose of Thought, It will help to make you better, and feel brighter, as you ought; Take some sweet extract of Cheerfulness to wash down every meal, And the laziness will vanish—you will daily better feel.</p>
        <p>We can mitigate this treatment just a little now and then— You can take a little Exercise with needle or with pen; • Persevere, and you will soon be in good health, sir, I foretell." He persevered as he was told—Mister Brain is now quite well</p>
        <p>HILDA MOTTNCE.</p>
        <p>ITEASHII&amp;A.'</p>
        <p>(Continued.)</p>
        <p>Oh, dear, what a lovely place it was! The malls of the Palace were of coral, the trees had emeralds for leaves and rubies for berries, the fishes' scales were of silver' and the dragon's tails of solid gold. Just think of the most beautiful, glittering things that you have ever seen, and put them all together, and then you wi now what this Palace looked like. And it all belonged to Urashima. for was he not the son-in-law of the Sea-God, the husband of the lovely Dragon Princess? • Well, they lived on happily for three years, wandering about every day among the beautiful trees with emerald leaves and ruby berries. But one morning ljrashima said to his wife: "I am very happy here. Still, I want to go home and see my father and mother and brothers and sis- ters. Just let me go for a short time, and I'll soon be back again." "I don't .like you to go," said she. "I aim very much afraid that something dreadful will happen. However, if you will go there is no help for it. Only you must take this box, and be very careful not to open it. If you open it, you will never be able to come back here." So Urashima promised to take great care of the box, and not to open it on any account; and then, getting into his boat, he rowed off, and at last landed on the shore of his. own country. But what had happened while he had been away? Where had his father's cottage gone to? What had become of the village where he used to live? The mountains, indeed, were there as before; but the trees on them had been cut down. The little brook that ran close by his father's cottage was still running,ebut there were no women washing clothes in it any more. It seemed very strange that everything should have changed so much in three short years. So, as two men chanced to pass along the beach, Urashima went up to them, and said: "Can you tell me please, where Urashima's cottage, that used to stand here, has been moved to?"</p>
        <p>ill I:</p>
        <p>"llrashimal " said they; "why! it was four hundred years ago that he was drowned out fishing. His parents and his brothers and their grandchildren are all dead long ago. It is an old, old story. How can you be so foolish as to ask after his cottage? It fell- to pieces hundreds of years ago." (To be concluded.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>.........+1•••</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have seen so many nice letters iii your magazine, until I thought I would send a short letter just to tell you what a comfort you are to me, and I want you to know that I love you. All the children tell about their pets, and, though I have a great many, I will only tell about my horse and dog. My horse is named St. Claire, and my dog is called "The Don " ; we call him Don all the time, and he knows his name as well as a boy. He is a shepherd dog, and very intelligent. I live in the country, and saddle my own horse, and when Don sees me get my bridle he jumps up and runs ahead, barking all the time with joy. But pony is better even than doggie, and I am never so happy as when galloping "over the hills and far away." I have, or rather, we have, taken, you ever since you commenced, and I wouldn't give you up for anything. I have a I:ttie cousin living with me now; his name is Frank, and nothing pleases him better than for me or Grandtha to read your stories to him. This letter is too long now, so good-bye. From your reader,</p>
        <p>DIMPLE IC-.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am a little boy eight years old, and have never attended school. I have two brothers and two sisters, all younger than myself. My little brother, Willie, is very funny. He is two years old and has invented a language of his own. He calls himself "Dine." A dog he calls a "boo-woo " ; a chicken, "oo- oo" ; a bird is " peep" ; "little" is "ee," and "large" is " 0 "; "upstairs" is "up-down," and "downstairs" is "down-up-down." He has his own words for everything, and his sentences, formed with these queer words, sound :very funny. Little sister Yeddy, four years old, is the only one who understands him. She acts, as his interpreter. Yours truly,</p>
        <p>MASON S. P-----.</p>
        <p>SHORTENING THE BABY. Our baby now is four months old, A bonnie boy, with hair like gold; And his long clothes are put away— For Mother shortened\ him to-day. And no* the rogue can kick about; His little feet go in and out As though they could not rest, and he Is just as happy as can be. Besides, he feels quite proud to-day With all his long clothes put away, And dressed so fine, and then you know, We praise the boy, we love him so! He has the loveliest of frocks, All trimmed with lace, and two pink socks That Father bought, the best by far And prettiest in the whole bazaar. His grandmother must see him soon; We,all will go this afternoon, , And take the pet, and stay for tea,— And what a riot there will be! At first, perhaps, she may not know The baby, he has dwindled so; But let her guess and do not say That Mother shortened him to-day</p>
