      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>QUICK CHANGE ARTIST.</head>
        <p>A QUICK CHANGE ARTIST.</p>
        <p>John Roberts, of Oxford-street, Widnes, was chi rged on Monday,with having stolen lOid. from the Baths Spring Vanit9, Widnes, the property of Thomas Hadley, on Sunday. He pleaded not guilty.—Superintendent Strickland said that at 1135 o'clock on the previous day prisoner went into the Baths Spring Vaults and asked for a glass of beer for which he paid. A man named Percival then came in asked for a pint of beer for which be tendered .a shilling in payment. The bar- • maid served him and handed the shilling to Mrs. Radley, wife of the licensee, to get change. Mrs. Radley changed the shilling and put the 103. d. on the counter. Prisoner then drank his beer, picked up the 10,1 d., dnd left the house. Mrs. Radley followed him, but the prisoner ran away. Later in the day be was apprehended When charged, he said : "I got the Mid., but I did not steal it."—Eliza Hadley corroborated.—The prisoner alleged that he had given Mrs. Radley a shilling.—Mrs. Radley denied this. Prisoner gave her a penny and a halfpenny.—Walter Percival, Davies-street, and Patrick Verdin, Cromwell-street, gave evidence 'corroborating the statement of the Superintendent.—Superintendent Strickland said the prisoner was a St. Helens man of very bad character. He bad been charged with various offences eleven times. He seemed to have been very lucky on several occasions.—The Bench studied the prisoner's record, and said that he had been very lucky. He would have to go to gaol for a month on this occasion.</p>
      </div>
