      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the ,Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>The Duties of School Managers.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E, C. E. Carleton has forwarded us the fol- lowing copy of a letter he has addressed to the Director of Education. Dear Sir,—With reference to the resolution of the Education Committee passed on the 25th ult, and,which you have forwarded to me I shall be glad if you will remind your Committee that I wrote quite a year or two ago giving von my reasons for not making the returns asked for. believe no notice was taken of the com- munication, which led me to conclude that the views I expressed were acquiesced in by the committee as far as I was concerned, and that no further comunication *as called for, even though one of the forms sent to the other schools might inadvertently reach me. Tbese• returns have never been made by me, and it is singular that after a few years the subject should be raised in the committee for the first time.</p>
        <p>The fact however that the Subject has now been dis- cussed will enable me to explain my view as to these returns,giving my explanation the same publicity in the press that the discussion of your committee has received. As you are aware the information dealt with in the returns is to be found in books that are always transmitted to your office on application. 'This informa- tion your committee has a right to have; but the work of tabulating the information for the convenience of your committee is clearly a work 'that ought to be done in the Education Office, for the doing of which the committee have an ample supply of paid labour at their command. The tabulation of this information is no part of that work of school management which devolves on the local managers; but is rather a part of the in- spection of that management which is the special department. of the Education Committee; and it is not a sound plan to ask the managers to do any part of this inspection even to save the committee trouble • indeed there would be no saving of trouble, as the Committee would be bound to check the returns sent, by comparing them with records in the minute book and log book. For this reason I rather regard the demand of your Committee on this matter as somewhat unbusinesslike. Of course we all know it is a good thing to tabulate the attendances of school managers as it is to tabulate the attendances of Town Councillors and Guardiatis,but everyone knows that this work is invariably done by the paid officials; and that because it is felt that the returns should have official authority. Of course if your Committe would like to submit the points in discussion to the Board of Education should be quite willing for this to be done. I think you will see from this letter that there is nothing unreasonable in the view I take, and that there has been no intentional discourtesy as far as I am con- cerned.—Yours faithfully.</p>
        <p>E. C. E. CARLETON. Holy Trinity Vicarage, Warrington, 4th April, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>" TICKET-OF-LEAVE-MAN." Performances by the Catholic Dramatic</head>
        <p>" TICKET-OF-LEAVE-MAN." Performances by the Catholic Dramatic</p>
        <p>Society.</p>
        <p>For some years the St. llary's Dramatic Society has done noble service in the cause- of charity and the enthusiasm displayed by the amateurs is worthy of all praise. A. new departure which has been adopted this season should have good results, for with the object of extending the scope of the Society its name has been changed to the Warrington Catholic Amateur Dramatic Society. The advantage of this step must be seen in the long run, for it will no doubt interest a wider circle in the Society's admirable work and open up a larger field of talent. There was a large audience at the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening when the Society presented a popular play by the late Tom Taylor entitled "The ticket-of-leave man." It is a somewhat lurid type of drama, full of incident and excitement, and tellirg the usual story of villainy triumphing over innocence' but only to be thwarted in the end. Robert Brierley,a Lancashire lad,goes "seeing life "in London and falls into the hands of sharpers who inveigle him into passing worthless cheques. He is arrested and serves three years imprisonment, and it is upon his release as a " ticket-of-leave man" that the plot unfolds itself. Brierley is hunted down relentlessly by the criminal associates who led him astray in the first inst ince, and is repeatedly foiled in his efforts to gain an honest livelihood. When on the verge of desperation he is persuaded to join in a burglarious enterprise against a former benefactor by whom he was discharged on his history becoming known, but his better feelings gain the mastery, and he warns the victim of the impending danger, He is helped by the friendly offices of a detective, who has . sympathy with him in his painful position and appears . on the scene in the nick of time, and the play ends with the overthrow of the villain and the restoration of Brierley to his old master's favour. All this is notachieved without desperate struggles, the flourishing of revolvers, and the manipulation of handcuffs, and excitement is worked up to fever beat. The end, however, is as it should be, and the curtain is rung down with the prospect of unalloyed bliss for everyone except the arch-conspirators who are within the clutches of the law.</p>
        <p>On the whole the 'Society is to be congratulated upon a creditable production, and that, it afforded , pleasure to the audience was evident from the • hearty applause. Most of the leading characters have figured with some success in previous ventures, but there are several new faces. The Ellowing :is the,drantatis persomae:— .Robert Brierley (a Lancashire Lad)...Mr C. J. Newton. James Dalton Mr. T. Burke. Hawkshaw (a Detective) Mr. H. Murray. Melter Moss (a Fence) Mr. George Newton. Green. Jones Mr. Gibson (a Billbroker) Mr. A. E. Price. ..Sarn Willoughby Mr. A. Kilduff. Maltby Mr. J. Newnes. Burton Mr. G. Stanton. :Sharpe Jackson Mr. J. Stirrup. ,Smith Brown Mr. C. McDonald. ' May Edwards Miss A. E. Marron. .Emily St. Evremond Miss E. V. Johnson Mrs. Willoughby Miss A. M. Stirrup' Guests, Navvies, Sm. As the hero of the piece, Mr. C. J. Newton has a .part which snits him admirably and he rises to the ,occasion. He acts with an ease and naturalness and in the bluff and hearty way characteristic of the typical Lancashire lad and his words can be distinc'ly heard by all., In the pathetic pas- 'cages he is particularly good. Miss A. E. Marron gives a creditable .poertrayal of May Edwards, the devoted wife .of the •"ticket-of-leave man," though at times she is hardly emotional enough. The role, how- ever, makes considerable dernauds upon an amateur, and Miss Marron fully deserved the applause which rewarded her efforts. There are two villains in the piece, one James Dalton with a couple of ill-sound- ing aliases and Melter Moss, an individual of a Jewish .caste. Mr. F. Burke achieves a triumph as Dalton, the anscrupulous criminal, and it is evident that he has made a very ,careful study of the part, for he brings out its varying moods excellently. Eri,-; disguises, too, are must effective, and it is difficult to realize that the desperate burglar is no other than the polished city " gentleman" who appeared in a previous scene. Mr. George Newton, too, is well placed as Moss, the accotoplice who runs little risk but prafits largely. He acts with great coolness but the simulation of the nasal accent some- times makes it rather difkult to hear all he says. Hawkshaw, the detective, is one of the leading figures in the drama and Mr. H. Murray's reading of the part is capital, especially when," made tip " as a navvy, he suddenly discloses his identity to the astonished Brierlev. Mr. W. F. Turner is one of the successes of the evening as the grotesque Green ;ones, and Miss E. V. Johnson also does well as Emily St. Evremond. Mr. Gibson, the billbroker, is admirably impersonated by Mr. A. E. Price, who comports himself with the calmness and dignity befitting the head of a city firm. There is much humour to relieve the piece and for this Mr. A. Kilduff as Sam Willoughly, a precocious youngster, is principally responsible. He is possessed of an unsusal degree of confidence and mightily amuses the audience with his escapades. A. word of praise is clue to Miss A. M. Stirrup who, as Mrs. Willoughby, has to treat all and sundry to mournful recitals of her trials which are mostly due to her undutiful grandson. Mr. J. Newnes is success- ful as Maltby and the other minor characters are ,well sustained. The perfermance was repeated on Thursday and again last evening and the drama will be produced for the last time this (Saturday) evening. The object to which the proceeds will be devoted—the Poor Children's Summer Camp —is a most deserving one and should appeal to all classes of the community. Mr. R. Whitfield's orchestra is in attendance anti plays selections between the acts.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST PRISON REFORMER. Warrington's Memorial to John Howard. Warrington's memorial to John Howard, the great prison reformer who was intimately associated with the town and published his epoch-making work on "The State of Prisons "here, was unveiled at noon yester- day. It takes the form of a bronze tablet which adorns the exterior of the handsome new premises of Messrs. J. E. Richardson and Son, which, it is inter- esting to note, occupy- the site of the house where John Howard lodged during his visits to Warrington. The tablet has been erected by the Warrington Society and bears a medallion of John Howard, and the fol- lowing inscription :—" John Howard lodged at a 'silversmith's shop which stood upon tho site of thes premises whilst printing his great works on prison reform 1777-80-84-89. This tablet was erected by the Warrington Society." The society was fortunate in inducing Major Arthur G. F. Griffiths, who served in the Crimean war, has acted as deputy governor of several large gaols and is the author of many works on prison life, to perform the unveiling ceremony. There was a large attendance to witness the proceed- ings, which were presided over by Alderman Bennett, one of the society who takes a great interest in the work.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he had to apologise for the absence of their president (Mr. William Long), of his co-secretary (Mr. E. J. Hall) and especially for the absence, through ill-health, of Mr. J. E. Richardson whose family had been connected with the premises which lately occupied that spot for over fifty years. 'To him and to Mr. J. H. Boydell they were indebted for that handsome edifice which was such an ornament to Bridge-street and the central part of which stood upon the site of the old shop "three doors from Dolman's- lane " where John Howard lodged with Mrs. Wild, a silversmith, whilst printing his im- mortal books: "The State of the Prisons in England and Wales," the " Appendix to the State of the Prisons," and the " Prisons and Lazarettos of Europe." When the claims of modern progress necessi- tated the removal of that and the two adjaceut shops Mr. Richardson, with admirable public spirit, agreed at once to call the new premises "Howard Buildings," and placed upon them at his own expense the striking I terracotta panels which adorned the front, and lent to it so much distinction. The Warrington Society were deeply sensible of Mr. Richardson's generosity, and they felt that the interest of the place would be still! further accentuated by the addition of a short descrip- tive tablet setting forth the salient facts. They accord- ingly invited subscriptions, Messrs. J. E, Wright, Garnett, and Wright, the architects who had designed the panels,were good enough to design a tablet, and this was beautifully executed in hammered bronze by I Messrs. George Wragge' Ltd. of Salford, and placetlin its position on the last day Of December. Various cir- cumstances had delayed the unveiling until now, but a more appropriate date they could not possibly have selected, for that was the 130th anniversary of the day on which,his earliest and greatest work just printed at the press of William Eyres in the Horse Market,l John Howard added the final touch by dedicating it to the Honourable the House of Commons" and sent it forth upon its epoch-making way. If they were fortunate in the date of their ceremony they were not less fortunate in the gentleman who had so kindly consented to perform it. Major Arthur Griffiths was a Crimean veteran, had held a high military position at Gibraltar, been deputy governor of three great London gaols, and was for nearly twenty years inspector of prisons to her late Majesty, was the author of many Well-known works on prisons and on prison problems, as well as on other subjects, and had had the distinguished honour of winning the gold medal offered by the late Czar for the best essay on Howard and his work in an international competition to commemorate the centenary of Howard's death at Cherson on the 20th of January 1790. Major Griffiths might indeed be said to be the official descendant of that other self-appointed and unofficial "Inspector of Prisons" whose memory they all so much revered and whose intimate connection with their town they were assembled to lovingly com- memorate. (Applause.) Major GniFFrnis in unveiling the memorial said there were many memorials to John Howard. There was a fine statue in St. Paul's and another in Howard's own county town of Bedford, but he did not think there were any to beat this in beauty or appropriateness, because Howard was in his way a Warrington man. He passed his great work through the Press in Warrington and was associated here with many chosen friends whose memory was still cherished. But his greatest memorial of all was his achievements—what he did for suffering humanity. (Applause). LUNCHEON AT THE OLD ACADEMY. MAJOR GRIFFITHS ON PRISON REFORM. Major GniFFITHs was subsequently entertained at luncheon at the Old Academy, and some interesting speeches were delivered. Mr. F. Monks presided over a large gathering and after luncheon proposed the toast of "the King "which was heartily honoured. Alderman BENNETT announced the receipt of apologies for non-attendance from Mrs. Garnett, Miss Crosfield, Councillor Ball, Mr. William Fletcher, Mr. J. W. Slack, Miss Bessie Dutton and the Rev. Father Wilson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
