      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 6, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 6, 1907.</p>
        <p>wish was rather to prevent critne than to punish it. We desired to counteract and correct it when it was still curable and before it had hardened into chronic and incorrigible forms. It was now an established fact that there was a criminal age and that if we took boys just entering it, in their early teens that was to say, we might wean them from their evil course and by keeping them in the straight road might very appreciably diminish the whole volume of crime. (Applause). The MAYOR proposed "Success to the Warrington Society" and expressed his warm approval of its efforts to perpetuate the memory of great men who had been associated with the good old town. Alderman BENNETT responded and said the programme of the Society practically involved the translation of this big industrial centre into a City Beautiful—a green city—and he had no doubt that would come in due time. Part of its work was the erection of tablets to the memory of distinguished people who had been associated with the town and already three had been unveiled.. They had not got half through their programme if they were to do justice to the antiquities of Warrington. (Applause.) Mr. C. MADELEY proposed the health of "Our Guest," and said they all knew the claims Major Griffiths had on their respect. They admired him as a soldier who took part -in the Crimean war—an event which to all of them was really the beginning of modern history. As a military critic the Major had for a quarter of a century been well known, and he (Mr. Afadoley) could recommend anyone who wished to read a clear and interesting account of the Boer War to per- use Major Griffiths' articles. As a popular novelist he had a great reputation, having written considerably over a score of books. They also knew him as a prison administrator for twenty-five years, and as a prison historian, and, finally, in the aspect in which he appealed to them most—as a prison reformer. To quote a sentence from one of his books, he believed that "Draconian laws have never succeeded in diminishing, still less in stamp- ing out crime." They were not, however, to suppose that the Major was a mere soft-hearted philanthropist of the kind they heard so much of whenever a murder or a murderer happened to be more conspicuous than usual, but he had a commonsense appreciation of the necessity that crime must be punished, and that the treatment of criminals must be preventive. Major GRIFFITHS responded and said it was a labour of love to him to come to Warrington and unveil the memorial to such a great philanthropist. (Applause.) Mr. U. STUART PAGE proposed the toast of "Designers of tablet and owners of Howard buildings" and said Messrs. Wright, Garnett and Wright were to be congratulated on the artistic quality of the work because although on a small scale it fulfilled one of the essentials of true art by its adaptation to the purpose for which it was intended. They were particularly indebted to the firm for giving the design free of charge. The public spirit of the owners of Howard Buildings in assisting in the commemoration of the great reformer was particularly commendable. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Sfr. ROBERT GARNETT briefly responded for Messrs. Wright, Garnett and Wright. Alderman BENNETT replied for Mr. Richardson and said it was a matter of great regret to him not to be able to attend, as he had.taken the keenest interest in the memorial.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER proposed the health of Mr. F. Monks who, he said, had hIl his life been honourably associated with the town. By his loyalty and generosity to Warrington he had left many memorials and had shown his interest in all good works. Mr. MONKS in responding said he had spent all his life in Warrington, having been born in a house in Winwiek-street which stood opposite to the Central Station warehouse and had recently been converted into a shop. He gave a number of interesting remini- scences of his early days. On the proposition of Mr. A. MOUNFIELD the health of the Mayor was drunk and his Worship replied. An interesting collection of books and mementoes of John Howard was on view and portraits of the reformer I which hang in the Academy were decorated with laurel.</p>
        <p>mittee resolved, that the Town Clerk ascertain how the claim is made up.</p>
        <p>BUDGET NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett will present the annual Budget statement to the Council at this meeting. A reduction in the rates in expected to be announced.</p>
        <p>Runcorn won the South-West Lancashire Cup on Saturday by trouncing Wigan, who have bad a bad Easter. Warrington's last chance vanished with the success of the " Linnets," who have fairly earned the honour. It was that surprise win at Wigan earlier in the season which made all the difference in the end.</p>
        <p>The table for the South-West Lancashire Competiti- tion is as follows : Pts.</p>
        <p>P. VV. 14 D. For Agg. 14s. Runcorn 10- ... 8 ... 2 ... 0 ... 142 ... Si 16 W'gton.. 10 ... 7 2 ... 1 ... 137 ... 79, 15 Wigan... 10 ... 7 ... 3 ... 0 ... 188 ... 89 ... 14 Leigh ... 10 ... ... 6 ... 1 ... 114 ... 115 ... Widnes.. 10 ... 3 ... 7 ... 0 ... 80 ... 214 ... 6 S.Helens 10 ... 2 0 ... 64 ... 149 ... 4</p>
        <p>The postponed match with Rochdale Hornets will take place at Warrington on Saturday, April 29th. This will be Warrington's last League engagement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of Tuesday's Meeting.</p>
        <p>The April meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening at 6 o'clock, when the following among other business will be transacted.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>Councillor Stott will move: On the proceedings of the Health Committee of the 19th March, 1907,;being monad for adoption: That the minute relating to the appointment of a new Medical Officer of Health be amended by the deletion of the sum of £l5O, the pro- posed salary, and the substitution therefor of the sum of £4OO.</p>
        <p>THE LATE CIIIEF CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>The Watch Committee has passed resolutions express- ing appreciation of the excellent service of the late Mr. Talbot, and conveying sympathy to his widow. It was further resolved : That a gratuity be granted to the widow of the late Luke Talbot of the sum of .C 375 being the amount of one month's pay for every -completed year of approved service. Advertisements had been issued for a successor to Mr. Talbot, and the salary offered is £350. P.C. Corbett has retired after 2G years service on a pension for life equal to two-thirds his animal pay. STREET IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee the Town Clerk reported that Mrs. Hewitt's solicitors were prepared to accept the sum of .£750 and to give immediate possession of the property, the sum named to include all solicitor's and surveyor's charges. It was resolved that the Town Clerk be authorised to offer the sum of £750 for the purchase of Mrs. Hewitt's property, such sum to include all legal and surveyor's charges. Mr. R. Curran's valuation of the property amounted to £667 10s. 4d.</p>
        <p>Major ARTHUR GRIFFITHS proposed the toast of "The immortal memory of John [-Toward" and said the great philanthropist often delignted to dwell in this ancient town. He (the Major) asked them to share his deep appreciation of John Howard's inestimable character and to remember his transcendent services to suffering humanity. In the words of the inscription ! on the statue raised to him in St. Paul's Cathedral this ; extraordinary man gained the admiration of the whole civilized world; from the dungeon to the throne he ; was spoken of with gratitude and respect. He spent : and lost his life in the ardent, unremitting exercise of Christian charity and diligently followed in the footsteps of the divine founder of our faith. He was ; never diverted from the path of duty ; no difficulties I deterred him, no personal danger affrighted him. With , undeviating directness and whole-souled unflagging energy he trod the road he had marked out for himself 1 till he finally reached a crown of martyrdom in a far off foreign land. His name must be ' ever cherished amongst us as one of the noblest and most illustrious among Englishmen. The Major : then recalled the history of John Howard and the , imperishable nature of his achievements. It was 1 HEALTH COMMITTEE. only when appointed High Sheriff of his county I This Committee has accepted the tender of Messrs. (Bedford) that he awoke suddenly to the grievous con- Wilson and Stockall, of Bury, for the supply of a dition of prisoners and the intolerable cruelty with brougham ambulance according to specification,delivered which they were treated. He saw with consuming at Warrington at £llO. indignation that free men, men acquitted in open court, 1 EXTENSION AT THE ELECTRICITY WORKS. were still detained in prison until they could pay the. Mr. F. V. L. Matthias, Electrical Engineer, has sub- gaolers' fees, and when he pressed for the abolition of ; mitted a lengthy report to his committee on the pro- this injustice by making the gaoler a salaried servant, I posed extension of the boiler plant. The estimated ex- he was invited to ascertain the practice in other juris- penditure is as follows : dictions. This led him to begin that Two 14,0001b5. Boilers, with superheaters, chain £ PAINFUL PILGRIMAGE grate stokers, and mountings which occupied him continuously to tha end. He travelled through the country far and Boiler House Superstructure and bunkers 1320 Economiser 550 wick indefatigable in his "circumnavigation of Coal conveyors, elevator, including bunkers for charity" as it was aptly styled by Edmund Burke, old boiler house llOO unwearied and undaunted in his determination to see Induced draught apparatus 350 with his own eyes and realize by his own feelings the Feed water softener extension 2OO abominable ill-usage of all doomed to detention within Excavating 1 ~- 175 for foundations lOO four walls. He penetrated the lowest depths of the ) ' Concreting prison house and brought to light its most horrible Brickwork for two boilers and economiser 250 secrets. Howard himself always went in danger of his ; Steam and water pipes, and valves • 370 own life in his visitation of these pestitential precincts. One case iron feed tank The interior of the prisons was so poisonous that , Water meter Governors would not enter them. The air he breathed , Flues B5 was so offensive and so infected h s apparel that Alterations to existing buildings, drain pipes, and he could not travel in a carriage but rode on horseback i cables from place to place- he constantly used disinfectants, Engineering contingencies 250 . but vinegar and smelling salts soon became useless. All 1 • • this and more might be read in his own account of his self-sacrificing peregrination through the abodes of Electrical Plant. misery in this enlightened land at the close of the 18th Balancer, £350 century. Hie immortal work "The State of Prisons," The Committee has resolved that the recommendation was one possessing peculiar inter