        <p>Two Bridge-street tradesmen Mr. T. Crampton (with 22 years tenaucy) and Mr. T. J. Lee (with 36 years tenancy) have been dealt with on the following terms. Resolved :—That in consideration of Mr. Crampton's long tenancy a plot of land having a frontage to Bridge- street of lb feet and containing about 180 square yards be leased to Mr. Grampton for a term of 75 years sub- ject to an annual ground rent of .£6O. That a plot of land having a frontage to Bridge-street of 28 feet containing 132 square yards or thereabouts be sold to Mr. Lee for the sum of £1716.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk reported that he had received from the Local Government Board an order for altering the Warrington Corporation Act, 1899, with regard to the new street behind Bridge-street, and that the Board had not inserted power to carry the street through to Sankey-street.</p>
        <p>8704</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>For the fifth time in the history of the Northern Union Cup Competition, Warrington have reached the semi-final tie. They travelled thus far in the first year, 18904, when they succumbed to Batley on the ground where they were s'o successful on Satur- day, viz., Huddersfield. Moreover they have beaten Batley in a cup-tie this season which is perhaps a hopeful augury. The "gallant youths" have been three tunes Cup-holders, which is a 'record no other club approaches except Halifax who won in 1893 and 1894. Warrington were three times in the final before they annexed the trophy, and everyone expects that they will figure in the final again this year. That they will win the Cup is a matter I am very doubtful about. Odds are certainly in favour of the survivor of the Salford-Oldham tie. There is just this to be said: Warrington are coming back into their best form, and that is good enough for anybody.</p>
        <p>According to , expectations, Huddersfield put up a stiff fight on Saturday, but in the end they had to adtpit the superior class of Warrington. The Fartown ground presented a fine spectacle with over 13,000 people present, and a glorious sun lighting up the famous terrace. I should have thought the crowd somewhat larger numerically, but at any rate it was the best of the day, and produced £IOS for the com- peting clubs to share. The turf was in fine order and everything pointed to the fast game which ensued. I was rather surprised that the balance of opinion in Huddersfield favoured the chances of Warrington, notwithstanding the improved form and keenness of the local men. That fifty points at Wilderspool had its effect in making the Wirepullers respected.</p>
        <p>There was plenty for the crowd to shout about early on. Huddersfield played with wind and sun at their backs and they gave Warrington a very starm time of it. The forwards were both fast and iierce and the backs played with a confidence which taxed all Warrington's cup-tie resource to keep them out. Still the defence wawsteady under pressure until Kitchen got past Brooks by main force and managed to pass the ball to Muxworthy who scored. What a roar there was! Warrington's lusty-voiced supporters were drowned in the sea ofesound. From this point- to the interval, the home team over-played Warrington at all points, and the latter were lucky to have no more than two penalty goals added to the score against them. Some• ; thing must be said \in praise of the defence, and the I tackling of Isherwood, Taylor, and Tilley when the I battle waged near the line. Little was seen of War- rington's cleverness in this half: They were biding 1 their time.</p>
        <p>I On changing ends Warrington set about the task of 'winning the game in a businesslike fashion. There was not a great deal of wind, but, Warrington over- played Huddersfield as much as till" themselves had I been over-played. In ten minutes they had drawn I level, and in a quarter of an hour they were on velvet, iso to speak. The first point came to Fish as the I result of smart passing near the line, and though the langh, was wide the scorer added a goal. Warrington , went great guns after this, and as the result of a hot attack in the home quarter, Ike Taylor dropped a fine goal with the left foot. , This was an extremely valuable point, as it practically made Warrington safe from defeat. But they meant winning outright, and before Huddersfield had got their second wind Brooks made a beautiful opening for Hockanhall, who sprinted finely for 40 yards, and grounded near the goal, Fish again converting —l2-7. At this point Huddersfield made a fine rally and were rewarded by ! another penalty goal from long range by Bartholomew. IThis was their last say in the game for Warrington rushed the ball back again and Heath got over with a strong individual effort. The ex-Salfordian ought to have got another five minutes later for Thomas gave I him a beautiful pass which was missed. There was ' little more of incidentand Warrington passed into the / semi-final by 15 points to 9 points.</p>
        <p>Chief praise is due to Brooks. He changed places with Hockenhall just before the interval and practi- cally won the game himself on resuming. His work behind the scrum was splendid and the way he beat the home halves and centres put the Warrington threequarters well on top. On his display at Hudders- field and Runcorn, Brooks is a better half than wing and that is saying a lot. He cossesses just that robustness of style which the others lack and which does so much to change the course of a game. Unluckily he got his right thumb badly hurt in the last five minutes. If he is not fit on the 13th be will be much missed.</p>
        <p>Warrington's forwards deserve credit also. They lasted better than the home six, and their extra weight told in the second half. Thomas and Heath were the pick. Isherwood, through he dropped a pass or two, played a sound game, while his fellow-centre, Taylor, was in grand form. Fish suffered much from deliberate obstruction, but he worked hard and perspired pro- fusely. He still carries too much weight and will have to do some hard training if he wishes to captain Warrington through the closing stages of the Com- petition. Tilley made some fine saves, but lack of speed got him into difficulties more than once. I thought he might have marked more in the first half and saved his forwards.</p>
        <p>Quite the feature of the cup-ties on Saturday was the defeat of the four Yorkshire clubs, though two of them were at home. The semi-finals are in con- sequence to be contested in Lancashire and the draw is as follows : Warrington v. Swinton, at Wigan. . Salford v. Oldham, at Rochdale. The final tie will be played on the Broughton Rangers' ground on April 27.</p>
        <p>Dame Fortune has smiled on Warrington all through and is apparently still looking this way. There is no doubt that Swinton will be tackled with confidence, even though the venue is Wigan, that abode of ill-luck for Warrington. Fish could do with a mascot—or a suit of armour—for the occasion. Surely he has got to the end of a long chapter of accidents at Central Park. At any rate he will have the sympathy of the crowd there.</p>
        <p>Warrington played League matches on Monday and Tuesday. They showed brilliant form against Leeds on Monday and avenged the heavy defeat sustained at Headingley. Jordan quite surprised his best friends by his display on the right wing. On Tuesday, War- rington took a mixed team to Huddersfield again, and the local men got the compensation of two League points. They found Warrington in a less determined mood and scored 14 points to 3. Oldham and Ferguson beat Wigan at Wigan on Monday, and knocked the latter clean out of the coveted fourth place. The same combination just won at Hull by 2-0 the following day, so that Oldham are well in the running for the double event this season, in the League replay they will be at home to Runcorn, while Halifax will have Keighley as ready victims.</p>
        <p>The League Table up-to-date is as follows For Agst. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P.Centage. Halifax 34 27 5 2 125133 649 11 49 229 8215 Oldham 34 26 7 1 77101 457 37 51 227 7794 Runcorn :10 23 7 0 75132 546 36 43 216.76 66 Keighley 24 17 6 1 70 97 431 36 53 231 7291 Wigan '34 93 10 1 103150 659 53 56 274 69.11 Leeds 3O 19 9 2 74 92 424 50 67 301 66.66 Elunslet 32 21 11 U 86116 520 66 73 351 65112 Warrington 33 20 12 1 73127 527 56 64 304 6212 Salford 3l 18 13 0 78 91 459 67 62 320 5896 Broughton 3O 17 12 1 77114 496 35 55 235 5533 Barrow 96 13 12 1 42 86 342 56 79 349 5192. Widnes 32 15 17 0 57 92 3911 63 80 366 46.87 Hull K.R 20 9 10 1 41 43 217 49 74 320 4750 Swinton 3l 14 17 0 52 68 308 62 83 373 45.16 Bradford 3O 12 16 2 64 87 389 59 83 367 4313 Leigh 97 11 15 1 45 74 312 58 64 308 42.59 Hud'stleld 29 12 17 0 56101 415 83 94 448 41.37 , Dewsbury 26 10 15 1 49 76 326 58 87 377 40.33 , Wak'fild T 25 915 1 39 60 258 52 92 380 38'00 Rochdale 94 8 15 1 30 67 261 35 67 271 3511 Batley 911 8 15 1 48 41 228 52 74 326 35.41 St. Helens 26 9 17 0 58 86 374 49 85 352 34161 Hull '32 11 21 0 56 75 337 85 115 515 3417 York 99 5 17 0 28 47 197 65 106 418 2272 Brantley Liverpl. C 29 029 0 14 16 76 171 332 1338 o'oo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>The members of the-Warrington Cycling Club opened their season on Thursday evening when a smoking concert was held at the Patten Arms Hotel, Mr. Peld- house, captain, presiding over a large attendance. Mr. T. R. Ford, who also acted as accompanist opened the concert with a well executed pianoforte solo. Songs were given by Messrs. J. L. Davenport, W. Clay and J. Babbitt. It is interesting to note that the last named sang a delightful new song "The dream of dreams" written and composed by Mrs. Hubi-Newcombe. Cornet solos by Ar. Rutter were well rendered. Mr. J. Law- less contributeda song with banjo accompaniment whilst, Mr. G. N. Legge was very successful with his humour- ous character songs. All the artistes were in excellent form and the concert was altogether up to the high standard of previous entertainments in connection with the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>"THE QUALITY OF RUBBER IN TYRES."</head>
        <p>"THE QUALITY OF RUBBER IN TYRES."</p>
        <p>The falling off ia the wearing and in the non-punctur- ing properties of tyres has bean most noticeable for some years past, and we have come to the conclusion that it is in a large measure owing to the source of supply. At any rate, knowing that Messrs. David Moseley and Sons, of Manchester used to manufacture for the Dunlop and other Companies,we recently obtained a pair of their tyres and are glad to say we have now got hold of a really good make, judging by their appear- ance and the " feel ." they give when riding them. Riders could not do better than specify them on their new machines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Last Meeting of the Old Board.</p>
        <p>The final meeting of the Warrington Board of Guar- dians as at present constituted was held at the Work- house on Wednesday, under the presidency of Mr. J. Billinge. There were also present :—Mrs. Wilkinson, Rev. T. R. Matthews. Messrs. A. J. Jolley, G. Mather, M. W. Seton, R. Forshaw. G. Thompson, A. Brooks, VV. C. Brooks, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Avvbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools).</p>
        <p>HOUSE COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the House Committee the resignation of B. Wilson, 'male lunatic attendant, was accepted. Messrs. Thompson, W. C. Brooks and Davies were appointed a sub-committee to confer with the master and report on the various points raised in the report of the Lunacy Commrnissioner which was referred by the Board to the House Committee. The question of appointing additional charge nurses was deferred until the constitution of the new committee. The draft pro- posed rules for hospital administration were submitted and it was decided to defer their consideration for a fortnight. The minutes were approved. THE COTTAGE HOMES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Padgate Cottage Homes Com- mittee it was resolved that the contract for bedsteads be given to the Firth Company for the sum of £3O is. Mr. E. L. Bent's gift of 207 buns for the children on Good Friday was acknowledged with thanks. The minutes were appproved. REGISTRATION OF PAUPERS.</p>
        <p>Alluding to the new register of paupers which the Local Government Board now requires all Boards of Guardians to compile, the Clerk said the Warrington Union in the first instance did not think the expenditure was justifiable for the purpose and they contended that whatever expenditure was incurred should be repaid by the Treasury and not fall upon the rates. A new feel- ing arose afterwards. It was found that most Unions in the country were complying, and by the direction of the committee to whom the question was referred, a competent man had been appointed to attend to the work. With regard to the expenditure the Local Government Board now wrote stating that they had no funds for the purpose and they could not hold out any hope of a grant from the Treasury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>RATES.</head>
        <p>THE RATES.</p>
        <p>The meeting approved the issuing of precepts upon the various perishes for a rate of Gd. in the S.!„ as previ- ously anticipated.</p>
        <p>MRS. CLAY'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. TITOMPSON said that twelve years ago it was their pleasure to welcome to the Board the first lady Guardian for the Warrington Union. He was sure it would be the wish of the Board that'they should now express their appreciation of the services rendered by Mrs. Clay during that period. She had worked very hard and very pleasantly with everyone and they all regretted her resignation. He moved that the Clerk should write to Mrs. Clay conveying the Board's appreci- ation of her services and he was sure that sentiment would be endorsed not only by every member of the Board but by all the officials. Mr. JOLLEY seconded.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said Mrs. Clay had always had the interests of the poor at heart and had endeavoured to serve them to the best of her ability. When she became a member it was a hew departure for a lady Guardian to be elected and she had fully demonstrated that in Poor Law administration there was a field of service which ladies could fulfil better than gentlemen. (Rear, hear.) Mr. FORSHAW said he had admired the unobtrusive way in which Mrs. Clay had done most effective work. She had a keen and correct judgment and by her resignation, which they all deplored, the Board lost one of its best members.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER, as Chairman of the Schools Committee, spoke in terms of high praise of Mrs. Clay's services in the vice-chair of that committee. She was gifted with business qualtities which were lacking in many men. The Rev. T. R. MATTHEWS said he had very strong reason for supporting that resolution, because Mrs. Clay was good enough to come and help a lady opponent of his in his first election. (Laughter.) However, he had been a member of the Board ever since and not- withstanding that episode he could say " With all thy faults I love thee still." (Laughter.) The vote was cordially passed.</p>
        <p>THANKING THE CHATRAIN AND VICE-CHAIRNIAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman for his services during the past year. He could say- without any exaggeration that Mr. Billinge had con- sistently adhered to the best traditions of the chair, and endeavoured to secure that the whole of the work of the Union should be carried on, as it had been carried on for some time—although with occasional frtotion and opposition—on the most sound, progressive lines of modern administration. Warrington had been a pioneer in a great many ways in the direction of reform, and they hoped to continue to be a pioneer Union. Their work during the past two years had been greatly facili- tated by the tact with which Mr. Billinge had presided over their proceedings, and the loyal help he had given to further every good reform suggested, not merely at the Board meetings but by steady attendance at com- mittee meetings. Mr. THOMPSON seconded and said he had admired Mr. Billinge's conduct in the chair. He had been most regular in his attendance, in fact he had never. been absent except when on deputations. Mr. FORSHA,W supported and said Mr. Billinge must feel satisfied with the work accomplished during his period of office. Changes had been made which as time went on would prove more and more valuable to the ratepayers. The Rev. T. R. 7,TA.rraEws, wile referred to Mr. Billinge as the" Pope of Haydock " said that not only by his urbanity but by his all-round capacity had he merited the thanks of the Board.</p>
        <p>'I he CLERK said all the officials would cordially agree with everything that had been said. The motion was unanimously carried. Mr. BILLINGN in ieply said he had always had the assurance of the confilence and interest of his fellow- Cuardians in.the work and they had on behalf of the poor and the ratepayers contributed a lot of valuable time. He sincerely hoped his successor in the chair would receive the same courtesy and assistance. During the whole of his life it would be a happy recollection to think of the period when he had the honour of presiding over that important Board. He concluded by moving a vote of thanks to the vice-chairman (Mr. W. Hickman) who had been a valuable colleague. Mr. SETON seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. FOR.SHAW supported and said ho admired Mr. Hickman for his strong opinions and the way in which he was prepared to fight for them. His resignation was a distinct loss to the Board because he was a man of sound business training and ability. They wished him long life, good health and prosperity. Mr..TROAIPSON said he knew it was a matter of great regret to Mr. Hickman that he was disqualified from serving any longer. He had only retired because he was obliged to. ' The vote was heartily approved. ~</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>OBITUARY.</head>
        <p>OBITUARY.</p>
        <p>Ex-Councillor Wright.</p>
        <p>After a lingering illness ex-Councillor Thomas Wright passed away in the Warrington Infirmary on Sunday evening, and by his death local labour circles lose a staunch advocate. Mr. Wright, who was 44 years ofdage, had been in ill-health for some time but early last year he received considerable benefit from a four months' stay in the Liverpool Sanatorium at Delamere, Latterly his conditinn grew worse, and he spcnt the last few weeks in Warrington Infirmary, having only a short time previously been an in-patient at that institution. The deceased came to Warrington at the age of ten, and for the greater part of his working life had been employed at Whitecross Wirevvorks. In his early married life he went to America, and obtained a situa- tion in Mr. Andrew earnegid's steel works at New- castle, Pennsylvania. There he remained for five years. when he returned to Warrington. He actively interested himself in Labonr affairs, being a prominent member of the Warrington 1.L.P., and looked after the interests of the workingmen during the two years he was the repre- sentative of Bewsey Ward on the Town Council. In that contest three candidates were nominated for the seat, one withdrew at the last moment, and the other retired from the contest, leaving Mr. Wright practically, a walk-over. Mr. Wright at the Council meetings dis- tinguished himself by his strenuous opposition to the proposal to ask Mr. Carnegie for a grant towards a new library. Mr. Wright retired from the Council last November, and owing to the state of his health did not seek re-election.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. In addition to the members, of the family there were present :—Mr. Edwin Wright (brother), Councillors Stott and Ashcroft, and Mr. Albert Briggs representing the Warrington Clarion Cycling Club, of which the deceased was a member. Rev. John Francis Matthews officiated at the interment. Wreaths were sent by the employes at Whitecross Works, Clarion Cycling Club and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Mr. T. G. Grounds.</head>
        <p>Mr. T. G. Grounds.</p>
        <p>General regret was felt on Sunday at the very sudden death of Mr. Thomas George Grounds. Early on Sunday morning he was apparently in his usual health, but later he had a seizure in the kitchen, and his wife, who found him partially unconscious, and unable to speak, sent for medical assistance, but Mr. Grounds passed away shortly afterwards. The deceased had been in the employ of Messrs. T. Sutton and Sons, Legh- street, for practically a quarter of a century, and for some years filled the position of income-tag clerk. In his early career he was a prominent athlete and swimmer, and was one of the most active members of the Warrington Swimming Club. For some years he was a teacher in the School of Art under the regime of the late Mr. J. Christmas Thompson. He was 4 L years of age, and leaves a wife and four children. The remains of the deceased were interred in the Cemetery on Tuesday afternoon, a brief funeral service having been previously held in St. Paul's Church, the Rev. W. Bracecamp officiating. There was a large attendance at the graveside, and floral tributes were sent by his wife, sister and children, Mr. and Mrs. William Baker (father and mother-in-law), Mr. and Mrs. Richard Baker, Mr. and Mrs. Harry Baker, Mr. Charles Baker (Liverpool), Mr. and Mrs. George Scotson, Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Ashton, Mr. and Mrs. Edward Burrows, Messrs. T. Sutton and Sons and staff, " From a few friends in the Market-place." Mr. and Mrs. J. Gerrard, " The clerkiiin the Union Offices Bewsey Chambers," I" I rom a few friends—J. Spinks, S. Thorley, A. Bright, Walter Hewist, J. Wpring, and T. Oultram ' and Miss Hardman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Mr. William Forshaw.</head>
        <p>Mr. William Forshaw.</p>
        <p>A prominent Warrington manufacturer passed away on Saturday in the person of Mr. William Forshaw, a menr bar of the firm of Messrs. E. Forshaw and Son, Napier- street. The deceased who was 62 years of age was thrown out of his trap about 15 months ago and seriously injured. An operation was found necessary and these two events may be said to have caused his death which took place at his residence, Grappenhall. The deceased, who was born in Church-street, was a son of the late Mr. Edward Forshaw, who founded the business in Napier-street in 1848, and it ham been suc- cessfully carried on since that gentleman's death by Mr. William Forshaw and his son-in-law (Mr. Arthur Boardman). Mr. William Forshaw, who was a first cousin of Mr. Alderman Forshaw, was much respected by a large circle of friends, and his death is much regretted. He was a member of the congregation worshipping in Cairo-street Unitarian Church. The funeral took place on Tuesday afternoon in Hill Cliffe burial ground, the Rev. C. Harvey Cook officiating. The principal mourners were Mr. Arthur Boardman (son-in-law), Master Arthur Boardman (grandson), Alderman Forshaw, Mr. Newell, Mr. Jabez Boardman, Mr. Alfred Podmore, Mr. John Bertham, Dr. Collins, Mr. W. Cook (nephew), and Mr. H. Cook (nephew). The deceased's workmen to the number of about 50 walked in procession from the house to the churchyard, and amongst others present in the church and at the graveside were Mr. F. Monks, Mr. F'. W. Monks, Mr. W. H. Woodcock, Mr. John Wainwright (Manchester), Mr. Bertham (Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.) and several representatives of the firm of Messrs. Joseph Crosfleld and Son.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of wreaths :—The widow, Mr. and Mrs. A. Boardman and three grandchildren; Mrs. Carter (sister), Miss Maggie Forshaw, Alderman and Mrs. Forshaw, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., Mr. and Mrs. Newall, Mr. and Mrs. All Podmore, the engineering department of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, employes of Messrs. E. Forshaw and Son, the office staff, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Catterall. Dr. and Mrs. Collins, Mr. and Mrs. Turner, Mrs. Smith (Bewsey-road), and Councillor Owen Toole. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons, of Buttermarket- street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Mr. William Cummins.</head>
        <p>Mr. William Cummins.</p>
        <p>We regret to have to record the death of Mr. William Cummins, which took place on Saturday morning last, at his house, 20, Miller-street Latchford, after an illness of ten days duration. The cause of death was pneumonia. Mr. Cummins, who at the time of his death was 7,1 years of age, was a saddler by trade, having been in the employment of Mr. Isaac Wilkinson ever since Mr. Wilkinson commenced business, 24 years ago. It was, perhaps, as a musician of considerable ability that Mr. Cummins was best known, he having been for many years past closely associated with local bands, both as conductor and also as a performer on the euphonium and the trombone. He received a thorough musical training while a boy at school, afterwards at different times becoming a member of such bands as the Sons of Temperance, Altrincham, Chorlton-cum- Hardy band, Lymtn Volunteer baud, Lymm Tradesmen's band and the Warrington Volunteer band. Mr. Cummins was up to the time of his death, enphonium- player in the late Mr. T. J. Down's orchestra and military band, with which he was first associated in the year 1881 i. He was one of the prime movers in the formatipn, some fifteen years ago, of Latchford brass band and occupied the dual position of conductor and solo euphonium. player in that band for about five years. He was,— from Easter 1897 to Easter 1903—conductor of Higher Walton brass band having previously assisted the band in the capacity of a player on the occasion of the band visiting Wales in the year 1888. Mr. Cummins was energetic and enthusiastic and was ever ready to do all in his power to assist the cause of music, which he had closely at heart. The esteem in which he was held by his many friends was shown by the large gathering which witnessed the interment at the Cemetery on Wednesday last, and also by the number of beautiful wreaths which were sent ; including one from Mrs.and Mr. A. Down, and one from the Members of the late T. J. Down's Orchestra and Military Bands. The funeral cortege, followed by a large contingent of local bands- men left the house at 1.30. The obsequies at the graveside were performed by the Rev. C. L. Stowe, Primitive Methodist minister, Legh-street. The deceased leaves a widow and several sons and daughters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Mr. Josiah Whittle.</head>
        <p>Mr. Josiah Whittle.</p>
        <p>Niany of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Josiah Whittle, a well-known tradesman, who passed away yesterday morning, at the age of sixty- eight years. For many years he had been in business as a plumber, succeeding his father in the old- established shop in Buttermarket-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LICENSING REFORM.</head>
        <p>LICENSING REFORM.</p>
        <p>National Temperance Federation and the Coming Bill.</p>
        <p>The 23rd annual meeeting of the National Temperance Federation was held at Exeter Hall, London, with the Prwident, Mr. A. Cameron Corbett, M.P., in the chair. The Countess of Carlisle, Mr. John McCallum, M.P., Mr. Lief Jones, M.P., Mr. Charles Roberts, M.P., and Mr. H. J. Wilson, M.P., were amen(' the Vice-Presidents attending, and the bulk of the l'slational and Semi- National and Religious Denorninasional Temperance Organisations of the United Kingdom were represented. The Annual Report referred to action and progress in all phases of the movement, showing, however, that the only Temperance legislative item passed last year was a small measure on hours of liquor sale. The delay in the in the introduction of the Government's promised Temperance Bill was creating a feeling of disappoint- ment. The following resolution was unanimously adopted : "That this National Temperance Federation thanks the Government for the prominent place accorded to the Licensing Bill in the King's Speech, in accordance with the promise of the Prime Minister that Temper- ance Reform should be the principal business' of the present Session. "It urges that the Bill should include those measures upon which Temperance opinion is united, and, above all, should provide for Local Option for the United Kingdom by direct popular vote, untrammelled by any option for what is termed' Disinterested' Management. "The Federation calls upon its Federated Organisa- tions and all good citizens, irrespective of party ties, to give earnest and energetic support in the country to secure such a measure, and it appeals to the Prime Minister to press forward this reform during the pre- sent session so that it may be passed into law."</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by Jon'N WALK SG Co., at The Examiner General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, and published by them at ths same rlace, and also at The Examiner Offices, ViCtOria-road WIDNES: 32, George-street, ST. Higurgs: 3, Market-, treet, EARLEsTowN; Church-street RuNCORN, Me</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
