      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-04-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EASTERTIDE.</head>
        <p>EASTERTIDE.</p>
        <p>AN IDEAL WEEK-END.</p>
        <p>The Eastertide of 1907 will long be remembered for a.spell of glorious weather remarkable for the time of the year. Following upon the congenial conditions of last week, Sunday and Monday were favoured with delightful sunshine, and the great many people who were on holiday bent spent a pleasant respite from toil at various popular resorts. On Saturday there was a considerable exodus from the town, the cup tie at Huddersfield proving a great attraction and the country roads were alive with cyclists and motorists. who enjoyed themselves to their hearts' content. Easter Day is the greatest religious festival of the year, and Sunday's services were of a particu- larly joyous character. The Anglican and Roman Catholic Churches were beautifully decorated with flowers. There was. a crowded congregation at the Parish Church in the evening, when tha Rev. Paul Bull concluded his visit with an eloquent sermon on the Resurrection. On Monday thousands of people availed themselves of the day excnrsions arranged by the Railway Companies, and seaside and country haunts were thronged with happy crowds. The following are some of the principal bookings from Bank Quay Railway Station :—Frodsham 757, Chester 444, Helsby 44, Blackpool 90, Birmingham and Wolverhampton 84, Rhyl 102, and from the Central Station :—Manchester 1,000 Belle Vue 200, Southport 900, Liverpool 2,000, Llandudno 50, and New Brighton 1,000. In Warrington the only attraction was the football match between Warrington and Leeds which attracted a large gate.</p>
      </div>
