n.—Advt. Warrington Loiterer Sentenced. John Mulligan who has been in the custody of the Warrington police for being in possession of a silver watch,for which he could give no satisfact n-y account, was sentenced at the Borough Police Court on Thurs- day to two months' imprisonment for loitering in the neigbourhood of Dallam-lane with intent to commit a felony. Early on Sunday morning Sergeant Cooper saw prisoner conceal the watch in a doorway and arrested him. Shop Fire • Considerable damage was caused by a fire at the shop of Mr. Arthur Booth, Buttermarket-street, on , Saturday. The outbreak was observed shortly after one o'clock and Superintendent Turner and a number of firemen at once turned out with the steam fire engine "Captain." On their arrival they found the beams running alongside the chimney well alight, the flames having been caused by a defect in the brick- work. To avoid damaging the goods a number of hand chemical extinguishers were used, and the &amp;- was soon extinguished. Ex-Soldier's Sad End. Au ex-soldier named John Stanfield, aged sixty- '- eight, residing at 57, Sharp-street, Warrington, was found hanging by a rope attached to a beam in an out- building at the reakof his house on Thursday. The tragic discovery was made by his son who got the assistance of some Corporation wOrkmen, and "MT body was cut down, but life was extinct. The deceased, who followed the occupation of a labourer, Shad been out of work for some time, and this seems to have caused despondency. Week-End Accidents. During the last week-end several minor accidents occurred in Warrington. On Friday a married woman, named Lena Doherty, was admitted into the Infirmary suffering from a severe lacerated wound on the side of her face and nose. It appears that she was driving in a trap when she fell out while passing ' over Wilderspool Crossings. After treatment she was taken home.—A woman named Margaret Brown, ~ of Sutherland-street, Stockton Heath, was admitted to • the Infirmary on Saturday evening, suffering from a broken arm, caused by a fall in Market-street.—A boy mined Thomas Gaffney residing at 15, Flaydock- street, was playing at Wilderspool on Saturday when he slipped off a fence and dislocated his arm. He was taken on a cart by P.C. Hamilton to the Infirmary for treatment. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Trades Exhibition. I From Tudsday next until Friday the 19th inst. a: trades, cookery and health exhibition will be held in.. the Parr Hall, admission to which will be free. These 1. exhibitions serve an extremely useful purpose in , bringing modern foods and hygenic appliances before the pablic who will be able to judge of the merits of the various preparations by testing the liberal samples which will be distributed. Not the least important I feature, and one which appeals more particularly to ! the ladies, is the series of free cookery lectures by a I highly qualified instructor and, as in the past, these ' should prove very popular. In addition promenade concerts have been arranged at which selections will Ibe given by the " Crescent " ladies orchestra, Miss Lily Fairhurst, Miss Woodland and Mr. R. A. Rebinson, and biographical displays will also be provided. The exhibition opens at 3 o'clock on Tues- day afternoon and every sweeding day will be open , between the hours of 2 and 9.30 p.m. SANKEY BRIDGE. ' The SURVEYOR reported that he had received a coin- Warrington Motorists harrow Escape. munication from the County bridge master with refer- ! A party of Wamington motorists, namely, Sergeant- ence to the widening of the bridge over the canal at Major Delinage, Mr. J. Sndlovv, Mr. A. Starkey, Mr. H. Sankey Bridges. Mr. Compton Hall said it was certain Rogers, Mr, J. Percival, and. a chauffeur, had an that the Corporation of Warrington would joitr in any extremely narrow escape on Saturday evening. They improvement which was made, and suggested that the were returning to Warrington by motor af